window.PANIM_CHAPTERS = [
 {
  "num": 1,
  "id": "ch01",
  "title": "The God Who Sees",
  "hook": "Two small silver scrolls, a blessing about a face, and the oldest words of the Bible anyone has ever found.",
  "glossary": [
   {
    "term": "panim",
    "gloss": "Panim — it is the Hebrew word for face, and it has a biography."
   },
   {
    "term": "merachefet",
    "gloss": "The Hebrew behind “hovering” is merachefet, and the verb appears only three times in the entire Hebrew Bible."
   },
   {
    "term": "lifnei",
    "gloss": "In Hebrew, the everyday word for “before,” the one inside standing before the LORD, is lifnei, built directly out of panim."
   },
   {
    "term": "El Roi",
    "gloss": "El Roi."
   },
   {
    "term": "ra'ah",
    "gloss": "And sitting inside Roi is the verb ra'ah, the plain physical act of eyes landing on something."
   },
   {
    "term": "YHWH-jireh",
    "gloss": "It is there on Mount Moriah, where Abraham names the mountain YHWH-jireh."
   }
  ],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch01-tomb"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The oldest words of the Bible ever found were found by a bored kid who was supposed to be sweeping.",
    "id": "ch01-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jerusalem, 1979.",
    "id": "ch01-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "On a rocky slope above the Hinnom Valley, an excavation is coming up empty. The tombs here were carved into the hillside more than twenty-five centuries ago, leaving grave robbers nearly all of history to find them. Chamber after chamber reveals the same grim verdict: dust, shattered pottery, and the empty silence of everything worth taking long gone.",
    "id": "ch01-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The archaeologist has one problem left on the site, and the problem is thirteen years old. A volunteer with too much energy and nowhere to put it. So the boy gets the assignment every excavation keeps ready for a restless kid. Chamber 25. Sweep it out. Stay out of the way.",
    "id": "ch01-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You are thirteen years old, and the chamber is cool and dim. The work is dull, and the afternoon has nowhere to go.",
    "id": "ch01-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You sweep for a while. You sit for a while. The dust drifts back into the places you just cleared.",
    "id": "ch01-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And somewhere in that long, dead hour, the silence begins to get to you.",
    "id": "ch01-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So you start knocking on the ancient stone floor.",
    "id": "ch01-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The stone knocks back hollow.",
    "id": "ch01-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You stop.",
    "id": "ch01-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then you hit it again.",
    "id": "ch01-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The sound comes back wrong. Not the dense, dead thud of solid stone, but a deeper note, as if the floor is answering from somewhere below.",
    "id": "ch01-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You hit the slab a third time, harder.",
    "id": "ch01-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the stone gives. A dry crack, then the slab dropping away under your hands into a dark you cannot see the bottom of.",
    "id": "ch01-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Beneath a floor that has held solid for twenty-five centuries, there is a space nobody has opened.",
    "id": "ch01-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The stonework has outlasted every grave robber in history. It does not survive a bored teenager’s curiosity.",
    "id": "ch01-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When the dust settles, you are on your knees at the broken edge, staring into a darkness no living person has ever seen: a hidden burial vault sealed beneath the chamber the robbers thought they had emptied.",
    "id": "ch01-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Inside are beads. Oil lamps. The bones of the dead, still lying where their families left them.",
    "id": "ch01-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And in the dirt among those bones, two small rolls of silver, each wound tightly around whatever someone wrote inside long ago.",
    "id": "ch01-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The silver is so brittle that unrolling it seems impossible. One wrong move, and the oldest secret in Jerusalem could crumble into flakes.",
    "id": "ch01-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "For three years, the scrolls wait while specialists work out how to open them. Then the first one yields.",
    "id": "ch01-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Under the lens, scratched letters begin to sharpen. One stroke at a time, hand-scored lines emerge from the silver. A roomful of experts leans closer, reading over one another’s shoulders.",
    "id": "ch01-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the conversations stop.",
    "id": "ch01-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch01-scroll"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The script is Hebrew, from the seventh century before Christ. Four hundred years older than the Dead Sea Scrolls. The earliest words of the Bible in existence.",
    "id": "ch01-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And of everything they could have said, they say this:",
    "id": "ch01-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The LORD bless you, and keep you;",
     "The LORD make His face shine on you,",
     "And be gracious to you;",
     "The LORD lift up His countenance on you,",
     "And give you peace."
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 6:24–26",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch01-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Someone was buried wearing those words.",
    "id": "ch01-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "By the seventh century before Christ, God had already said a great deal: creation, covenant, commandments, an entire thundering library of prophets.",
    "id": "ch01-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And yet, out of all of it, these were the words the people of Jerusalem chose to carry into the grave. The words they carried with them against their bones in the dark.",
    "id": "ch01-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A prayer that God’s face would turn toward them and shine.",
    "id": "ch01-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Of everything He ever said — why that?",
    "id": "ch01-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The answer is a single Hebrew word, and this book is going to follow it across the whole Bible the way you would follow a life. <em>Panim</em> — it is the Hebrew word for face, and it has a biography.",
    "id": "ch01-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Its story opens on the Bible’s very first page.",
    "id": "ch01-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Before Abraham. Before Israel. Before the law or the temple. Before a single human face exists to be blessed or to hide, darkness is over the deep, and the Spirit of God is hovering over the face of the waters.",
    "id": "ch01-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Genesis 1:2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Hebrew behind “hovering” is <em>merachefet</em>, and the verb appears only three times in the entire Hebrew Bible. Once here. Once for a man whose bones are shaking. And once in a song, written at the very end of Moses’s life, where it describes an eagle over her young.",
    "id": "ch01-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That song begins by telling you where God found the people He was hovering over. In a desert. In a howling waste. And the Hebrew for that waste is <em>tohu</em>, the same word sitting in the second verse of the Bible, over the dark water, before anything had a shape.",
    "id": "ch01-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the eagle. He spread His wings, the song says, and carried them out on them.",
    "id": "ch01-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Deuteronomy 32:10–11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So the hovering was never only about oceans.",
    "id": "ch01-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Protective. Attentive. Present over what has not yet become what it will be.",
    "id": "ch01-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This is how the Bible chooses to begin.",
    "id": "ch01-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first face the Bible ever mentions is the face of the waters, and God is turned toward it.",
    "id": "ch01-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "From its opening line, this is a book about a God who faces what He loves.",
    "id": "ch01-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the story of that word closes on the Bible’s last page, in a sentence still waiting to come true. Between those two pages, the face hides, shines, bleeds, and comes home.",
    "id": "ch01-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is a night river on that road, and a man clinging in the dark to Someone he cannot see and will not release.",
    "id": "ch01-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The glory of God gets up and leaves a building, and out of everyone alive on earth, exactly one man sees it go.",
    "id": "ch01-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Farther on, there are tears on the face of God, and spit, and a blindfold, and a morning waiting on the far side of everything.",
    "id": "ch01-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is a love story, the oldest one still running.",
    "id": "ch01-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Once you start watching for the word, it will not leave you alone. In Hebrew, the everyday word for “before,” the one inside standing <em>before the LORD</em>, is <em>lifnei</em>, built directly out of <em>panim</em>. It means, literally, “to the face of.” So when God tells Abram, “Walk before Me,” He is saying something no English Bible can quite show you:",
    "id": "ch01-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Walk before My face.</em>",
    "id": "ch01-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Live your whole life turned toward Me. Genesis 17:1.",
    "id": "ch01-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David danced the day the ark came home to Jerusalem, out in the street with everything he had, spinning out of his royal robes until his own wife despised him from a window. Your Bible says he danced <em>before the LORD</em>. The Hebrew says he danced <em>before the face of the LORD</em>. He was not performing for the crowd, and he was not working up a feeling; he was a man dancing at the face of Someone he could feel watching him, and loving every second of it.",
    "id": "ch01-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The woman at the window was Saul’s daughter. She came out to tell him how he had looked in front of the servant girls, and he did not defend the dancing. He told her which face he had been dancing for, and then he named her father. <em>It was before the LORD, who chose me above your father.</em>",
    "id": "ch01-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Her father had lost a kingdom explaining himself in one sentence. <em>I feared the people and listened to their voice.</em>",
    "id": "ch01-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She had grown up in a house that played to the room. And when she told her husband he had humiliated himself in public, he said he would go lower than that, and be low in his own eyes.",
    "id": "ch01-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the same key cuts the other way: when a man sins <em>before the LORD</em>, he is sinning in front of a face, and that sentence is suddenly much harder to read. The Bible does not just record what happened. Page after page, it records Whose face it happened before.",
    "id": "ch01-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Counting its compound forms, the word appears more than two thousand times, which means the face of God has been sitting inside sentences you have read all your life, hidden under a word as ordinary as before. You may have met the face in its famous verses, the blessings and the psalms. It was also standing on nearly every other page.",
    "id": "ch01-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And sometimes it is not hidden at all. Sometimes it has been taken out.",
    "id": "ch01-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Three years of famine in David’s country, year after year, and the king goes looking for God.",
    "id": "ch01-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The text says he sought the face of the LORD.",
    "id": "ch01-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You have only ever read that he inquired.",
    "id": "ch01-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One of those is a man at a counter. The other is a man looking for someone.",
    "id": "ch01-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the first person who ever looked toward that face and understood what she was seeing was a slave.",
    "id": "ch01-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She is Egyptian, property in the household of Abram, pregnant with a child she did not choose. The pregnancy was her mistress’s plan. In that world a slave could be used however her owners wished, her body conscripted into someone else’s dream. And the story hides a detail in plain sight: to this point, no one has spoken her name. Sarai says “my maid.” Abram says “your maid.” She is discussed, handed over, blamed. Never once addressed.",
    "id": "ch01-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the household turns cruel, and Hagar has nowhere to appeal, no one to protect her, no door that opens for her.",
    "id": "ch01-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She runs.",
    "id": "ch01-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Out into the desert. Down the road toward Egypt, toward the only other life she has ever known.",
    "id": "ch01-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And what is waiting at the end of that road?",
    "id": "ch01-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Egypt is a country of gods with stone faces, the faces Hagar grew up beneath, gilded and staring down the Nile. Not one of them ever looked back at a slave girl.",
    "id": "ch01-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She is running home to the gods who do not see.",
    "id": "ch01-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Genesis 16:6–7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then, at a spring in the wilderness, a voice finds her.",
    "id": "ch01-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She does not know yet whose voice it is.",
    "id": "ch01-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But before He asks her for anything, He gives her something no one in her world ever has.",
    "id": "ch01-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Her name.",
    "id": "ch01-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Hagar, servant of Sarai, where have you come from, and where are you going?” Gen 16:8",
    "id": "ch01-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He already knows both answers. He asks anyway. Orders are what you give property; a question is what you give a person whose story matters. And this question hands her back the two things slavery had erased: a past worth telling, and a future worth asking about. God does not begin by telling her what to do. He begins by asking who she is.",
    "id": "ch01-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When the encounter ends, this pregnant runaway does something found nowhere else in all of Scripture.",
    "id": "ch01-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "No patriarch gives Him one. No prophet. No king.",
    "id": "ch01-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She gives God a name.",
    "id": "ch01-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Then she called the name of the LORD who spoke to her, “You are a God who sees.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 16:13",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch01-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>El Roi.</em> Two Hebrew words: <em>El</em>, God, and <em>Roi</em>, who sees me. And sitting inside <em>Roi</em> is the verb <em>ra'ah</em>, the plain physical act of eyes landing on something. Once you have met that verb, you find it standing at the hinges of the whole story.",
    "id": "ch01-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is there on Mount Moriah, where Abraham names the mountain <em>YHWH-jireh</em>. Your Bible translates it “the LORD will provide,” but <em>jireh</em> is <em>ra'ah</em> again: literally, <em>the LORD will see</em> — He will see to it. Even our English keeps the secret: “provide” comes from a Latin word meaning <em>to see ahead</em>. Provision was never a separate miracle. It was what seeing looked like when the One who saw you was God.",
    "id": "ch01-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "For Hagar, being seen and being cared for were the same mercy, moving toward her from the same face. Genesis 22:14.",
    "id": "ch01-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The verb returns centuries later, at the bottom of Israel’s darkest stretch, when four hundred years of slavery finally crack open against a single line: “God saw the sons of Israel, and God took notice of them.” Ex 2:25.",
    "id": "ch01-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The whole exodus — the plagues, the sea, the mountain — swings on a verb of the eyes. Rescue begins the same way it began for Hagar: not with an army, but with a look.",
    "id": "ch01-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Her theology did not come from a scroll or an altar or a vision of thrones. She was simply seen. Seen all the way through, where she came from and where she was going, by Someone who did not look away.",
    "id": "ch01-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The lowest-ranked person in the story walked out of that desert knowing what to call God before the patriarchs did.",
    "id": "ch01-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She is the first of a long line.",
    "id": "ch01-p85"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Maybe that is why her story still finds people, four thousand years later. You were built to notice faces. Deep in your brain, a small patch of circuitry is devoted almost entirely to recognizing them. Almost nothing else you look at gets that. Not trees, not mountains, not the sunsets you stop the car for. Faces alone get their own machinery.",
    "id": "ch01-p86"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that wiring is installed before almost anything else is ready. A newborn’s eyes arrive focused to roughly one distance, and it is not the distance to the window or the ceiling. It is eight to twelve inches: the distance to the face of whoever is holding them. From your first hours of life, you were scanning for a face, and you have never stopped.",
    "id": "ch01-p87"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Science can map the circuitry. It can describe the mechanism. But it cannot tell you what a face is for.",
    "id": "ch01-p88"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hagar could.",
    "id": "ch01-p89"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You were built to search for a face. This book is about the One you were made to find.",
    "id": "ch01-p90"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One man in the Gospels acted that out with his whole body.",
    "id": "ch01-p91"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He was the chief tax collector in Jericho, which in that town meant a man everyone could name and nobody would stand beside. He was also short. So when the crowd closed up along the road, he did the undignified thing: he ran on ahead of everybody and climbed a sycamore, because a face was coming through and he could not see over the shoulders in front of him.",
    "id": "ch01-p92"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The face stopped underneath him.",
    "id": "ch01-p93"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jesus looked up, and used his name, and invited Himself to dinner.",
    "id": "ch01-p94"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He came to see. He got seen. And it ended at his own table, with the neighbors out in the street complaining about who was eating in there.",
    "id": "ch01-p95"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Luke 19:1–7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And one page earlier, Luke had already put another tax collector in the temple, too ashamed to try any of that.",
    "id": "ch01-p96"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He stood off at a distance. He would not lift his eyes off the floor. All he could do was strike his own chest and ask God for mercy.",
    "id": "ch01-p97"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jesus said that one went home justified.",
    "id": "ch01-p98"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two tax collectors, back to back in the same Gospel. One climbed a tree to get his eyes up. One could not raise his at all.",
    "id": "ch01-p99"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Neither of them missed.",
    "id": "ch01-p100"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And there was a third man, earlier still, who was not looking for anything.",
    "id": "ch01-p101"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A skeptic named Nathanael gets dragged to Jesus by a friend, arrives with his arms folded, and is undone inside of one sentence. Not by an argument. By a location.",
    "id": "ch01-p102"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Before Philip called you, when you were under the fig tree, I saw you.” John 1:48.",
    "id": "ch01-p103"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody was there. A man alone under some leaves, and Someone was already looking.",
    "id": "ch01-p104"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that search comes with a promise, and the promise is older than the man who made it.",
    "id": "ch01-p105"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One of the psalms is built like a question at a door. Who may go up the hill of the LORD? Who gets to stand in the holy place? And the answer comes back the way answers come back at doors — short. Clean hands. A pure heart. Then the psalm names that crowd, and the name it gives them is the one this whole search has been reaching for: <em>the generation of those who seek Him, who seek Your face.</em>",
    "id": "ch01-p106"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Centuries later a man stood on a hillside with no temple in sight and gave the door’s own answer back to a crowd.",
    "id": "ch01-p107"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God."
    ],
    "ref": "Matthew 5:8",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch01-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The door had offered a way in. He offers the face.",
    "id": "ch01-p108"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He named who gets to.",
    "id": "ch01-p109"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody has collected on it yet.",
    "id": "ch01-p110"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The oldest Bible on earth is a prayer about the face of God, and the only name a human being ever gave Him is <em>the One who sees</em>.",
    "id": "ch01-p111"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "With a beginning like that, the rest should be simple: A God who loves to look. A people learning to be seen. Glory the whole way home.",
    "id": "ch01-p112"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is not what happens.",
    "id": "ch01-p113"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Back in the garden, in the cool of the day, on the first evening after everything went wrong, God comes looking for two human faces.",
    "id": "ch01-p114"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They hide.",
    "id": "ch01-p115"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "num": 2,
  "id": "ch02",
  "title": "The Hiding",
  "hook": "Shame never waits to find out whether God has turned away. It turns first, and assumes He has.",
  "glossary": [
   {
    "term": "satar",
    "gloss": "The verb he reaches for is satar: hiddenness on purpose, a face deliberately turned away."
   },
   {
    "term": "millifnei YHWH",
    "gloss": "In one sentence he says it twice: Jonah ran millifnei YHWH, from the face of the LORD, and then he did it again, fleeing not merely an assignment but the face itself."
   },
   {
    "term": "shachar",
    "gloss": "The verb for their seeking is shachar, the same word Hebrew uses for the dawn."
   }
  ],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch02-trees"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Evening settles over the garden. The heat of the day lifts off the grass, the shadows under the trees stretch long, and through the leaves comes a sound the two of them have loved their whole short lives. It is the sound of Him walking, the way He walks every evening, in the cool of the day.",
    "id": "ch02-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Yesterday, that sound was the best part of being alive.",
    "id": "ch02-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Tonight it is the most terrifying sound in the world.",
    "id": "ch02-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The same footsteps that meant joy yesterday now sound like a sentence. The change did not happen in the garden. It happened in them.",
    "id": "ch02-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Shame never waits to find out whether God has turned away. It turns first, then assumes He has.",
    "id": "ch02-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They hear Him coming, and they run the wrong way.",
    "id": "ch02-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Among the trees, where the shade is deepest and the leaves grow thickest, they press themselves in and go still. Two faces turned into the leaves, away from the sound they once ran toward.",
    "id": "ch02-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two bodies pressed into the dark. The new knowledge sitting in their stomachs like a stone.",
    "id": "ch02-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the footsteps keep coming, unhurried, at the same easy pace as every other evening. Which is somehow worse.",
    "id": "ch02-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hiding runs on one desperate hope: stay still enough, quiet enough, and whatever is coming will pass by.",
    "id": "ch02-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The footsteps do not pass by.",
    "id": "ch02-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "They heard the sound of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day, and the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God among the trees of the garden."
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 3:8",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the voice.",
    "id": "ch02-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Where are you?</em>",
    "id": "ch02-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is the first question God ever asks a human being, and He is not asking for directions. By the time He asks it, He has already kept the appointment they were hiding from. He came for the evening walk as if the walk were still on.",
    "id": "ch02-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He does not open with the crime. He opens with a question. The same question He will ask a runaway slave at a spring centuries down the road.",
    "id": "ch02-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He knows exactly where they are.",
    "id": "ch02-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What He is asking is whether they will come out.",
    "id": "ch02-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One generation later, the hiding is hereditary. Cain brings his offering, and God does not turn toward it.",
    "id": "ch02-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The story does not go to the altar, or the smoke, or the rejected offering. It goes to the face.",
    "id": "ch02-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first anger recorded in the Bible does not shake a fist at heaven. It drops a face toward the ground.",
    "id": "ch02-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "but for Cain and his offering he had no regard. So Cain was very angry, and his face fell."
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 4:5",
    "translation": "ESV",
    "id": "ch02-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What God does with a fallen face is easy to read past.",
    "id": "ch02-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Before there is any blood, He comes to the angry man the way He came to the garden: asking.",
    "id": "ch02-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Why are you angry? Why has your face fallen? If you do well, will it not be lifted up?</em> (Gen. 4:6–7)",
    "id": "ch02-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is offering to raise the very thing that fell.",
    "id": "ch02-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the word He reaches for does two jobs in Hebrew. <em>Nasa.</em> It is the word for lifting a face. It is also the word for taking something heavy off a man’s back and carrying it away yourself.",
    "id": "ch02-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Cain does not answer.",
    "id": "ch02-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He goes out to the field.",
    "id": "ch02-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first murder in history begins with a man walking away from a face God had offered to lift.",
    "id": "ch02-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the blood is in the ground, the sentence comes down, and Cain opens his mouth to tell God what the punishment really is.",
    "id": "ch02-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the first thing out of it is that same word, turned down.",
    "id": "ch02-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>My iniquity is greater than I can bear.</em> Word for word: greater than can be lifted.",
    "id": "ch02-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Six verses ago he was offered a lifted face. Here he answers that the weight cannot be moved.",
    "id": "ch02-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then he tells God the rest.",
    "id": "ch02-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God had spoken of soil and of wandering. Cain heard something worse underneath. Of everything he is about to lose, the loss he says out loud is a face.",
    "id": "ch02-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The verb he reaches for is <em>satar</em>: hiddenness on purpose, a face deliberately turned away. The first murderer in history describes his own damage, and nowhere in it does he mention the exile.",
    "id": "ch02-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That word does not stay in Genesis. A nation is going to need it.",
    "id": "ch02-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Behold, You have driven me this day from the face of the ground, and from Your face I will be hidden, and I will be a vagrant and a wanderer on the earth, and whoever finds me will kill me."
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 4:14",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Cain, the first man ever born, measures his exile in a single unit, and it is not miles and it is not years. It is a face he will no longer see.",
    "id": "ch02-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then he stands up and does it.",
    "id": "ch02-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Genesis gives the moment one short line and then declines to follow him. Cain went out from the presence of the LORD. From the face. He settles east of Eden, in a country the text calls Nod, which is the Hebrew word for wandering.",
    "id": "ch02-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He named the place after the thing he had become.",
    "id": "ch02-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hiding keeps needing more room. A garden was enough for Adam. Cain needed a country. The next man will need an ocean.",
    "id": "ch02-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God says go east, to Nineveh. Jonah goes down to the harbor and buys passage west, to Tarshish, the far edge of the known map — the opposite direction entirely.",
    "id": "ch02-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the narrator will not let you miss what he is really fleeing. In one sentence he says it twice: Jonah ran <em>millifnei YHWH</em>, from the face of the LORD, and then he did it again, fleeing not merely an assignment but the face itself.",
    "id": "ch02-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "But Jonah rose up to flee to Tarshish from the presence of the LORD. So he went down to Joppa, found a ship which was going to Tarshish, paid the fare and went down into it to go with them to Tarshish from the presence of the LORD."
    ],
    "ref": "Jonah 1:3",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Down to Joppa. Down into the ship. Down into the hold. As if the face of God were a kind of weather, something you could get below deck and wait out.",
    "id": "ch02-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So God sends weather.",
    "id": "ch02-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch02-storm"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The text says the LORD hurled a great wind onto the sea. Hurled, the way a man throws a stone. The storm is bad enough to make lifelong sailors start praying and pitching their livelihood over the side.",
    "id": "ch02-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When they finally throw the runaway into the sea, a fish is already waiting for him. Appointed to catch him.",
    "id": "ch02-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "From inside the dark, with the sea still in his lungs, Jonah prays the truest thing he has ever said. And it pivots on a single word.",
    "id": "ch02-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "So I said, “I have been expelled from Your sight. Nevertheless I will look again toward Your holy temple.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Jonah 2:4",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nevertheless.",
    "id": "ch02-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nineveh was not a blank slate waiting politely for a sermon. That world bowed to the fish-god Dagon, and Mesopotamia told an old story of a man-headed fish that rose out of the sea to bring wisdom to mankind. And up from the beach walks a prophet of the living God, delivered onto dry land out of the mouth of a fish, carrying the word of the God Dagon was supposed to be.",
    "id": "ch02-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Their own legend, outdone in front of them by the God it had been reaching toward in the dark. And the fish was appointed before Jonah ever boarded the ship.",
    "id": "ch02-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He did not just get His runaway to Nineveh. He got him there as a sermon the city could not look away from.",
    "id": "ch02-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The material He chose to build with: a prophet’s disobedience.",
    "id": "ch02-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There are consequences to fleeing the voice of the LORD, and Jonah could tell you about the storm and the smell. But every mile he ran from Nineveh was already a mile toward it. He wastes nothing. Not even the running.",
    "id": "ch02-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In 1975, a laboratory captured the whole theology on film.",
    "id": "ch02-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Developmental psychologist Edward Tronick put a mother and her baby face to face and asked her, for a while, simply to play. She coos and mirrors and lights up whenever the baby lights up, and the baby is delighted.",
    "id": "ch02-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then, on a signal, the mother goes blank.",
    "id": "ch02-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The mother is still right there. The distance has not changed. Only the face has.",
    "id": "ch02-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "First the smile, offered again, harder.",
    "id": "ch02-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the reaching and pointing, the little attempts to get her back.",
    "id": "ch02-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the shriek.",
    "id": "ch02-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And before the three minutes are up, when the face still gives nothing back, the baby folds inward and begins to grieve.",
    "id": "ch02-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The baby was warm, fed, and safe the whole time. The mother was still right there.",
    "id": "ch02-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The only thing that went out was the light in her mother’s face. That was enough.",
    "id": "ch02-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "We are wired from our first weeks of life for a face that answers, and nobody has to teach us what to do when it stops.",
    "id": "ch02-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Tronick needed a lab to prove it.",
    "id": "ch02-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Scripture has been saying it since the first couple hid in the trees.",
    "id": "ch02-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So when the prophets finally name the disaster, everything hangs on who is doing the verb. Not God, sulking behind a cloud. <em>Your sins have hidden His face from you.</em> The sins are the subject of the sentence.",
    "id": "ch02-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "But your iniquities have made a separation between you and your God, and your sins have hidden His face from you so that He does not hear."
    ],
    "ref": "Isaiah 59:2",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hiding was something we built. It was never a mood that came over God.",
    "id": "ch02-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Job did nothing. That is the entire premise. Heaven says so out loud in the opening pages, twice, before anything happens to him. And then everything happens to him.",
    "id": "ch02-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He loses the children, the livestock, the house, the skin. His friends arrive and sit with him in silence for seven days, which is the best thing they ever do, and then they open their mouths for thirty-five chapters and explain to a bereaved man what he must have done to deserve it.",
    "id": "ch02-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Job will not take the deal. He knows he did not earn this. So he keeps aiming his question higher than his friends, past them, at the only One who could answer it.",
    "id": "ch02-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Why do You hide Your face and consider me Your enemy?"
    ],
    "ref": "Job 13:24",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is Cain’s verb, in the mouth of a man with clean hands.",
    "id": "ch02-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The friends have an explanation. Job would rather have a face.",
    "id": "ch02-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And when God finally answers him, out of a storm, He never once addresses the argument. He does not explain the suffering. He does not defend Himself, or produce the reason, or mention the wager. He asks Job about wild goats and ostriches and where the light lives, for four chapters, and by the end of it the man who wanted an answer is not asking anymore.",
    "id": "ch02-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear;",
     "But now my eye sees You."
    ],
    "ref": "Job 42:5",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Heard, and then saw.",
    "id": "ch02-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The argument was never settled. It was outlived.",
    "id": "ch02-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And if that is true, then the one whose sky has gone dark is allowed to say so out loud. The Psalms hand over the exact words:",
    "id": "ch02-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>How long, O LORD, will You hide Your face from me?</em>",
    "id": "ch02-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The God whose face the psalmist says he cannot see preserved the complaint and set it to music, so that even our anger would have somewhere to go.",
    "id": "ch02-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It sits in the hymnbook under a heading that says <em>For the choir director</em>. The words you would be ashamed to whisper alone, Israel learned by heart and sang out loud, together.",
    "id": "ch02-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Cain turned away from the face and walked. Jonah turned away and sailed.",
    "id": "ch02-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The psalmist does neither.",
    "id": "ch02-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He plants himself beneath the empty sky and says it straight into the silence:",
    "id": "ch02-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "How long, O LORD? Will You forget me forever? How long will You hide Your face from me?"
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 13:1",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He asks it four times in two verses. Then he stops, and he never asks it again.",
    "id": "ch02-p85"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And what he asks for instead is stranger than the complaint. Enlighten my eyes, he says. Make them shine again. In the middle of protesting that the face is hidden, the thing he reaches for is the shining.",
    "id": "ch02-p86"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the psalm turns. He says he has trusted in the lovingkindness after all, and that his heart is going to rejoice. And the last line is the one he had no business reaching:",
    "id": "ch02-p87"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "I will sing to the LORD because he is good to me."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 13:6",
    "translation": "NLT",
    "id": "ch02-v10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nothing has happened.",
    "id": "ch02-p88"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "No rescue arrives between the fourth how long and the singing. The enemy is still standing there. The sky has not changed. Six verses, and the only thing that moves in the entire poem is which direction the man is facing.",
    "id": "ch02-p89"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He begins facing the silence. He ends facing the character of God.",
    "id": "ch02-p90"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Complaint is not the opposite of faith. Turning away is.",
    "id": "ch02-p91"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And one song in the book never gets that turn.",
    "id": "ch02-p92"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every other lament climbs back toward the light before the last line. The psalm you just heard ends singing.",
    "id": "ch02-p93"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Psalm 88 starts in the dark and keeps going down.",
    "id": "ch02-p94"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It keeps praying the whole way, verse after verse. No rescue comes. No dawn breaks.",
    "id": "ch02-p95"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the last word of the song, in Hebrew, is <em>machshak</em>.",
    "id": "ch02-p96"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Darkness.",
    "id": "ch02-p97"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "O LORD, why do you cast my soul away? Why do you hide your face from me?… You have caused my beloved and my friend to shun me; my companions have become darkness."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 88:14, 18",
    "translation": "ESV",
    "id": "ch02-v11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "If that is your song right now: God kept it.",
    "id": "ch02-p98"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He had a whole book to fill, and He saved a page for the prayer that ends in the dark, so that when you got there, the words would already be waiting.",
    "id": "ch02-p99"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the love poem has a silence of its own.",
    "id": "ch02-p100"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A woman is in bed, warm, late, and her lover is outside the door. She gives the reason people give: she has already undressed, she has already washed her feet. Then she changes her mind and gets up, and he is gone. <em>I sought him but I did not find him.</em> Song 5:6. The verb under <em>sought</em> is the one God uses when He says seek My face.",
    "id": "ch02-p101"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She was not guilty of anything except being slow.",
    "id": "ch02-p102"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Job had clean hands. So did she.",
    "id": "ch02-p103"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David was guilty of all of it, and he still did not turn away.",
    "id": "ch02-p104"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There was something he was not talking about, and he says the silence got into his body. His bones wore out. Whatever was alive in him dried up the way ground dries in the heat of summer. Then he stopped protecting it.",
    "id": "ch02-p105"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "I acknowledged my sin to You, and my iniquity I did not hide; I said, “I will confess my transgressions to the LORD”; and You forgave the guilt of my sin."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 32:5",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first line of that same song calls a man blessed whose sin is covered.",
    "id": "ch02-p106"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hide and covered are the same word in Hebrew. He would not hide it. So it got covered.",
    "id": "ch02-p107"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And beneath all this hiding, something stranger keeps happening in the human heart: the ache to be found never leaves.",
    "id": "ch02-p108"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The same man gives it away in a single line about seeking God’s face. Before the heart ever speaks, God speaks first.",
    "id": "ch02-p109"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "When You said, “Seek My face,” my heart said to You, “Your face, O LORD, I shall seek.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 27:8",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The ache to be seen is not a reach into an empty room. It is an answer.",
    "id": "ch02-p110"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then, at the very bottom of all this hiding, God says something nobody expects:",
    "id": "ch02-p111"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>I will return to My place until they seek My face.</em>",
    "id": "ch02-p112"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What kind of hiding announces itself?",
    "id": "ch02-p113"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He tells them He is going. He tells them why. And He names the exact thing that will bring Him back.",
    "id": "ch02-p114"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody hides like that unless they want to be found.",
    "id": "ch02-p115"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is the hiding of a father playing with a small child, tucked behind a curtain with his shoes still showing.",
    "id": "ch02-p116"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The verb for their seeking is <em>shachar</em>, the same word Hebrew uses for the dawn. This is dawn-seeking: the kind you do at a dark window when sleep will not come, watching the east for the first light.",
    "id": "ch02-p117"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And three lines later, the promise returns to the same sky.",
    "id": "ch02-p118"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Let us know; let us press on to know the LORD. His going forth is as certain as the dawn.</em>",
    "id": "ch02-p119"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Watch for Him like the dawn, says the verb.",
    "id": "ch02-p120"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And how does He say He will come?",
    "id": "ch02-p121"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "I will go away and return to My place until they acknowledge their guilt and seek My face… So let us know, let us press on to know the LORD. His going forth is as certain as the dawn; and He will come to us like the rain, like the spring rain watering the earth."
    ],
    "ref": "Hosea 5:15–6:3",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hiding was never the end of the sentence. It was the courtship.",
    "id": "ch02-p122"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And three chapters earlier in the same scroll, Hosea had already written down how the courtship works.",
    "id": "ch02-p123"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The wife has gone after other men. God’s first move is not a speech. He builds a fence.",
    "id": "ch02-p124"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "I will hedge up her way with thorns, and I will build a wall against her so that she cannot find her paths."
    ],
    "ref": "Hosea 2:6",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every road she likes stops going anywhere. He does not take away the desire and He does not take away the freedom. He makes the running boring.",
    "id": "ch02-p125"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the very next line tells you what she does with the freedom she has left.",
    "id": "ch02-p126"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "She will pursue her lovers, but she will not overtake them; and she will seek them, but will not find them. Then she will say, “I will go back to my first husband, for it was better for me then than now.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Hosea 2:7",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch02-v16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She seeks. She does not find.",
    "id": "ch02-p127"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is the same verb the woman at the door used, and the same empty room at the end of it. That one was looking for the right face and got there late. This one is looking in every direction except the one where a face is already turned toward her.",
    "id": "ch02-p128"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So she stands in the middle of her own blocked road and says it out loud. It was better for me then than now.",
    "id": "ch02-p129"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then comes the line everything else is downstream from.",
    "id": "ch02-p130"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Therefore, behold, I will allure her, and bring her into the wilderness, and speak tenderly to her."
    ],
    "ref": "Hosea 2:14",
    "translation": "ESV",
    "id": "ch02-v17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Allure is the word Scripture uses for seduction. It is the verb used of Delilah working on Samson. God had every word in creation to use. He chose the vocabulary of a suitor.",
    "id": "ch02-p131"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He does not take her anywhere impressive. He takes her to the desert, which is the one place on earth with nothing in it to look at but Him.",
    "id": "ch02-p132"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It does not say He speaks tenderly to her. It says He speaks upon her heart.",
    "id": "ch02-p133"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He gives her back her vineyards. He gives her the Valley of Trouble, the worst ground in the national memory, and calls it a door.",
    "id": "ch02-p134"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Faced with a species that runs and hides, He does not pull back. He does not go quiet and leave us to the silence we chose.",
    "id": "ch02-p135"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He comes closer.",
    "id": "ch02-p136"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He calls a whole nation to a burning mountain and offers them His face.",
    "id": "ch02-p137"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "num": 3,
  "id": "ch03",
  "title": "The Face They Fled",
  "hook": "A whole nation washes its clothes for two days to meet God face to face, and then asks to be excused from it.",
  "glossary": [
   {
    "term": "panim el panim",
    "gloss": "When Hebrew says that anywhere else, it says panim el panim, face to face."
   },
   {
    "term": "panim be-panim",
    "gloss": "But what Moses says about Sinai is stranger, and closer: panim be-panim, face in face, a construction that appears nowhere else in Scripture."
   },
   {
    "term": "Panim",
    "gloss": "When Hebrew says that anywhere else, it says panim el panim, face to face."
   },
   {
    "term": "mi-yitten",
    "gloss": "The Hebrew is mi-yitten, literally, “Who will give?” There is no true English equivalent, because it is neither a request nor a command."
   },
   {
    "term": "l'fanenu",
    "gloss": "And the people at the bottom, unable to bear the waiting and the silence, go to Aaron with a sentence that answers Sinai point for point: “make us gods who will go before us.” The Hebrew says l'fanenu, before our faces."
   }
  ],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch03-mountain"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every god they had ever seen had a face.",
    "id": "ch03-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is what it meant to grow up in Egypt. Gods were things you could look at: falcon heads and jackal heads gilded on temple walls, stone faces the height of ten men staring down the Nile, idols that priests washed and dressed and fed every morning like helpless kings.",
    "id": "ch03-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A god had an address. A god had a face you could locate, and the face never moved, and it never, ever looked back. For four hundred years, they had lived under faces like that.",
    "id": "ch03-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And now this crowd of freed brickmakers is camped in open desert at the foot of a mountain, seven weeks out of Egypt, and Moses comes down from the summit with a command nobody gives to brickmakers. “Wash your clothes. Be ready on the third day. For on the third day, the LORD will come down in the sight of all the people.”",
    "id": "ch03-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the sight of all the people. There is no inner circle to this invitation, and no priesthood exists yet to form one. Everyone. So they spend two days washing, an entire nation scrubbing Egypt out of the fabric and out from under their fingernails, getting clean the way you get clean for a wedding, and nobody can quite say out loud what they are getting clean for.",
    "id": "ch03-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The third morning comes, and the mountain is on fire.",
    "id": "ch03-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A cloud has swallowed the summit with thunder rolling around inside it, and lightning keeps tearing the gray open from within. Smoke climbs off the peak like the smoke of a furnace. And the ground itself is moving. The bedrock under their bare feet, which a human being trusts without ever thinking about it, is trembling like a struck drum.",
    "id": "ch03-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Threaded through all of it comes a trumpet, growing louder instead of fading, which is the one thing a sound is never supposed to do. Every sound they have ever heard has died away. This one is building, and it is coming down the slope.",
    "id": "ch03-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When Exodus describes that morning, it uses a sentence that should not work: <em>all the people saw the thunder</em>. Ex 20:18. Not heard — saw. The morning hit them so hard the senses stopped keeping their lanes. There was no version of standing in that camp that left a person unshaken.",
    "id": "ch03-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then Moses does the unthinkable. While a whole nation leans away from the mountain, he turns them around, walks them out of the camp, and lines them up at the foot of it. The text gives his reason plainly: he brought them out to meet God.",
    "id": "ch03-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And this was the plan all along. God had told Moses days earlier: “I am coming in a thick cloud, so that the people themselves can hear Me when I speak.” Nobody in that camp would have to take another man’s word for what God said. Every one of them was about to hear Him first-hand, with their own ears.",
    "id": "ch03-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What is descending onto that mountain is not a show of force but a meeting. The thunder is not the point. The voice is.",
    "id": "ch03-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Forty years later, when Moses tells the story of that morning, he reaches for words that should have been impossible to say.",
    "id": "ch03-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The LORD spoke to you face to face at the mountain from the midst of the fire,"
    ],
    "ref": "Deuteronomy 5:4",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch03-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Face to face.",
    "id": "ch03-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When Hebrew says that anywhere else, it says <em>panim el panim</em>, face to face. But what Moses says about Sinai is stranger, and closer: <em>panim be-panim</em>, face in face, a construction that appears nowhere else in Scripture.",
    "id": "ch03-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And underneath it sits something English cannot show you. <em>Panim</em> has no singular. The Hebrew word for face is always, grammatically, faces.",
    "id": "ch03-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "As if the language itself knew that no one is ever just one face.",
    "id": "ch03-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You have a market face and a midnight face. A face for strangers and a face only grief has seen. The face you wear in a crowd and the one that emerges when no one is watching.",
    "id": "ch03-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Plural meeting plural.",
    "id": "ch03-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "All of Him turned toward all of you.",
    "id": "ch03-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that means nobody in that crowd was an audience member. Every single upturned face in the desert dust had the full attention of God in the same moment, the way the sun lands on every face in a crowd at full strength without becoming weaker for each face it touches.",
    "id": "ch03-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This was never a broadcast. It was thousands of private meetings, all happening at once.",
    "id": "ch03-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then, in that same sermon, Moses gives them a detail that seems to pull the whole thing apart.",
    "id": "ch03-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Then the LORD spoke to you from the midst of the fire; you heard the sound of words, but you saw no form — only a voice."
    ],
    "ref": "Deuteronomy 4:12",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch03-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The two statements sit there together.",
    "id": "ch03-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Face to face.",
    "id": "ch03-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And you saw no face.",
    "id": "ch03-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses says both within minutes of each other and never slows down to apologize. Both are true at once.",
    "id": "ch03-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every god in Egypt had a face. Not one of them had ever spoken.",
    "id": "ch03-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This God is the exact reverse.",
    "id": "ch03-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He refused them a shape.",
    "id": "ch03-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He left nothing to carve, nothing a goldsmith could copy, and gave them instead the one thing no idol had ever given anyone: His voice, unmediated, speaking directly to them.",
    "id": "ch03-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "If the nation saw no form, and Moses still swears under oath that it was face to face, then face cannot mean eyes and a nose and a mouth.",
    "id": "ch03-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God’s face is His presence.",
    "id": "ch03-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His attention, turned toward you, with nothing standing in between.",
    "id": "ch03-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And from this morning on, the great question of the Bible is no longer, Can a human being see God?",
    "id": "ch03-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is: Will a human being stand there?",
    "id": "ch03-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Because the <em>you</em> in face to face was never aimed at Moses. Moses needs no convincing; he is the one man here who keeps running toward the fire.",
    "id": "ch03-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The sentence is aimed at the crowd at the bottom: brickmakers, grandmothers who buried children in Egypt, teenagers still sunburned from the road out of slavery. There was no priesthood yet, no veil, nothing to hide behind. On the first morning of the covenant, God’s opening position with an entire people was as simple as it will ever be stated: every face, His face, nothing in between.",
    "id": "ch03-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He did not begin with rules. The commandments came out of the fire, but the fire came first. The order is the message: He comes near before He asks for anything.",
    "id": "ch03-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The thunder keeps rolling. The trumpet keeps building.",
    "id": "ch03-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Somewhere in the rows, one person takes the first backward step. Then it is everyone: a whole nation edging away from the voice until they stand at a distance.",
    "id": "ch03-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And out of that retreat comes a request that will reorganize the rest of the Bible.",
    "id": "ch03-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Then they said to Moses, “Speak to us yourself and we will listen; but let not God speak to us, or we will die.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 20:19",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch03-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is a rejection that outlasts every empire, and it is worded like a memo. God has just made the largest offer the world has ever heard: Himself, directly, to everyone. And humanity, given its one chance to respond, sends back a counterproposal.",
    "id": "ch03-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They are not walking away from God. They want the covenant. They want the commandments, the protection, the promise. They ask for one amendment only: put a man in the middle. You talk to Moses; Moses talks to us; let that be the arrangement. They are negotiating the terms of nearness, signing up for the relationship while moving it out of arm’s reach.",
    "id": "ch03-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God offered His face. They asked for a spokesman.",
    "id": "ch03-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The request is painfully easy to recognize. “We will listen,” they said. “Just let it come through you.”",
    "id": "ch03-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I was twelve, in Mexico City.",
    "id": "ch03-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I do not remember what I did. I have tried. Whatever it was, it was small enough that a grown man cannot get it back, and big enough that at twelve I could hear my own heart in my ears, running the next hour over and over and never finding a version of it that did not end with their faces.",
    "id": "ch03-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What I remember is what I did next.",
    "id": "ch03-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I already knew exactly what my parents’ faces were going to do when they found out. So I sent my brother in to tell them.",
    "id": "ch03-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Let him say it. Let the reaction land in the other room.",
    "id": "ch03-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I stayed in the hallway, close enough to hear everything and far enough that I would not have to watch. I heard his voice. Then the pause. Then my father’s voice changed. Not the words. The pitch.",
    "id": "ch03-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I remember being glad I was not in the room.",
    "id": "ch03-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I never asked my brother whether he knew what I was using him for.",
    "id": "ch03-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I took the consequence later, in words, secondhand. I would have taken twice as much to keep from standing there while the news moved across their faces.",
    "id": "ch03-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They did not refuse God’s words. They refused His face while He said them.",
    "id": "ch03-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that instinct did not die at Sinai.",
    "id": "ch03-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the Bible plants a counter-witness on the same mountain, in the same month.",
    "id": "ch03-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "While the nation stood at the bottom asking for a man in the middle, seventy of their own elders went up.",
    "id": "ch03-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Then Moses went up with Aaron, Nadab and Abihu, and seventy of the elders of Israel, and they saw the God of Israel… Yet He did not stretch out His hand against the nobles of the sons of Israel; and they saw God, and they ate and drank."
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 24:9-11",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch03-v4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They saw God. And nothing happened to them. No fire, no death, no hand stretched out.",
    "id": "ch03-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Somebody passed the food.",
    "id": "ch03-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A thousand years of Scripture will insist that no one can see God and live. Here is the exception, and the exception is a dinner.",
    "id": "ch03-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seventy men had the very thing the nation had just declined, and they had it sitting down.",
    "id": "ch03-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Centuries later a farmer named Gideon, threshing wheat down in a winepress to hide even his harvest, looks up into the face of the angel of the LORD and cries out the same fear that gripped the crowd at Sinai, certain the sight has killed him. Heaven’s answer is almost tender: “do not fear; you shall not die.”",
    "id": "ch03-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The crowd at Sinai could not have known that. But the whole retreat was built on a misread. They fled the one thing on the mountain that was not there to hurt them.",
    "id": "ch03-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God’s reply is two sentences long, and the second one has a wound in it.",
    "id": "ch03-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "“I have heard the voice of the words of this people… They have done well in all that they have spoken. Oh that they had such a heart in them, that they would fear Me and keep all My commandments always, that it may be well with them and with their sons forever!”"
    ],
    "ref": "Deuteronomy 5:28–29",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch03-v5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first sentence: They have done well. He grants the request, and the compliment is not ironic. Their fear was not foolish. He made them; He knows what standing unshielded in that fire costs a mortal body, and He does not mock His creatures for trembling at Him. So the arrangement is approved.",
    "id": "ch03-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "From now on, Moses climbs alone, and the nation gets the words secondhand.",
    "id": "ch03-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Exactly as requested.",
    "id": "ch03-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They asked for distance, and distance was granted. And it stood, curtain after curtain, priest after priest, for more than a thousand years.",
    "id": "ch03-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God honors human choices even when they cost us Him.",
    "id": "ch03-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Be careful what you ask God for out of fear.",
    "id": "ch03-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He may dignify it by saying yes.",
    "id": "ch03-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then comes the second sentence.",
    "id": "ch03-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Our English translations render it, “Oh that they had such a heart,” a wistful phrase.",
    "id": "ch03-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Hebrew is <em>mi-yitten</em>, literally, “Who will give?” There is no true English equivalent, because it is neither a request nor a command.",
    "id": "ch03-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Who will give them a heart like this, always?",
    "id": "ch03-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The closest thing English owns is if only. This is God talking, the same voice that is, at this very moment, shaking a mountain, and He is saying if only. He is asking a question into the open air with no one above Him to answer it. He is not gathering information. He is longing, out loud.",
    "id": "ch03-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He could have the thing He wants. He could level the mountain into the plain. He could reach into every chest in that crowd and rewrite every heart where it stands. He will not.",
    "id": "ch03-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Because a heart is the one room He refuses to enter by force. It only opens from the inside. Fear He can produce by the ton, and the mountain is producing it right now. Terror obeys only while the fire burns.",
    "id": "ch03-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What He wants is a heart that stays after the mountain goes quiet. And that is the single thing even God cannot manufacture, because the moment it was forced it would stop being what He wanted. It can only be given.",
    "id": "ch03-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So the God of Sinai stands inside His own fire and sighs the sentence of every unrequited love in history: “Who will give?”",
    "id": "ch03-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the question does not remain unanswered.",
    "id": "ch03-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Centuries later, speaking to the descendants of this very crowd from inside the wreckage of exile, God answers His own question.",
    "id": "ch03-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He answers it with the same verb.",
    "id": "ch03-p85"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Moreover, I will give you a new heart and put a new spirit within you; and I will remove the heart of stone from your flesh and give you a heart of flesh."
    ],
    "ref": "Ezekiel 36:26",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch03-v6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the mountain: Who will give? From the exile: I will give. The question was never rhetorical. It was a promise waiting for its verse. The heart He would not force on them, He was already planning to gift to them.",
    "id": "ch03-p86"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He agrees with their reasons and mourns their choice in the same breath, and between those two sentences sits the whole grief of God. He signs what they asked for, and before the ink is dry He is already drafting the promise that will one day undo it. It is the sound of a father saying yes to being loved from farther away — while quietly writing, in the other room, a will that leaves the children everything, including the nearness they were afraid of.",
    "id": "ch03-p87"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And if you wonder whether His if only knew something real, the same mountain answers inside of six weeks.",
    "id": "ch03-p88"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses goes up into the fire to receive the words. The fire keeps burning. And the people at the bottom, unable to bear the waiting and the silence, go to Aaron with a sentence that answers Sinai point for point: “make us gods who will go before us.” The Hebrew says <em>l'fanenu</em>, before our faces. They want a face out front again, the kind they grew up under.",
    "id": "ch03-p89"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And what they commission is the exact inverse of what they fled. Sinai was a voice with no form. The calf is a form with no voice — molten, portable, and above all silent. Silent is the feature.",
    "id": "ch03-p90"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A calf will receive your gold and your dancing and your festival and never once interrupt. It holds perfectly still while its owners live however they like in front of it, because a face you can control is the entire point. It will never call a name out of a crowd and never ask where are you. No golden thing has ever stood in its own fire and sighed if only.",
    "id": "ch03-p91"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the psalms know what a face like that does to the people watching it: <em>those who make them will become like them.</em> Psalm 115:8.",
    "id": "ch03-p92"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The crowd at the foot of the mountain wanted God’s words without His face. The crowd around the calf wanted a face without God’s words. Message without meeting, or statue without speech: any arrangement at all, so long as it is not the living encounter. They wanted a god. They wanted one with a face that could not look back.",
    "id": "ch03-p93"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This is the hour most of the Bible will be downstream from. The priesthood, the curtains, the one man once a year behind a veil, all the machinery of mediated distance: every bit of it gets built on the far side of this day.",
    "id": "ch03-p94"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "We read the middle of the Bible as if the curtains were God’s temperament. They were never His idea of the relationship. They were His accommodation of ours.",
    "id": "ch03-p95"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In Eden, the hiding was something we built. At Sinai, the distance was something we asked for.",
    "id": "ch03-p96"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the true size of what was refused that morning is measured in the world’s own throne rooms.",
    "id": "ch03-p97"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the empires of the Bible’s day, seeing the king’s face was a rank.",
    "id": "ch03-p98"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The book of Esther names the seven men, seven in an empire of a hundred and twenty-seven provinces, who were allowed to look upon the king’s face.",
    "id": "ch03-p99"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Everyone else stayed clear of that room, because the law of the inner court was one sentence long:",
    "id": "ch03-p100"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Enter unsummoned and you die, unless the king extends the golden scepter.",
    "id": "ch03-p101"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Even the queen was not exempt.",
    "id": "ch03-p102"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Esther wore the king’s crown and shared the king’s bed, and before she tried that door, she fasted for three days and walked toward it carrying a five-word will:",
    "id": "ch03-p103"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“If I perish, I perish.”",
    "id": "ch03-p104"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Israel had a proverb that said it from the other direction. In the light of a king’s face is life.",
    "id": "ch03-p105"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Esther wrote a will because she knew what the dark of it was worth.",
    "id": "ch03-p106"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is what a face cost in the ancient world.",
    "id": "ch03-p107"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The superpowers of the earth allowed seven men to see a face.",
    "id": "ch03-p108"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The God of heaven brought an entire nation of freed slaves to the base of His mountain, people who had spent their whole lives as property, who held no rank of any kind, who had kept exactly none of the commandments because the commandments had not yet been given.",
    "id": "ch03-p109"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And His opening move was to offer every last one of them the access that empires executed men over.",
    "id": "ch03-p110"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Offered, not awarded.",
    "id": "ch03-p111"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nothing on their résumés earned it, because there was nothing on their résumés at all.",
    "id": "ch03-p112"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The presence came before the performance.",
    "id": "ch03-p113"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When the psalms later cry, <em>seek His face</em>, this is the vocabulary they are reaching for: commoners petitioning for a royal audience, except that this King has left the petition pre-approved.",
    "id": "ch03-p114"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that court title was never abolished.",
    "id": "ch03-p115"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Where it finally lands is nearly the last sentence in the Bible.",
    "id": "ch03-p116"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Moses’ sermon has a date on it.",
    "id": "ch03-p117"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is speaking four decades after the mountain, at the far edge of the wilderness, and nearly every adult who stood in those rows is buried in the sand behind him.",
    "id": "ch03-p118"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His audience is their children.",
    "id": "ch03-p119"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses looks out at a congregation of people who were not there and tells them this:",
    "id": "ch03-p120"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The LORD did not make this covenant with our fathers, but with us, with all those of us alive here today."
    ],
    "ref": "Deuteronomy 5:3",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch03-v7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "By every rule of memory, that sentence is false.",
    "id": "ch03-p121"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses says it on purpose, because covenant does not run on memory’s rules.",
    "id": "ch03-p122"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "History says: that was them, back then.",
    "id": "ch03-p123"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Covenant says: this is you, right now.",
    "id": "ch03-p124"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He hands a generation an encounter they never attended and dares them to carry it as their own.",
    "id": "ch03-p125"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He refuses to give the children a story about their parents’ most terrifying day.",
    "id": "ch03-p126"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He gives them an inheritance instead.",
    "id": "ch03-p127"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The morning God spoke, he tells them, God spoke with you.",
    "id": "ch03-p128"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And underneath it, Moses is setting the precedent this entire book stands on.",
    "id": "ch03-p129"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "If the meeting at the mountain could be handed intact to one generation that missed it, it can be handed to forty.",
    "id": "ch03-p130"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The offer travels.",
    "id": "ch03-p131"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It has been traveling ever since, sermon to sermon, page to page, down all the centuries to wherever you are sitting as you hear this.",
    "id": "ch03-p132"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The audience of that sermon was never only the children standing in front of Moses.",
    "id": "ch03-p133"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was everyone who missed the mountain.",
    "id": "ch03-p134"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Which is, by now, you.",
    "id": "ch03-p135"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Sinai leaves one picture behind: a whole nation standing face to face with God, begging Him to stop.",
    "id": "ch03-p136"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Thousands of faces backing away from the only fire that ever wanted them near it.",
    "id": "ch03-p137"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is an older picture than this one, and there is only one man standing in it.",
    "id": "ch03-p138"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Centuries before Sinai ever smoked, in the dark at a river crossing, the same Presence came down for one man, alone, at night, with no trumpet and no crowd to hide in.",
    "id": "ch03-p139"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that man did not step back.",
    "id": "ch03-p140"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He locked his arms around what a whole nation would one day flee, and he held on until the sky went gray.",
    "id": "ch03-p141"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At Sinai, the crowd begged God to stop.",
    "id": "ch03-p142"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the river, God had to ask a man to let go.",
    "id": "ch03-p143"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "num": 4,
  "id": "ch04",
  "title": "The Word He Kept Rehearsing",
  "hook": "Jacob has one word he cannot stop rehearsing, and it is the word this whole book is about.",
  "glossary": [],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch04-river"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is the black middle of the night at a river crossing, and a man is sending away everything he loves.",
    "id": "ch04-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The river is the Jabbok, and its ford is shallow enough to walk. All night he has been handing his life across it: two wives, two servant girls, eleven children, then the herds, the flocks, the camels, everything twenty years of labor has earned him. All of it passes over the dark water to the far bank until nothing remains on the near bank but him. Genesis marks the moment with one short sentence: Jacob was left alone.",
    "id": "ch04-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is not camping. He is bracing. Days ago, he sent messengers south with careful, appeasing words for the brother he has not seen in twenty years. The messengers came back with a report that has been sitting in his stomach ever since: your brother is coming to meet you, and four hundred men are coming with him. Four hundred men is an army. Jacob knows exactly what he did to raise it.",
    "id": "ch04-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Because twenty years ago there was another darkness, and he was on the other side of it. His father Isaac lay old in his tent, his eyes too dim to see, ready to speak the blessing that would set the family’s future. And Jacob walked in wearing his brother’s clothes, with goatskin tied over his smooth hands and neck. And when the blind man asked the question that should have ended everything, “Who are you, my son?” Jacob said, “I am Esau.”",
    "id": "ch04-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His father was not easy to fool. He called the boy close, felt the hands, and said, half to himself, “The voice is the voice of Jacob, but the hands are the hands of Esau.” Then he blessed him anyway.",
    "id": "ch04-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The old rabbis could not leave that blindness alone. His eyes had failed, they said, from the day on the mountain when he was bound — a man who looks into that much glory does not keep his sight. Maybe.",
    "id": "ch04-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What the text makes certain is quieter and heavier: this covenant was never going to travel by what Isaac could see. He loved the wrong son, and he meant to hand him the blessing by the taste of his cooking and the smell of his coat. It went where it went for another reason. Years earlier, when the twins first wrestled inside her, Rebekah had gone and asked God herself, and He had told her the older would serve the younger. She is the only mother in Genesis who goes looking for God’s word and comes home carrying it. She made sure the blessing found the son that word had already named.",
    "id": "ch04-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Sight failed on that mountain. The word did not.",
    "id": "ch04-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The scheme had a single requirement, and it is the requirement Jacob’s whole life has been built on: a face that could not be checked. His first blessing came out of a darkness where no one could see who he was. And when the truth surfaced, his brother consoled himself out loud with a schedule: their father would die soon, and then so would Jacob.",
    "id": "ch04-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So Jacob ran. He has been living at the far end of that running ever since.",
    "id": "ch04-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now the running is over. The land God has ordered him to return to is on the other side of this river, and so is Esau.",
    "id": "ch04-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So Jacob spends his last day doing what Jacob has always done. He engineers. He splits his camp in two, so that if Esau falls on one half, the other might escape. He assembles a gift that is really a ransom, more than five hundred animals, and he refuses to send it as one caravan. He sorts it into waves and puts open ground between them, so that his brother will ride into apology after apology all morning long, each wave driven by a servant coached in the same script: “these belong to your servant Jacob, they are a present, and he himself is behind us.” And then, for once, we hear what Jacob is saying to himself.",
    "id": "ch04-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "and you shall say, “Behold, your servant Jacob also is behind us.” For he said, “I will appease him with the present that goes before me. Then afterward I will see his face; perhaps he will accept me.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 32:20",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch04-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>I will appease him</em>, our translation says; the Hebrew says <em>I will cover his face</em>. <em>The present that goes before me</em>: <em>before my face</em>. <em>Then afterward I will see his face</em>. <em>Perhaps he will accept me</em>: literally, <em>perhaps he will lift up my face</em>. Four times in one anxious breath. His face, my face, his face, my face.",
    "id": "ch04-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A frightened man circling a single word the way a tongue keeps finding a sore tooth. It is not Esau’s army he keeps saying out loud. It is Esau’s face. The face he cheated, the face he fled, the face he has been seeing with his eyes closed for twenty years.",
    "id": "ch04-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the last phrase in his speech opens another door: <em>perhaps he will lift up my face</em>.",
    "id": "ch04-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jacob means it the way a guilty man means it. He is picturing himself face-down in the dirt in front of an offended lord, waiting to find out whether he will be raised to his feet or run through.",
    "id": "ch04-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But in his panic, he has reached for words with a long future ahead of them. Centuries from this night, God will fold that exact phrase into the blessing His priests are commanded to lay on every face in Israel.",
    "id": "ch04-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The frightened man at the ford is praying it first.",
    "id": "ch04-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the first phrase in that same speech opens a door of its own.",
    "id": "ch04-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>I will cover his face.</em> By the time Israel has a tabernacle, <em>kaphar</em> will be its word for atonement.",
    "id": "ch04-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is trying to buy a covering for a face he cannot bear to meet. And one noun comes off that verb. <em>Kapporet</em>. The covering. It is what Israel will call the gold lid at the center of everything. The mercy seat.",
    "id": "ch04-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two words, in one frightened breath at a river. One of them becomes the blessing every priest says over every face in Israel. The other becomes the place.",
    "id": "ch04-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But something shows through the seams of that plan. <em>I will see his face</em>. That is not the sentence of a man arranging to slip past his brother. A man who only wanted to survive Esau would be walking the other way. Strip the terror from the sentence and what is left underneath looks almost like longing.",
    "id": "ch04-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the last of the herds shakes itself dry on the far bank, and he is alone in the dark with the sound of the water.",
    "id": "ch04-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And someone grabs him.",
    "id": "ch04-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Genesis gives the moment no introduction at all. No light in the sky, no voice, no warning that anything is near. Just this: <em>a man wrestled with him until daybreak</em>.",
    "id": "ch04-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A man.",
    "id": "ch04-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is all the text will say at first, and the silence is honest because it is all Jacob knows. Arms out of the darkness, a stranger’s full weight slamming into his, and no face. The night is too black to show him one.",
    "id": "ch04-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the dark is doing something to this man that nothing else in his life ever managed. Every scheme Jacob ever ran was a game played on faces. He read them, charmed them, dressed for them, fooled one who could not see.",
    "id": "ch04-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now he is locked body to body with someone whose face he cannot find and who will not say a word, hour after hour, and none of it works down here. There is nothing to read and no one to perform for.",
    "id": "ch04-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "For the first time in his life, all Jacob has is Jacob.",
    "id": "ch04-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They fight until the sky begins to think about morning, and what ends the long stalemate is stranger than the fight. <em>When the man saw that he had not prevailed against him</em>, Genesis says, <em>he touched the socket of Jacob's thigh, and the hip came out of joint</em>. Not struck. Touched. A touch that unsockets a hip is not a wrestling move; it is a disclosure.",
    "id": "ch04-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Anyone who can end the match with a touch could have ended it in the first minute, which means every hour of that stalemate was chosen. The strength in the dark had been measuring itself down to the size of one exhausted man all night long, on purpose. Whoever this is was never fighting to escape Jacob’s grip. He was staying inside it.",
    "id": "ch04-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then, out of the dark, the first words of the whole night.",
    "id": "ch04-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Then he said, “Let me go, for the dawn is breaking.” But he said, “I will not let you go unless you bless me.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 32:26",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch04-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The famous half of that verse is Jacob’s: I will not let you go. But the first half is stranger, and it belongs to God.",
    "id": "ch04-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Let me go.”",
    "id": "ch04-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hand that unsocketed a hip is asking permission to leave. He does not pry the fingers loose; He could. Instead, the One who could have ended this in the first minute stands inside a wounded man’s arms and asks.",
    "id": "ch04-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He will overpower a hip, and He will not overpower a hold.",
    "id": "ch04-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Why He must be gone before sunrise, Scripture never explains. The text does not look up. It watches Jacob’s arms.",
    "id": "ch04-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“I will not let you go unless you bless me.”",
    "id": "ch04-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What is left of the schemer? His hip is out of joint. The wrestling is over. The only move remaining to him is to hang his whole weight on the neck of someone he cannot beat and refuse to let go.",
    "id": "ch04-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He does not ask to be spared. He says, “Bless me.”",
    "id": "ch04-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This is the man who bought his brother’s birthright for a bowl of stew and stole his brother’s blessing in a costume. Every blessing Jacob had ever received had come through deception, disguise, or someone else’s blindness.",
    "id": "ch04-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Tonight, ruined and clinging, he asks for one. Out loud. To someone’s face.",
    "id": "ch04-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is a kind of prayer that has run out of arguments and pride. All it has left is the refusal to let go of God.",
    "id": "ch04-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is Jacob’s prayer.",
    "id": "ch04-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And soon, that kind of holding on will have a name.",
    "id": "ch04-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is the second time in Jacob’s life that a voice from the darkness has asked him who he is. The first time, in his blind father’s tent, the question was, “Who are you, my son?” A blessing hung on the answer, and the answer was a lie.",
    "id": "ch04-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now the scene has come back around: darkness again, a blessing on the table again, the question again, in different words. And Jacob, held and hurt and out of costumes at last, says:",
    "id": "ch04-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Jacob.”",
    "id": "ch04-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One word. The truth. The first time, his name was the one thing the blessing required him to hide. This time, his real name is the one thing the blessing requires him to say. And the moment it is out of his mouth, the voice in the dark takes it from him. “You will no longer be called Jacob, but Israel.”",
    "id": "ch04-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Israel.",
    "id": "ch04-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Scripture gives the reason in the same breath: <em>you have striven with God and with men, and have prevailed.</em> Not beaten Him. Held Him.",
    "id": "ch04-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God takes the one night a man fought Him and writes it into the name. He could have called him the one who yielded. He called him the one who wrestles with God. He did not forgive the struggle. He honored it.",
    "id": "ch04-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then He named a nation after it. Every psalm that ever argued with heaven has been praying under a flag God stitched Himself, at this river, in the dark.",
    "id": "ch04-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The name does not arrive when Jacob wins, and it does not arrive when he behaves. It comes the moment he stops pretending to be his brother and says his own name out loud. He had spent twenty years in another man’s clothes to get a blessing. He could not become who he was made to be until he stopped performing a man he was not.",
    "id": "ch04-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The truth first. Then the name.",
    "id": "ch04-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He blessed him there. The blessing he once stole in a darkness he engineered, he receives at last in a darkness that came for him, under his own name.",
    "id": "ch04-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "So Jacob named the place Peniel, for he said, “I have seen God face to face, yet my life has been preserved.”",
     "Now the sun rose upon him just as he crossed over Penuel, and he was limping on his thigh."
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 32:30–31",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch04-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Peniel. It is Hebrew for the face of God. Jacob limps out of the only fight of his life he could not win, having survived a night with God and been handed a new name, and he names the ground for neither one. He names it for the seeing. <em>I have seen God face to face</em> — said of a night in which he saw nothing at all. The dark made sure of it, start to finish. And he is not wrong anyway. For one entire night there was nothing in between, not even the width of a shadow. A whole nation will learn that lesson at a burning mountain someday. Jacob learned it with his arms.",
    "id": "ch04-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then Genesis gives him a sunrise.",
    "id": "ch04-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>The sun rose upon him just as he crossed over</em>. The dawn His opponent would not stay for comes up anyway on the man He blessed, and finds him limping.",
    "id": "ch04-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The name and the limp arrive one verse apart, and they will share his body for the rest of his life. He asked for a blessing and got one, and it came with a wound he will lean into at every step from here to his grave, a limp he will wear the way other men wear a wedding ring: the permanent mark of the night he was claimed.",
    "id": "ch04-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody stands that close to God and walks away merely improved.",
    "id": "ch04-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jacob walks away marked.",
    "id": "ch04-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He looks up from the new limp, and the future he has dreaded for twenty years is coming across the open ground toward him. Esau. The four hundred.",
    "id": "ch04-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "All his engineering is already out there ahead of him: the waves of livestock, the coached servants, the whole machinery of appeasement grinding forward.",
    "id": "ch04-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man who once put a bribe and a river between himself and this face now walks out in front of his wives and children, first in the column, nothing between him and his brother. He bows himself to the ground seven times as he comes. The choreography a beaten man owes the king who spared him.",
    "id": "ch04-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Esau wrecks the choreography. He runs. Genesis needs a whole spill of verbs to keep up with him: <em>he ran to meet him, embraced him, fell on his neck, kissed him, and they wept</em>. Twenty years of rehearsed terror, an army standing idle down the road, and the meeting Jacob sent five hundred animals trying to survive turns into two gray-bearded brothers standing in a field, holding each other, crying.",
    "id": "ch04-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Esau tries to wave the ransom home. “I have plenty, my brother; keep what is yours.” And Jacob presses it on him, and the reason he gives, out loud, in daylight, is the sentence the whole night was for.",
    "id": "ch04-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Jacob said, “No, please, if now I have found favor in your sight, then take my present from my hand, for I see your face as one sees the face of God, and you have received me favorably.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 33:10",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch04-v4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>I see your face as one sees the face of God</em>. Hours. That is all the distance between Peniel and this sentence, between the face he could not see and the face he could not stop rehearsing. Nowhere else in Scripture do the two meetings stand this close together, the face of God and the face of a brother, one sunrise apart.",
    "id": "ch04-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The night with God came first. It is what made the morning with Esau survivable. And there, in the middle of an ordinary sentence, is the phrase he rehearsed at the ford: <em>you have received me favorably</em>, the raised-up, lifted-face pardon he had asked God for in the dark.",
    "id": "ch04-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It happened. The face he dreaded turned kind the morning after the night he finally stopped managing faces and showed his own. Not every Esau runs. But Jacob’s twenty years of rehearsing ended somewhere, and it was not at the meeting he feared. It ended the night before, at the river, in the grip of the One whose face makes the other faces bearable.",
    "id": "ch04-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jacob lives a long time after the Jabbok, and the night never wears off. The limp sees to that, every step, for the rest of his story.",
    "id": "ch04-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But it is the vocabulary that goes deepest. It surfaces again near the end of his life. He is old, a famine is strangling the land, and the son he loved most, the boy he has mourned as dead for the better part of his old age, turns out to be alive in Egypt, alive and powerful and asking for him.",
    "id": "ch04-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They carry the old man south in wagons. Joseph rides out to meet the caravan, and when he sees his father he falls on his neck and weeps there a long time. And Israel, holding his son, reaches all the way back to a riverbank for his words.",
    "id": "ch04-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Then Israel said to Joseph, “Now let me die, since I have seen your face, that you are still alive.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 46:30",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch04-v5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Now let me die, since I have seen your face.”",
    "id": "ch04-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A whole lifetime, distilled.",
    "id": "ch04-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The young man at the ford counted his life in livestock, five hundred animals deep; the old man in Egypt sums his up in one son’s face and calls it enough.",
    "id": "ch04-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seeing the face has become his word for finishing. Once a man has it, even death loses its leverage.",
    "id": "ch04-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Scripture liked that sentence far too much to leave it in Genesis. Centuries later, in the temple courts in Jerusalem, an old man named Simeon is waiting out the last of his life on a strange promise: the Spirit of God has told him he will not see death before his eyes have seen the Lord’s Christ. One morning a young couple comes in from the countryside carrying a newborn baby, and the old man crosses the court, takes the child up into his arms, looks down into the small new face, and gives Jacob’s sentence back to heaven.",
    "id": "ch04-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "“Now Lord, You are releasing Your bond-servant to depart in peace, according to Your word; for my eyes have seen Your salvation,”"
    ],
    "ref": "Luke 2:29–30",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch04-v6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Now You are releasing me. My eyes have seen.” It is the Jabbok sentence, aged nearly two thousand years, resting on a baby: an old man of Israel holding a face and telling God he is free to die content. Somebody kept Jacob’s arithmetic all that time. One face. That was always the whole sum.",
    "id": "ch04-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jacob got one night. The next man will get forty years.",
    "id": "ch04-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And on the first day of it, he will do what Adam did.",
    "id": "ch04-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He will hide his face.",
    "id": "ch04-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "num": 5,
  "id": "ch05",
  "title": "Mouth to Mouth",
  "hook": "The fire never shouted at him. It burned, and waited, and only said his name once he had turned his face toward it.",
  "glossary": [
   {
    "term": "Ehyeh asher Ehyeh",
    "gloss": "Ehyeh asher Ehyeh."
   },
   {
    "term": "ehyeh",
    "gloss": "Ehyeh asher Ehyeh."
   },
   {
    "term": "chillah panim",
    "gloss": "The idiom is chillah panim: to soften the face, to stroke the face, the motion of a hand gentling a cheek."
   },
   {
    "term": "Panim",
    "gloss": "The idiom is chillah panim: to soften the face, to stroke the face, the motion of a hand gentling a cheek."
   },
   {
    "term": "kavod",
    "gloss": "The word he uses is kavod, and at its root it means weight, heaviness, sheer mass."
   },
   {
    "term": "Peh el peh",
    "gloss": "Peh el peh: my mouth to his mouth, said of no one else God ever spoke with."
   },
   {
    "term": "temunah",
    "gloss": "The word is temunah, form, and we met it once already, at Sinai, in the nation’s protection clause: you saw no form, only a voice."
   }
  ],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch05-bush"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Before the mountain burned for a nation, it burned for an audience of one, and the fire was small enough to fit inside a bush.",
    "id": "ch05-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The one man is eighty years old, and the sheep he is keeping are not his. Forty years ago he was a prince of Egypt, raised in the palace at the center of the world. Then he killed a man to stop a beating, buried the body in the sand, and found out by the next afternoon that his secret was already walking around without him.",
    "id": "ch05-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He ran for his life, and the running took everything with it: the prince became a fugitive, the fugitive married into a shepherd family in Midian, and the shepherd grew old walking another man’s flock through the same brown wilderness, year after year, until the silence out there became the closest thing he had to a native language.",
    "id": "ch05-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Today he has pushed the animals farther than usual, to the west side of the wilderness, to the foot of a mountain called Horeb.",
    "id": "ch05-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When it burns for the nation there will be six hundred thousand men on foot at the base of it, the text says, besides the women and the children. Today there is no nation, no thunder, no trumpet. One old man, and sheep.",
    "id": "ch05-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then, at the edge of his eye, fire. A thornbush has caught, which in a desert means nothing; dry brush goes up all the time, burns hard for a minute, and collapses into ash. Moses has watched it happen his whole exile.",
    "id": "ch05-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So he waits for the collapse, and it does not come. The bush burns and stays a bush. The flames keep working and the branches will not surrender to them, a fire acting like it needs nothing from what it is burning, and the shepherd puts down four decades of routine and walks toward it: “I must turn aside and see this marvelous sight.”",
    "id": "ch05-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>When the LORD saw that he turned aside to look, God called to him from the midst of the bush.</em> Ex 3:4. First the man turns, then the voice comes. The fire did not shout at a shepherd walking past. It burned, and waited, and let itself be noticed, and only when a man gave it his face did it say his name. “Moses, Moses.” And the old fugitive answers with the two words that will become the truest thing he ever offers God: “Here I am.”",
    "id": "ch05-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Sandals off. The dirt he has trudged for forty years is holy ground. And the voice tells him Whose voice it is.",
    "id": "ch05-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "He said also, “I am the God of your father, the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob.” Then Moses hid his face, for he was afraid to look at God."
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 3:6",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Here I am,” he said. Then he found out to whose voice he was answering, and hid.",
    "id": "ch05-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His hands come up before he decides anything. That is what a body does. But the Torah writes the moment down and never forgets it, because it is the first sentence Scripture ever puts between this man and the face of God: Moses hid his face, afraid to look.",
    "id": "ch05-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Five books from now, on its very last page, the Torah will write one more sentence about Moses and the face of God.",
    "id": "ch05-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What follows at the bush is an argument, and Moses loses it one objection at a time. “Who am I,” he asks, “that I should go to Pharaoh?” And God declines the question entirely. “Certainly I will be with you.” Moses’s résumé is beside the point; the only credential that will ever matter on this errand is Who is going along.",
    "id": "ch05-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So Moses reaches for a bigger objection. “Suppose I go to the sons of Israel and tell them the God of their fathers has sent me, and they ask me His name. What shall I say?” He is asking for a credential of a different kind, and what comes out of the fire is a name no one on earth has heard.",
    "id": "ch05-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "God said to Moses, “I AM WHO I AM”; and He said, “Thus you shall say to the sons of Israel, ‘I AM has sent me to you.’”"
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 3:14",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Ehyeh asher Ehyeh.</em> I AM WHO I AM. The strangeness was the point. Every god in Egypt had a name that worked like a label on a statue: this one is Ra, that one is Horus, sort them by falcon and jackal. The voice in the bush hands over a verb. To be. Present tense. First person. An existence, not a label: nothing you could carve, only something you can stand in front of.",
    "id": "ch05-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the name has something Moses may not have caught in the moment: God had already used the word. “Certainly I will be with you,” He said, one objection ago, and in Hebrew that promise runs <em>ehyeh</em> immach. I AM, with you.",
    "id": "ch05-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The name was already inside the promise. Presence is not something this God occasionally does. It is what He calls Himself.",
    "id": "ch05-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses keeps arguing anyway. “What if they will not believe me?” God puts signs in his hands, a staff that becomes a serpent, and still the old man will not come out from behind the last objection, the one he has been saving, the one he has built his whole life around.",
    "id": "ch05-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Then Moses said to the LORD, “Please, Lord, I have never been eloquent, neither recently nor in time past, nor since You have spoken to Your servant; for I am slow of speech and slow of tongue.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 4:10",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He has surveyed this wound completely. Never eloquent. Not recently. Not in time past. And then the clause that costs him the most: nor since You have spoken to Your servant. Even now, Lord. Even with the bush still burning in front of me, even after Your voice has said my name twice, the tongue has not loosened; even You, speaking, have not changed it.",
    "id": "ch05-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Our English says slow of speech, and the Hebrew is blunter: heavy of mouth and heavy of tongue. That is how it feels from inside, and Moses has had forty years of desert silence to feel it. The words weigh too much. By the time his mouth has lifted them, the moment they were for has passed.",
    "id": "ch05-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every other objection was about Israel, about Pharaoh, about circumstances. This one is about him. This is the sentence he has rehearsed so long it has started to sound like identity, and he lays it down in front of the fire like a verdict he expects God to sign.",
    "id": "ch05-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God answers the heavy mouth with a question that has a whole country behind it.",
    "id": "ch05-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The LORD said to him, “Who has made man’s mouth? Or who makes him mute or deaf, or seeing or blind? Is it not I, the LORD?”"
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 4:11",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The edge on that question is Egyptian. There was a ritual in the country Moses grew up in, performed in its temples and at every funeral it held, called the Opening of the Mouth. Priests took a ritual adze, a small copper blade on a handle, and held it to the carved lips of statues and the bandaged mouths of mummies, so that the dead could breathe, eat, and speak in the world to come.",
    "id": "ch05-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every god Moses had grown up around had a mouth that human hands had to open. Gods who could not speak until a human being pried them open. Into that memory God drops His question. Who has made man’s mouth? I do not open mouths with an adze. I made the mouth.",
    "id": "ch05-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then comes the promise the whole chapter hangs on: “Now therefore go, and I, even I, will be with your mouth and teach you what you are to say.”",
    "id": "ch05-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Under the Hebrew of that promise, the name is already there. “I will be with your mouth”: <em>ehyeh</em> im picha. There is the name again.",
    "id": "ch05-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The name God unveiled to human history minutes ago, I AM, He now sets down directly on the one part of Moses that Moses believes disqualifies him.",
    "id": "ch05-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I AM, with your mouth.",
    "id": "ch05-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He offers no cure for the mouth, and no argument against Moses’s verdict on it. Instead He takes the holiest word Moses has just heard, His own name, and places it inside the very weakness Moses has brought Him.",
    "id": "ch05-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Even then Moses flinches. “Please, Lord, send someone else.” And God allows the compromise; Aaron can come along and do the talking. But the concession cannot dislodge the promise, and the wording proves it: “I will be with your mouth and with his mouth.” Aaron is added. The promise does not move.",
    "id": "ch05-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God never once argues with Moses about whether the weakness is real. He does not tell Moses he is wrong about himself. He tells him that this weakness is not the end of the conversation.",
    "id": "ch05-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "My own heavy tongue is one word long.",
    "id": "ch05-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "For a while, I was living in two rooms.",
    "id": "ch05-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In one, I stood in front of people telling them what God could do, and then watched Him do it. I watched people get healed. I watched God catch somebody on the way down.",
    "id": "ch05-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then I drove home.",
    "id": "ch05-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The room emptied behind me. The people were gone. The microphone was gone. Every story I had told that night was about what God had done for somebody else.",
    "id": "ch05-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I pulled into the driveway and turned the engine off, and sat there in the quiet a car makes when it stops. The street had nothing on it. One light was on in the front of the house, low, the kind you leave burning yourself so the place will not look empty when you come back to it.",
    "id": "ch05-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then I went inside, and there was the life I could not tell from the stage.",
    "id": "ch05-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Divorced.",
    "id": "ch05-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There it is.",
    "id": "ch05-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The word I spent years hiding inside longer sentences so I would never have to let it stand there by itself.",
    "id": "ch05-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I know what I would have thought about a man carrying that word.",
    "id": "ch05-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I had thought it about other men.",
    "id": "ch05-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Never to their faces.",
    "id": "ch05-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So I brought it to God the way Moses brought Him his mouth.",
    "id": "ch05-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I set the word down between us, the way you put something on a table when you want the other person looking at it instead of at you.",
    "id": "ch05-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Here. This is why You cannot use me.",
    "id": "ch05-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I waited for Him to agree.",
    "id": "ch05-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He never has.",
    "id": "ch05-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He looked at the word.",
    "id": "ch05-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He kept looking at me.",
    "id": "ch05-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He has never told me it did not hurt. He has never promised me the doors would open again.",
    "id": "ch05-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He just keeps coming to me where I am.",
    "id": "ch05-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the heavy mouth holds. It tells Pharaoh to let a nation go and keeps saying it through ten catastrophes. It sings on the far shore of a sea with a drowned army in it.",
    "id": "ch05-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That road ends back at this same mountain, with a nation begging for God’s voice to come secondhand, and gold poured into a mouth that would never open.",
    "id": "ch05-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the last chapter left one piece of that terrible afternoon up on the summit, and that is where the heavy mouth finally opens.",
    "id": "ch05-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "While the calf is taking shape at the bottom of the mountain, God tells Moses what the people are doing below. And then He says a thing that has no business in a sentence spoken by God: “Now then let Me alone, that My anger may burn against them.”",
    "id": "ch05-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Let Me alone.”",
    "id": "ch05-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody is holding Him. Moses has not spoken, has not moved, has not lifted a hand; and God is talking like a grip is already on Him that He will not break by force.",
    "id": "ch05-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He said it once before. At a river crossing in the dark, to a man who would not let go.",
    "id": "ch05-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jacob held on with his arms. Moses holds on with his mouth.",
    "id": "ch05-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He entreats the LORD his God, the text says, and pleads a nation’s case into the fire: what will Egypt say, remember Abraham, remember what You swore.",
    "id": "ch05-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And under the English word “entreats,” Hebrew is drawing a picture. The idiom is <em>chillah panim</em>: to soften the face, to stroke the face, the motion of a hand gentling a cheek.",
    "id": "ch05-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is the Bible’s own word for intercession. Not paperwork pushed across a courtroom bench toward a judge. A hand laid on the face of God, and the face does not turn away.",
    "id": "ch05-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two scenes, one mountain, the same afternoon.",
    "id": "ch05-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the base, a crowd is rubbing oil into a golden face that cannot feel it, courting a god they made precisely because it makes no demands.",
    "id": "ch05-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the summit, a shepherd with a heavy mouth lays his words against the living face that made him, and the face lets itself be touched, and softens.",
    "id": "ch05-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The calf costs them dearly, and its aftermath produces the most daring negotiation of Moses’s life.",
    "id": "ch05-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God tells Moses to take the people up to the promised land. An angel will go ahead of them and clear the way. Nothing promised is withdrawn: the land still flows with milk and honey, the enemies still fall, the future they walked out of Egypt toward still arrives on schedule.",
    "id": "ch05-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is one change in the terms, stated almost gently: “I will not go up in your midst,” because a stiff-necked people could not survive My nearness on the road.",
    "id": "ch05-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "On paper, the offer is generous. Moses reads it as what it is: everything, minus everything.",
    "id": "ch05-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses, by now, meets God at a tent pitched outside the camp, where the pillar of cloud comes down and the LORD speaks to him face to face, as a man speaks to his friend.",
    "id": "ch05-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A man who has known that kind of nearness knows exactly what is being subtracted.",
    "id": "ch05-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He turns the offer down flat.",
    "id": "ch05-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "And He said, “My presence shall go with you, and I will give you rest.”",
     "Then he said to Him, “If Your presence does not go with us, do not lead us up from here.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 33:14–15",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Our English Bibles print “presence,” and “presence” is not wrong. But the Hebrew word underneath it is the word this whole book is tracking: <em>panim</em>. <em>My face shall go with you. If Your face does not go, do not carry us up from here.</em>",
    "id": "ch05-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses is not haggling over logistics. He is saying: keep the milk and honey. A promised land without Your face is just a prettier Egypt, and Moses was raised in the best country a faceless world ever built. He knows exactly what it is worth.",
    "id": "ch05-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Do not move us one step toward paradise if paradise is where You stop walking with us.",
    "id": "ch05-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He would rather have the desert with the Face than the destination without it.",
    "id": "ch05-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And God says yes. Moses gets the one thing he refused to live without. Then he asks for more.",
    "id": "ch05-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“I pray You, show me Your glory.”",
    "id": "ch05-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The word he uses is <em>kavod</em>, and at its root it means weight, heaviness, sheer mass. Heavy is what Moses called his own tongue at the bush, the same Hebrew root, the word he used to disqualify himself.",
    "id": "ch05-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now the man with the heavy mouth stands on the mountain asking to see the full weight of God.",
    "id": "ch05-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And God’s answer is a yes folded inside a no.",
    "id": "ch05-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Yes: “I Myself will make all My goodness pass before you, and I will proclaim the name of the LORD before you.”",
    "id": "ch05-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "No: “You cannot see My face, for no man can see Me and live.”",
    "id": "ch05-p85"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The no is not a door slammed; it is a physician explaining a limit. A mortal frame cannot stand under that much weight and survive it, any more than an eye can stare into the sun that lights everything else it sees.",
    "id": "ch05-p86"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So God engineers a mercy, and every instinct says the mercy should be distance.",
    "id": "ch05-p87"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Instead He does not send Moses one step farther away. He tucks him closer.",
    "id": "ch05-p88"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "and it will come about, while My glory is passing by, that I will put you in the cleft of the rock and cover you with My hand until I have passed by."
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 33:22",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The glory that would unmake a man is going to pass an arm’s length away, and God’s whole protection is this: a crack in the mountain with Moses pressed into it, and over the opening, the palm of the very One passing by.",
    "id": "ch05-p89"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God shields a man from God with God.",
    "id": "ch05-p90"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A psalm would say it in five words and never explain them.",
    "id": "ch05-p91"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "For the LORD God is a sun and a shield."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 84:11",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the gesture in that crack has a name, and the name runs all through Scripture. The Hebrew is <em>kanaph</em>. It means a wing. It also means the edge of a garment, the part of your coat you can throw over somebody.",
    "id": "ch05-p92"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is what the eagle does over the nest, in the song Moses writes at the very end of his life.",
    "id": "ch05-p93"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is what the two gold figures do over the lid of the ark: wings out, faces turned toward each other, over the one address in the world where God said He would meet a human being.",
    "id": "ch05-p94"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And it is what a widow asks for on a threshing floor at midnight, from a man with every legal right to refuse her. Spread your wing over me, Ruth says to Boaz, because you are a redeemer.",
    "id": "ch05-p95"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He had blessed her with those exact words weeks before, out in the barley. She had been face-down in the dirt when he said it, asking him why a stranger was worth noticing. <em>May you be rewarded by the God under whose wings you have come to take refuge.</em> She heard it. She kept it. And she handed it back to him as a request.",
    "id": "ch05-p96"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every wing in Scripture goes over something that cannot survive being uncovered. A nest in a desert. A lid with blood on it. A woman with no husband and no land.",
    "id": "ch05-p97"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And a man in a crack in a rock, with the glory coming.",
    "id": "ch05-p98"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses stands in the dark of that grip, close enough to be killed, kept alive by the nearness itself, and what he is given in there is not a sight but a sound: the LORD passing before him and preaching His own name.",
    "id": "ch05-p99"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“The LORD, the LORD God, compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, and abounding in lovingkindness and truth.”",
    "id": "ch05-p100"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He asked to see the weight; God gave him the character behind it, spoken aloud, a self-portrait made entirely of sound.",
    "id": "ch05-p101"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the first quality God names is slowness.",
    "id": "ch05-p102"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Slow to anger.",
    "id": "ch05-p103"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man who spent a lifetime ashamed of being slow of speech stands in the dark of God’s hand and hears Him call Himself slow too, in the place where slowness matters most.",
    "id": "ch05-p104"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Years later the nearness shows its work twice more, back to back.",
    "id": "ch05-p105"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The nation is wailing for meat, Moses is telling God he would rather die than carry this people another mile, and God answers by spreading the load onto seventy elders, who prophesy.",
    "id": "ch05-p106"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But two of them, Eldad and Medad, catch the Spirit off in the camp where the ceremony never reached, and Joshua, Moses’s aide, is scandalized: “Moses, my lord, restrain them.”",
    "id": "ch05-p107"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The one man on earth who has been given the access turns around and gives away the guard’s whole case.",
    "id": "ch05-p108"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "But Moses said to him, “Are you jealous for my sake? Would that all the LORD’s people were prophets, that the LORD would put His Spirit upon them!”"
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 11:29",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "All of them.",
    "id": "ch05-p109"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man who speaks with God as a friend wants the doors torn off their hinges.",
    "id": "ch05-p110"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God answers the wish with nothing, no yes and no refusal, and it hangs over the camp, unanswered, for the rest of the Old Testament.",
    "id": "ch05-p111"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The next complaint comes from closer in.",
    "id": "ch05-p112"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This time it is not a stranger at the tent door.",
    "id": "ch05-p113"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is his own blood.",
    "id": "ch05-p114"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Miriam and Aaron, his own sister and brother, begin to murmur: “Has the LORD spoken only through Moses? Has He not spoken through us as well?”",
    "id": "ch05-p115"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The LORD hears it, summons all three to the tent, and comes down in the pillar to answer them Himself.",
    "id": "ch05-p116"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And in defending Moses, God reaches past every majestic word available to Him, past glory and fire and mountain, and points to the very thing Moses once held up as evidence against himself.",
    "id": "ch05-p117"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "With him I speak mouth to mouth, even openly, and not in dark sayings, and he beholds the form of the LORD. Why then were you not afraid to speak against My servant, against Moses?"
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 12:8",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Mouth to mouth.",
    "id": "ch05-p118"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hebrew already owns face to face. It spent that phrase on this same man, at that tent, as a man speaks to his friend. And here God will not reach for it. He goes one organ closer.",
    "id": "ch05-p119"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Peh el peh</em>: my mouth to his mouth, said of no one else God ever spoke with.",
    "id": "ch05-p120"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And our own language keeps that phrase in exactly one place. Mouth to mouth is what we call it when a person cannot breathe on their own, and someone bends down and lends them breath.",
    "id": "ch05-p121"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the bush, an old man led with the failure of his mouth: heavy of speech, heavy of tongue, even since You have spoken to me.",
    "id": "ch05-p122"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Scripture never says the heaviness left him. No verse records a healing.",
    "id": "ch05-p123"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God did something farther up than healing. He moved in.",
    "id": "ch05-p124"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I AM, with your mouth.",
    "id": "ch05-p125"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And here, decades later, in front of the man’s own accusing family, is where that promise was always headed.",
    "id": "ch05-p126"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The organ Moses was ashamed of is now the site God names when He wants to say how close they are.",
    "id": "ch05-p127"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Heaven, asked to give the measure of the closest a human being has ever stood to God, does not mention the man’s strengths at all.",
    "id": "ch05-p128"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It names the mouth.",
    "id": "ch05-p129"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The same verse hands over one thing more. <em>He beholds the form of the LORD.</em>",
    "id": "ch05-p130"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The word is <em>temunah</em>, form, and we met it once already, at Sinai, in the nation’s protection clause: <em>you saw no form, only a voice.</em>",
    "id": "ch05-p131"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now the same word returns, but the distance has changed.",
    "id": "ch05-p132"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Withheld from a whole nation, the form was mercy in the distance: protection for a crowd that had begged for distance. Shown to one man, it is intimacy.",
    "id": "ch05-p133"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Same fire. Same God.",
    "id": "ch05-p134"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The difference is forty years of standing closer.",
    "id": "ch05-p135"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Those forty years were a sentence, and it was handed down at a border.",
    "id": "ch05-p136"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two years out of Egypt, the nation camps at the southern edge of the land, and Moses sends twelve men in to look at it. They come back forty days later carrying one cluster of grapes on a pole between two of them, and the report splits down the middle.",
    "id": "ch05-p137"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The land is everything God said it was. The people living in it are enormous.",
    "id": "ch05-p138"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Ten of the twelve count what they saw and reach the same answer. One of them quiets the crowd and says the other thing.",
    "id": "ch05-p139"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Let us go up at once and occupy it, for we are well able to overcome it."
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 13:30",
    "translation": "ESV",
    "id": "ch05-v10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Caleb is not arguing about the giants. He walked the same forty days and saw exactly what the other ten saw. His answer is not that they are small. What he and Joshua argue is something else: <em>their protection has been removed from them, and the LORD is with us.</em>",
    "id": "ch05-p140"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Hebrew under <em>protection</em> is <em>tzel</em> — shade, what a body has over it when the sun can kill.",
    "id": "ch05-p141"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They have nothing over them anymore, Caleb says. We do.",
    "id": "ch05-p142"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The crowd votes for the ten. They start talking about going back to Egypt. And the sentence comes down: this generation will not see the land. One year in the wilderness for every day the scouts were inside it.",
    "id": "ch05-p143"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Overnight, they change their minds.",
    "id": "ch05-p144"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Same crowd, same border, and now they are willing, up before dawn with their packs on: <em>we have indeed sinned, but we will go up to the place which the LORD has promised.</em>",
    "id": "ch05-p145"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Moses steps in front of them and says a sentence he has said before.",
    "id": "ch05-p146"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "“Do not go up, or you will be struck down before your enemies, for the LORD is not among you.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 14:42",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is quoting himself. On the mountain he had told God: if there is no face of Yours going, do not take us up from here. Three words carry that sentence in Hebrew — <em>ein</em>, there is not; <em>al</em>, do not; <em>alah</em>, go up — and all three are in his mouth again now, turned around and aimed at his own people. He had refused the land without the Face. He is watching them leave for it.",
    "id": "ch05-p147"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They went anyway.",
    "id": "ch05-p148"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "But they went up heedlessly to the ridge of the hill country; neither the ark of the covenant of the LORD nor Moses left the camp."
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 14:44",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The ark stayed in the camp.",
    "id": "ch05-p149"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They were beaten back down that hill as far as a place they named Hormah.",
    "id": "ch05-p150"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Yesterday they would not go in with Him. This morning they would go in without Him. They did not count Him either morning.",
    "id": "ch05-p151"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The life runs out where it began, on a mountaintop, alone with God.",
    "id": "ch05-p152"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses climbs Nebo at a hundred and twenty, and the text pauses to note something it did not have to keep: <em>his eye was not dim.</em> He could still see, clear to the horizon, all the way to the end.",
    "id": "ch05-p153"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "An old father in a tent, whose eyes had gone too dim to check the face in front of him. Every deception in that family walked in through the gap.",
    "id": "ch05-p154"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses is spared that particular old age. The man God knew face to face never lost the eyes to look back with.",
    "id": "ch05-p155"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He stands on the ridge and sees the whole promised land laid out under the light, the country he will not enter, and he dies there, the text says, according to the word of the LORD.",
    "id": "ch05-p156"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>By the mouth of the LORD.</em>",
    "id": "ch05-p157"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The old rabbis read that phrase and said what it sounds like: “he died by a kiss.”",
    "id": "ch05-p158"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Scripture does not confirm them. It only tells us who handled the burial.",
    "id": "ch05-p159"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God buried him in a valley in Moab, in a grave no human being has ever found. That funeral had one mourner, and it was God.",
    "id": "ch05-p160"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the last thing that ever happened to Moses was the hand of God closing the earth over him, the same hand that once closed over a cleft in the rock while the glory went by.",
    "id": "ch05-p161"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the Torah ends. Five books, creation to covenant to law, the foundation under everything else in the Bible, and it has one sentence left to spend on the life at its center. It does not spend it on the sea, or the plagues, or the tablets. Every achievement of that life goes unlisted. The sentence the Torah chooses to end on is about acquaintance.",
    "id": "ch05-p162"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Since that time no prophet has risen in Israel like Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face,"
    ],
    "ref": "Deuteronomy 34:10",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Whom the LORD knew.",
    "id": "ch05-p163"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The last word on the greatest man in the Torah is somebody else’s name.",
    "id": "ch05-p164"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two sentences bracket this life. The whole distance he traveled lives between them.",
    "id": "ch05-p165"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It began:",
    "id": "ch05-p166"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Moses hid his face, for he was afraid to look at God."
    ],
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It ends:",
    "id": "ch05-p167"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "whom the LORD knew face to face."
    ],
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch05-v15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A man began at one sentence and died at the other.",
    "id": "ch05-p168"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the engine that moved him was never his eloquence, because he never had any.",
    "id": "ch05-p169"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was the God who took His own name, the name that means presence, and kept saying it over the broken place: I AM. With your mouth.",
    "id": "ch05-p170"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the Torah is still holding one scene back, from the middle of the story, the morning after the cleft of the rock.",
    "id": "ch05-p171"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses came down the mountain that day with the new tablets under his arm and forty days of glory behind him, and the camp caught sight of him on the slope and did not run out to meet him.",
    "id": "ch05-p172"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It stopped.",
    "id": "ch05-p173"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Aaron went still. Mothers pulled their children in close.",
    "id": "ch05-p174"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every face in Israel turned toward him, saw something, and turned away again. Not one person would come near enough to tell him why.",
    "id": "ch05-p175"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Forty days beside the glory had done something to his face, and he was the only man in Israel who did not know it.",
    "id": "ch05-p176"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "num": 6,
  "id": "ch06",
  "title": "Borrowed Light",
  "hook": "His face is burning, the whole camp has backed away from him, and nobody will come tell him why. The lamp is the last to know it is lit.",
  "glossary": [
   {
    "term": "masveh",
    "gloss": "Hebrew has to mint a brand-new word for that piece of cloth: masveh, a word that appears nowhere else in the Bible, before or after."
   },
   {
    "term": "qaran",
    "gloss": "The Hebrew verb for what Moses’s face did is qaran — to shoot out rays, to horn out light the way the sun horns over a ridgeline."
   },
   {
    "term": "melammu",
    "gloss": "In Babylon they called it melammu: the terrifying radiance the gods wore like armor and kings claimed at coronation — conferred in a ceremony, stripped from you when you fell, transferred to your rival."
   },
   {
    "term": "panim",
    "gloss": "Countenance is a Sunday word, and underneath it is panim."
   }
  ],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch06-shine"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody will come tell him.",
    "id": "ch06-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses is standing at the bottom of the mountain of God with a tablet of stone under each arm, forty days of fasting behind him, and the whole camp of Israel in front of him, close enough to see his face and too afraid to come near it. He starts toward them and they move backward, the way a crowd moves back from a fire.",
    "id": "ch06-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He looks at his brother. And Aaron, the high priest of Israel, the man who has done all the talking for forty years, cannot produce one word.",
    "id": "ch06-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "It came about when Moses was coming down from Mount Sinai (and the two tablets of the testimony were in Moses’ hand as he was coming down from the mountain), that Moses did not know that the skin of his face shone because of his speaking with Him.",
     "So when Aaron and all the sons of Israel saw Moses, behold, the skin of his face shone, and they were afraid to come near him."
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 34:29–30",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses did not know.",
    "id": "ch06-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The detail is startlingly physical. The verse does not say a light surrounded him, or that an aura hung over him. It says the skin of his face shone.",
    "id": "ch06-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His actual flesh, the same sunburned eighty-year-old face he has washed his whole life, is doing something flesh does not do.",
    "id": "ch06-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And he cannot feel it.",
    "id": "ch06-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is no heat in it, no weight. Everyone in the desert can see it; the one man who cannot is the man wearing it.",
    "id": "ch06-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The lamp is the last to know it is lit.",
    "id": "ch06-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The truest mark that a man had been with God was the one thing about him he could not fake, could not work up, could not even check in a mirror.",
    "id": "ch06-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The standoff has a bitter architecture, and Israel drew the plans themselves. At this same mountain they had begged for a middleman. “Let a man stand between us and the fire. Let him go up for us. We will listen from a safe distance.” God said yes.",
    "id": "ch06-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And now the middleman comes down from a meeting with the fire still on his face, and the arrangement collapses into comedy: the nation needs a buffer from the buffer. Distance, once you order it, keeps ordering more of itself. They asked to be shielded from the face of God, and now they cannot even manage the face of the man who saw it.",
    "id": "ch06-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then Moses opens his mouth.",
    "id": "ch06-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The text says <em>Moses called to them, and Aaron and the rulers of the congregation returned</em>.",
    "id": "ch06-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The face was unbearable, but the voice was still Moses, the same familiar, heavy-tongued voice they had followed out of Egypt, and it was the voice that brought them back.",
    "id": "ch06-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At Sinai, the nation fled the voice of God and asked for a man.",
    "id": "ch06-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Today, they flee the face of the man, and his voice retrieves them.",
    "id": "ch06-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So Moses does for them what God has been doing for them all along: he rations himself.",
    "id": "ch06-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When he finishes speaking the words of the LORD, he puts a veil over his face, and Israel gets its buffer.",
    "id": "ch06-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hebrew has to mint a brand-new word for that piece of cloth: <em>masveh</em>, a word that appears nowhere else in the Bible, before or after.",
    "id": "ch06-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the text is careful to show us when the veil came off.",
    "id": "ch06-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Whenever Moses went in before the LORD to speak with Him, he took the veil off.</em>",
    "id": "ch06-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Veiled toward the people. Bare toward the glory.",
    "id": "ch06-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The covering was never between Moses and God, not once, not for a minute, not by a thread. It hung in one direction only: between the shine and the crowd that could not bear it.",
    "id": "ch06-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every time he stepped into the presence, the cloth came down and the light landed on an uncovered face. Every time he walked back out to the people, the cloth went up.",
    "id": "ch06-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That square of fabric is Israel’s choice at the mountain, sewn and worn in public: God still offering everything, His man still receiving it bare-faced, and a nation, by its own request, taking its glory filtered.",
    "id": "ch06-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The shine even has a comic afterlife. The Hebrew verb for what Moses’s face did is <em>qaran</em> — to shoot out rays, to horn out light the way the sun horns over a ridgeline. Centuries later a translator rendered it a shade too literally, and that is why Michelangelo’s Moses sits in Rome to this day with two small horns on his head.",
    "id": "ch06-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the ancient world would not have laughed at the shining itself. The ancient world had a word for it. In Babylon they called it <em>melammu</em>: the terrifying radiance the gods wore like armor and kings claimed at coronation — conferred in a ceremony, stripped from you when you fell, transferred to your rival. A crown made of light. Like every crown, it marked rank.",
    "id": "ch06-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses’s radiance is something else entirely. He does not put it on. He does not possess it.",
    "id": "ch06-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody conferred anything on him in any ceremony. He cannot take it off; he has to throw cloth over it. He could not hand it to Joshua if he tried.",
    "id": "ch06-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And he does not know he has it.",
    "id": "ch06-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A Babylonian king wore his radiance the way a man wears a drawn sword. Moses wears his the way you carry the warmth of the sun on his skin after a long day outside: absorbed without thinking about it, a side effect of where he has been standing.",
    "id": "ch06-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The nations’ gods hoarded their light and rented it out to power. Israel’s God lets His glory spill over.",
    "id": "ch06-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It gets on whatever stays close.",
    "id": "ch06-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then He puts it in writing. What happened to one face simply by being near Him, He turns into standing law for every face in the nation.",
    "id": "ch06-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The instrument is a blessing.",
    "id": "ch06-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You have been carrying its opening words since the first minutes of this book, when they lay in the dark under Jerusalem, against the bones of the dead, on two small rolls of silver.",
    "id": "ch06-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The LORD bless you and keep you. The LORD make His face shine on you."
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 6:24–25",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The oldest known biblical text, buried with the dead.",
    "id": "ch06-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What the tomb could not tell you is where those words came from.",
    "id": "ch06-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is here.",
    "id": "ch06-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Though the first priest in the Bible was not an Israelite, and he blessed somebody centuries before Aaron was born.",
    "id": "ch06-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Long before the wilderness — before there was a nation, or a law, or a priesthood — a man walks out of nowhere to meet Abram. Genesis gives him three verses and never says where he came from. He brings out bread and wine. And he blesses.",
    "id": "ch06-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is a priest and a king, and the city he is king of is Salem — the city that will one day be called Jerusalem.",
    "id": "ch06-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Salem. <em>Shalom.</em> The letter to the Hebrews reads his title twice over: king of righteousness, and then king of peace.",
    "id": "ch06-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first priest in the Bible was the king of peace, and the blessing God is about to dictate in the wilderness ends on his word. It begins on his too. Both ends of that blessing belong to a man who was not even an Israelite.",
    "id": "ch06-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And in those three verses, there is no altar.",
    "id": "ch06-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every priest who comes after him will be defined by one. The fire, the knife, the blood, the smoke, the curtain standing behind him. This one has none of it. He walks out of nowhere with food in his hands, says a blessing, and goes.",
    "id": "ch06-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hebrews builds a whole argument out of what Genesis leaves out about him: no father, no mother, no genealogy, no beginning of days. It reads the silences on purpose. And the loudest one is the altar.",
    "id": "ch06-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He was not a barrier. He was a meal.",
    "id": "ch06-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the wilderness, in the aftermath of the shine, God dictates the words Himself and hands them to Aaron and his sons with orders attached: what soaked into one man’s face at the summit is to be prayed down onto every face in the camp, forever.",
    "id": "ch06-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He builds it to open as it goes. In Hebrew the first line is three words. The second is five. The third is seven. Every line is longer than the one before it, so the blessing widens in the mouth of the man saying it.",
    "id": "ch06-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And every word of it is singular.",
    "id": "ch06-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The priest stands in front of a nation and says <em>you</em>, and the <em>you</em> he says is the one Hebrew keeps for one person. Not all of you. You. The blessing widens as it goes and it never once turns plural.",
    "id": "ch06-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Thousands of people standing in that camp, and the grammar is aimed at one face at a time.",
    "id": "ch06-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The middle line, the five-word one, is the face.",
    "id": "ch06-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The blessing is fifteen words in Hebrew. Three, then five, then seven, opening as it goes — which means it has a middle. One word with seven in front of it and seven behind. And on either side of that one word, a face: His face shining, two words before it; His face lifting, three words after.",
    "id": "ch06-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The word in the middle is <em>vichunecha</em>. And He will be gracious to you. Under it is <em>chen</em>, grace, and in Israel that was also a girl’s name.",
    "id": "ch06-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The LORD lift up His countenance on you,",
     "And give you peace."
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 6:26",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Countenance is a Sunday word, and underneath it is <em>panim</em>. The LORD lift up His face toward you. And in Israel, that phrase had a rap sheet. Lifting someone’s face is courtroom language, and in the law of Moses it is the crooked kind: it is what a bought judge does. “You shall not be partial,” God orders Israel’s courts — and the Hebrew beneath partial says, <em>you shall not lift up faces</em>. Do not let the verdict tilt because you like the man standing in front of you.",
    "id": "ch06-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And God holds His own bench to His own rule, in the same courtroom Hebrew: <em>the great, the mighty, and the awesome God, who does not lift up faces and does not take a bribe</em>.",
    "id": "ch06-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You cannot charm Him, pay Him, or network your way past Him. His courtroom is incorruptible, which is wonderful news, right up until you remember that you are the defendant.",
    "id": "ch06-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then God writes the priestly blessing and commands the priests of that same incorruptible courtroom to stand in front of the entire nation, hands raised, and say to every person present: the LORD lift up His face toward you.",
    "id": "ch06-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The one thing God’s own law forbids a judge to do, God now orders spoken over His people as a blessing.",
    "id": "ch06-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is a change of rooms.",
    "id": "ch06-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In court, His face does not turn for the powerful or away from the weak.",
    "id": "ch06-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the blessing is spoken over family.",
    "id": "ch06-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And inside the family, the Judge stands up, takes off the robe, and turns His face toward everyone at once.",
    "id": "ch06-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And one man saw it coming. A frightened schemer at a river ford, face-down in the dark before the brother he had wronged, begging for exactly this: <em>perhaps he will lift up my face</em>. It was a long shot when Jacob prayed it.",
    "id": "ch06-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now the priests of Israel are under standing orders to promise Jacob’s long shot to every face in the nation, every time they bless.",
    "id": "ch06-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then the blessing gives us its own interpretation.",
    "id": "ch06-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "“So shall they put my name upon the people of Israel, and I will bless them.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 6:27",
    "translation": "ESV",
    "id": "ch06-v4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The blessing carries the name.",
    "id": "ch06-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Speak these words over the people, God says, and My name is placed on them: pressed in like a seal on a document, given like a family name to a child.",
    "id": "ch06-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And you already know, from a burning bush, what that name is.",
    "id": "ch06-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I AM.",
    "id": "ch06-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Presence itself. The name that is a promise to be with.",
    "id": "ch06-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "To carry it is to walk around marked: His, addressed as His, spoken for.",
    "id": "ch06-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "For generation after generation, Israel has heard this blessing, and the name has settled on them layer by layer, the way light settles on a face.",
    "id": "ch06-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God has never issued a recall.",
    "id": "ch06-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the synagogue, to this day, when the priests rise to speak this blessing, they lift their hands, palms forward, fingers split, and the congregation looks down.",
    "id": "ch06-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You do not stare at the priests during the blessing. That is the ancient custom.",
    "id": "ch06-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The tradition has an answer: the Presence rests on the priests’ hands.",
    "id": "ch06-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the rabbis, hunting for where Scripture whispers such a thing, reached into the Song of Songs, into the love poem, and pulled out this verse.",
    "id": "ch06-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "My beloved is like a gazelle or a young stag. Behold, he is standing behind our wall, he is looking through the windows, he is peering through the lattice."
    ],
    "ref": "Song of Songs 2:9",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is behind the wall.",
    "id": "ch06-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the window.",
    "id": "ch06-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Pressing His face to the lattice for a glimpse of the one He loves.",
    "id": "ch06-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the lattice, said the rabbis, is the priests’ fingers.",
    "id": "ch06-p85"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "While Israel stands with bowed heads, safely buffered at last, given the distance they asked for, God is on the other side of the mediation, His eye to the gaps, peering between the knuckles of the priests at the faces of His people.",
    "id": "ch06-p86"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Israel asked for a middleman, and God consented.",
    "id": "ch06-p87"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But no one at Sinai read the fine print: the middleman would have spaces between his fingers, and the God of Israel would spend the next fifteen hundred years looking through them.",
    "id": "ch06-p88"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Mediation was supposed to be distance.",
    "id": "ch06-p89"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He turned it into a lattice.",
    "id": "ch06-p90"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They could have the buffer.",
    "id": "ch06-p91"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They could not make Him stop looking.",
    "id": "ch06-p92"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the shine He kept looking for was never reserved for clean faces.",
    "id": "ch06-p93"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It found David in his worst hour.",
    "id": "ch06-p94"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He has already been anointed king. He is already the most famous soldier in Israel. And the running has grown so desperate that he crosses enemy lines into Gath, Goliath’s own hometown, hoping to disappear into a foreign crowd.",
    "id": "ch06-p95"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is recognized before the day is out.",
    "id": "ch06-p96"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Is this not David?” the servants ask their king. “The one they sing about, the one the dances say has slain his ten thousands?”",
    "id": "ch06-p97"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The song that made him famous has followed him behind enemy lines, and it is about to get him killed.",
    "id": "ch06-p98"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So the anointed of Israel does something no hero in any story wants to do.",
    "id": "ch06-p99"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He acts insane.",
    "id": "ch06-p100"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He scrabbles at the doors of the gate. He lets his spit run down into his beard, in public, in front of pagan royalty, until the king of Gath waves him off with a sneer:",
    "id": "ch06-p101"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Do I lack madmen, that you bring me this one?”",
    "id": "ch06-p102"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "1 Samuel 21:10–15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Ten verses earlier, in that same chapter, a priest had handed him the only bread in the house: the loaves off the golden table that sat all week in front of the face of God.",
    "id": "ch06-p103"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man who will write nearly half the book of Psalms shuffles out of that court with his beard wet.",
    "id": "ch06-p104"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is hard to imagine a face lower.",
    "id": "ch06-p105"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Years later, that man writes a psalm, and the heading names this exact episode: <em>when he feigned madness and was driven away</em>.",
    "id": "ch06-p106"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Of everything David could have remembered while he wrote, he chose the worst thing his face ever did and set it beside this line:",
    "id": "ch06-p107"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "They looked to Him and were radiant,",
     "And their faces will never be ashamed."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 34:5",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Radiant, from the pen of the man whose face once ran with spit in Gath.",
    "id": "ch06-p108"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Never ashamed, from the man who lived his deepest shame in public.",
    "id": "ch06-p109"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is handing Moses’s discovery to anyone willing to turn his face toward God:",
    "id": "ch06-p110"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "the shine is not rank or reward. It is exposure. And no face has ever fallen too far to stand in it.",
    "id": "ch06-p111"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His signature at the top of the psalm is the proof.",
    "id": "ch06-p112"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And it had been settled about him long before, on an afternoon when nobody in the room could see it happening.",
    "id": "ch06-p113"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A prophet comes to Bethlehem to anoint a king out of one family, and the father lines his sons up. The oldest walks in first, tall and striking, and the prophet takes one look at him and is certain.",
    "id": "ch06-p114"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He has made this mistake before. The last king he anointed was the best-looking man in Israel and stood a head above everybody in it, and that is the king who lost a kingdom watching the crowd. Here he is again, reading a face and calling it a verdict.",
    "id": "ch06-p115"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God stops him mid-thought.",
    "id": "ch06-p116"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Do not look at his appearance or at the height of his stature, because I have rejected him; for God sees not as man sees, for man looks at the outward appearance, but the LORD looks at the heart."
    ],
    "ref": "1 Samuel 16:7",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Greek translation the synagogues had been reading for generations says it harder than ours does. <em>Man looks at the face. God looks at the heart.</em>",
    "id": "ch06-p117"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seven sons pass by that afternoon and God says no to all seven. The one He came for is not in the house. He is out with the animals, the youngest, not worth calling in from the field for.",
    "id": "ch06-p118"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They have to send for him.",
    "id": "ch06-p119"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The whole thing was decided about a face that was not in the room.",
    "id": "ch06-p120"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Which is why what happened to that face in Gath could never touch it.",
    "id": "ch06-p121"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And centuries later the shine lands on a man nobody had heard of.",
    "id": "ch06-p122"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The church had an argument about food distribution, so they appointed seven men to wait tables, and one of them was Stephen. Within a few chapters he is dragged in front of the council on a charge that is going to kill him, and Luke stops the scene to record what the men sitting in judgment on him could see.",
    "id": "ch06-p123"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "And fixing their gaze on him, all who were sitting in the Council saw his face like the face of an angel."
    ],
    "ref": "Acts 6:15",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses came down a mountain with it. Stephen got it waiting tables, and wore it into the room where they voted to stone him.",
    "id": "ch06-p124"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody conferred it. He had been standing near the fire, and it showed up where everyone could read it but him.",
    "id": "ch06-p125"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That council had seen it once already, and had no word for it then either. Two chapters earlier they had hauled in two fishermen over a healing, men with no schooling and no rank, and what the room could not get past was not the argument.",
    "id": "ch06-p126"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "they were amazed, and began to recognize them as having been with Jesus."
    ],
    "ref": "Acts 4:13",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They could not say what they were looking at. They could only say who put it there.",
    "id": "ch06-p127"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And three verses below that line, in David’s psalm, sits the invitation.",
    "id": "ch06-p128"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "O taste and see that the LORD is good."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 34:8",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He could have written look and see. He wrote taste.",
    "id": "ch06-p129"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And a whole psalm, Psalm 67, takes the priests’ words out of the priests’ mouths and hands them to everybody.",
    "id": "ch06-p130"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The congregation prays the blessing over itself, all together:",
    "id": "ch06-p131"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "God be gracious to us and bless us,",
     "And cause His face to shine upon us."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 67:1",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then, mid-prayer, the reason surfaces, and it is not the reason anyone asking for blessing expects:",
    "id": "ch06-p132"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "That Your way may be known on the earth,",
     "Your salvation among all nations."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 67:2",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch06-v12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The light that lands on your face was never traveling to you.",
    "id": "ch06-p133"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was traveling through you.",
    "id": "ch06-p134"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses shone and a nation saw.",
    "id": "ch06-p135"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Israel shines and the nations see.",
    "id": "ch06-p136"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was lit for the ones still out in the dark, steering by whatever light they can find.",
    "id": "ch06-p137"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This would be the place to stop, in full sunshine: a shining man, a blessing on standing orders, a name settling on a people, a Lover at the lattice.",
    "id": "ch06-p138"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But there is still the veil.",
    "id": "ch06-p139"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses kept it on among the people and took it off before God, and in between, in the ordinary hours, that square of cloth hung over the most famous face in Israel and kept its counsel.",
    "id": "ch06-p140"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Everyone assumed they knew what it was hiding.",
    "id": "ch06-p141"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Brightness.",
    "id": "ch06-p142"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Too much glory for comfort.",
    "id": "ch06-p143"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And at first, that was true.",
    "id": "ch06-p144"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But glory on a human face is reflected glory, and reflected glory obeys the oldest rule there is: it lasts only as long as the face remains turned toward its source.",
    "id": "ch06-p145"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Under the cloth, between one meeting with God and the next, something was happening to the shine that no one in Israel ever saw, and the veil made sure no one ever would.",
    "id": "ch06-p146"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The shine had a secret.",
    "id": "ch06-p147"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was fading.",
    "id": "ch06-p148"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "num": 7,
  "id": "ch07",
  "title": "The Glory Backs Out",
  "hook": "The light leaves the temple one slow, deliberate step at a time, and the building goes on gleaming as if nothing has happened.",
  "glossary": [
   {
    "term": "haster astir",
    "gloss": "In Hebrew the verb comes in twice, back to back — haster astir — Hebrew’s way of writing a word in bold."
   },
   {
    "term": "hester panim",
    "gloss": "Hester panim, the hiding of the face."
   },
   {
    "term": "qol demamah daqqah",
    "gloss": "And after the fire comes something the Hebrew can barely say and English cannot: qol demamah daqqah — a voice of thin silence."
   }
  ],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch07-gate"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The saddest thing God ever showed a human being, He showed to a man sitting nine hundred miles away from where it happened.",
    "id": "ch07-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man is a priest named Ezekiel, and he has never once served in the temple. He was born for it. Raised toward it. Priests entered service at thirty. The opening line of his book says the heavens opened in the thirtieth year.",
    "id": "ch07-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This was the year.",
    "id": "ch07-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The year his hands were finally supposed to touch the altar, he is in Babylon instead, on the banks of an irrigation canal, deported with the first wave of exiles: a priest with no altar, in a land with no temple, nine hundred miles from the only room on earth his ordination was for.",
    "id": "ch07-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And around every cooking fire in the settlement, the exiles run the same question in circles: how could God let them take Jerusalem? Because the temple is still standing back home. That is the thing everyone keeps coming back to. The city holds. The services run.",
    "id": "ch07-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Surely, they tell each other, the worst is over.",
    "id": "ch07-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They are wrong.",
    "id": "ch07-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A shine can sit on a face while it dies under the veil.",
    "id": "ch07-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A temple can stand there gleaming while the same secret runs its course inside.",
    "id": "ch07-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Borrowed light was fading in Jerusalem, and nobody in Jerusalem was told.",
    "id": "ch07-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then a hand takes the priest by the hair.",
    "id": "ch07-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hand of the LORD, the text says, seizes Ezekiel by a lock of his head and carries him west in visions, all nine hundred miles, and sets him down inside the temple courts.",
    "id": "ch07-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Home at last, in the one way he would never have chosen.",
    "id": "ch07-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He has spent his entire life trying to get inside this place.",
    "id": "ch07-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now God brings him in to show him what is happening to it.",
    "id": "ch07-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then God walks him through the house, room by room: the house of God as God sees it.",
    "id": "ch07-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the entrance, an idol, planted at the gate like a rival’s flag.",
    "id": "ch07-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In a dark chamber, seventy elders of Israel swing censers to carved images, telling each other the sentence that explains everything:",
    "id": "ch07-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“The LORD does not see us; the LORD has forsaken the land.”",
    "id": "ch07-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They turned their backs first, and then declared Him blind.",
    "id": "ch07-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And in the inner court, between the porch and the altar, at the very hinge of holiness, stand twenty-five men in the one spot every Israelite dreamed of reaching.",
    "id": "ch07-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Their backs are to the temple of the LORD.",
    "id": "ch07-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Their faces are toward the east.",
    "id": "ch07-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They bow to the sun.",
    "id": "ch07-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Inside the house of the face.",
    "id": "ch07-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jeremiah was working the same years in the same city, and he wrote down God’s sentence for it. They have turned to Me the back and not the face.",
    "id": "ch07-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is a small thing buried in that line. The Hebrew word for face, <em>panim</em>, comes from the verb <em>panah</em>, to turn. A face, in Hebrew, is the turned-toward part of a person.",
    "id": "ch07-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So the charge is not about manners. Those men in the inner court have taken the one part of themselves that was named for turning toward Him, and aimed it somewhere else.",
    "id": "ch07-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Ezekiel watches it the way a man watches a slow-motion catastrophe he cannot reach.",
    "id": "ch07-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The glory of the God of Israel begins to leave.",
    "id": "ch07-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Ezekiel had described the creatures under that throne in his first vision, and he had counted their faces. Four apiece. The glory of God rides out of its own house on a throne carried by faces.",
    "id": "ch07-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the leaving is unlike any other departure in Scripture.",
    "id": "ch07-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The glory lifts from its place over the cherubim and moves to the threshold of the house.",
    "id": "ch07-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And stops.",
    "id": "ch07-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then from the threshold to the east gate of the courtyard.",
    "id": "ch07-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And stops.",
    "id": "ch07-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The glory of the LORD went up from the midst of the city and stood over the mountain which is east of the city."
    ],
    "ref": "Ezekiel 11:23",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The mountain east of the city.",
    "id": "ch07-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Mount of Olives.",
    "id": "ch07-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And there it stands.",
    "id": "ch07-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But glory does not need a road.",
    "id": "ch07-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It reached Ezekiel in Babylon without distance slowing it down. It can be anywhere it wills, instantly.",
    "id": "ch07-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Which means a departure in stages is not an evacuation.",
    "id": "ch07-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is reluctance.",
    "id": "ch07-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The rabbis counted the stops and found ten of them, and said out loud what the stops were for: at every one the Presence lingered, waiting, hoping the city below would look up and call after it.",
    "id": "ch07-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And they heard in that slow withdrawal the echo of a verse:",
    "id": "ch07-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "I will go away and return to My place,",
     "Until they seek My face."
    ],
    "ref": "Hosea 5:15",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the glory withdraws still facing the people.",
    "id": "ch07-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It does not turn and go.",
    "id": "ch07-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God backs out of His own house, eyes on His family the whole way.",
    "id": "ch07-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "No one calls.",
    "id": "ch07-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "No one in Jerusalem even notices. The silence is the hard part. There is no earthquake the morning the glory leaves. No darkness at noon. The priests keep the schedule, the smoke goes up on time, the choirs rehearse.",
    "id": "ch07-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The only human being on earth who sees the light leave the building is a deportee beside a canal in Babylon. And for years afterward the temple ran the way a body runs after the soul has gone: warm, familiar, still performing the motions of life.",
    "id": "ch07-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So when the Babylonians finally come and put it to the torch, they believe, and history remembers, that they have destroyed the temple of the God of Israel. They have burned an empty house. What made it a temple had left on its own, eastward, years earlier, facing backward the whole way out.",
    "id": "ch07-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Israel had a name for this, and the name was issued in advance. Forty years before the nation ever entered the land, in the last week of Moses’s life, God told the man who knew Him face to face exactly how the story would go wrong.",
    "id": "ch07-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "“Then My anger will be kindled against them in that day, and I will forsake them and hide My face from them… But I will surely hide My face in that day because of all the evil which they will do, for they will turn to other gods.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Deuteronomy 31:17–18",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I will surely hide. In Hebrew the verb comes in twice, back to back — <em>haster astir</em> — Hebrew’s way of writing a word in bold.",
    "id": "ch07-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is Cain’s verb. <em>From Your face I will be hidden</em>: the small word a marked man spoke east of Eden, the first human sentence in Scripture about what it means to live outside the face of God. That small word grew into a doctrine with a name. <em>Hester panim</em>, the hiding of the face. Israel’s own name for its darkest weather.",
    "id": "ch07-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It does not mean the absence of God. It means the averted gaze of God, and the second is heavier. Absence is nobody’s decision. An averted gaze is someone’s.",
    "id": "ch07-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the next thing God says, on that same afternoon, is not another threat.",
    "id": "ch07-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He tells Moses to write a song. Teach it to them, He says. Put it in their mouths, so it will never be forgotten from the lips of their children. And Moses wrote it that same day.",
    "id": "ch07-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That song is the one about the eagle.",
    "id": "ch07-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He announced the dark and handed them the words for it in the same breath.",
    "id": "ch07-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And once, one prophet took that whole weather system and made it a name.",
    "id": "ch07-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Truly, You are a God who hides Himself,",
     "O God of Israel, Savior!"
    ],
    "ref": "Isaiah 45:15",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>El mistater.</em> The hiding God. It is the only place in all of Scripture where anyone calls Him that, and the man who said it did not stop to soften it.",
    "id": "ch07-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He put Savior in the same breath, with nothing between them but a comma.",
    "id": "ch07-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the rabbis, saying that doubled verb over and over, heard something hiding inside it. <em>Haster astir</em>. The words run together in the mouth: <em>haster astir</em>… Esther. It is a sound, not an etymology. But the Talmud asks, “where is Esther in the Torah?” and answers with this verse: the promise that God would hide His face, and the woman through whom He would hide Himself while saving His people.",
    "id": "ch07-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Because the book of Esther is the strangest scroll in the Bible: an entire book of Scripture in which God is never once named. He has no lines. He makes no appearance. And yet count what the book calls coincidence. The queen happens to be deposed at the one banquet that opens her throne to an orphan girl. The king happens to lie sleepless on precisely the right night, and happens to call for precisely the archive that names her cousin. The gallows is finished just in time for the man who built it. In the book named for the hidden face, God never speaks, never appears, and never misses. Hidden is not gone. A medieval rabbi, Chizkuni, pressed one step further and dared to write down why a father would turn his face away while his child is being punished: because he cannot bear to watch.",
    "id": "ch07-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You have read both of these books. You have never once read them the way the people inside them had to live them.",
    "id": "ch07-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man in the ashes was never told about the wager. You were — on the first page, before a single animal died. He argued all the way to the last line of his book with the one thing that would have explained everything sitting in a chapter he could not reach. Nobody ever told him. He died without it.",
    "id": "ch07-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In this one, nobody is told anything. No wager. No voice. Not even the name. And you can see Him on every page anyway.",
    "id": "ch07-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One book hands you the answer and keeps it from the man in the story. The other keeps it from everybody, and lets you watch it work regardless.",
    "id": "ch07-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You are not in the first book. You are in the second one.",
    "id": "ch07-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Which means nobody is going to explain it to you, and nothing that has happened to you is loose.",
    "id": "ch07-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And you have been asking Him for the first page of Job.",
    "id": "ch07-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hidden face has a cost at the scale of temples, and it has a cost at the scale of a kitchen table. Scripture keeps one story for the second kind, and it is a king’s.",
    "id": "ch07-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David’s third son, Absalom, killed his brother, and fled the country. Three years pass; the text says the king’s heart longed for his son; and finally, through a general’s scheming, David allows the boy home to Jerusalem. The exile is over. The son is home. And then the pardon comes with a rider.",
    "id": "ch07-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "However the king said, “Let him turn to his own house, and let him not see my face.” So Absalom turned to his own house and did not see the king’s face."
    ],
    "ref": "2 Samuel 14:24",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two years. Two years of being home without being received.",
    "id": "ch07-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Absalom lives in his father’s city, under his father’s rule, with his father’s provision, and in all that time his father will not look at him once.",
    "id": "ch07-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is home and in exile at the same time, which is a thing only a withheld face can accomplish.",
    "id": "ch07-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A pardon you cannot see in someone’s eyes does not feel like a pardon.",
    "id": "ch07-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It feels like a sentence with better food.",
    "id": "ch07-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Absalom finally breaks.",
    "id": "ch07-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He sets a general’s barley field on fire, not out of spite, but because a burning field forces a conversation. And the message he sends ahead of him is as raw as anything a son ever said to a father:",
    "id": "ch07-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“If there is iniquity in me, let the king put me to death.”",
    "id": "ch07-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In other words: kill me or look at me.",
    "id": "ch07-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Anything but this.",
    "id": "ch07-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two chapters earlier, the same king did the opposite for a man with no claim on him at all.",
    "id": "ch07-p85"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Not a son. The grandson of the man whose throne David was sitting on. And a cripple.",
    "id": "ch07-p86"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David sent for him, and Scripture closes his story in one breath: he ate at the king’s table every day of his life, and he was lame in both his feet.",
    "id": "ch07-p87"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One of them was home and could not see his father’s face. The other could not walk, and never missed a meal in front of the king’s.",
    "id": "ch07-p88"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So David summons him at last.",
    "id": "ch07-p89"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Absalom comes in and bows his face to the ground, and his father kisses him.",
    "id": "ch07-p90"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The text records no words.",
    "id": "ch07-p91"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The face has finally turned toward him.",
    "id": "ch07-p92"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Within a few chapters the son who could not get his father’s face is taking his father’s kingdom instead, and dying in the attempt, and David is climbing the stairs over the city gate, coming apart in front of his own soldiers:",
    "id": "ch07-p93"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“O my son Absalom, my son, my son Absalom.",
    "id": "ch07-p94"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Would I had died instead of you.”",
    "id": "ch07-p95"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The most famous sentence anyone ever wrote about a table was written by this man.",
    "id": "ch07-p96"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "You prepare a table before me in the presence of my enemies;",
     "You have anointed my head with oil; My cup overflows."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 23:5",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David knew exactly what it was to be given a seat. He had been fed in front of men who wanted him dead. And for two years he ran a house where his own son had a roof, an allowance, and no chair.",
    "id": "ch07-p97"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Which is not the only thing that was happening at that table.",
    "id": "ch07-p98"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Years before Absalom ever came home, David had gone looking for anyone left alive from the house of Saul — the house that had hunted him through the wilderness for a decade — and found one survivor. A grandson of the man who threw spears at him. The boy had been dropped in his nurse’s arms the day the news came from the battlefield, and he had been lame in both feet ever since.",
    "id": "ch07-p99"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David sent for him. And what he handed him was not a pension.",
    "id": "ch07-p100"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Mephibosheth ate at David’s table as one of the king’s sons."
    ],
    "ref": "2 Samuel 9:11",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Four times in one short chapter the text says it, the way a man repeats a thing he cannot get over. At my table. At my table regularly. As one of the king’s sons. And then the chapter closes on the detail it had been saving: <em>Now he was lame in both feet.</em>",
    "id": "ch07-p101"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So there were two men in that house.",
    "id": "ch07-p102"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The enemy’s grandson, who could not walk, ate at the king’s table as a son.",
    "id": "ch07-p103"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The king’s own son, who could walk anywhere he liked, lived down the street for two years and never saw his father’s face.",
    "id": "ch07-p104"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A chair in that house was never about deserving it. It was about who the king had decided to look at.",
    "id": "ch07-p105"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Pardon without presence is not mercy at half strength. It is forgiveness that never reaches the wound.",
    "id": "ch07-p106"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David forgave his son and went on hiding his face for two years, because a hurt human heart can hide indefinitely.",
    "id": "ch07-p107"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God’s cannot.",
    "id": "ch07-p108"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is a story Jesus told, and He told it because He had been accused of eating with the wrong people. <em>This man receives sinners and eats with them</em> — that was the charge, and the story is His answer to it.",
    "id": "ch07-p109"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And when He built the father in that story, He built him out of somebody.",
    "id": "ch07-p110"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The younger son is still a long way off when the father sees him, and Luke spends three verbs on what happens next. He ran. He fell on his neck. He kissed him. In Greek those are the same three verbs, in the same order, that the Greek Old Testament uses for exactly one other reunion — the morning Esau came over the ridge with four hundred men behind him and ran at the brother who had spent twenty years certain he was coming to kill him.",
    "id": "ch07-p111"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jesus was accused of eating with the wrong people. He answered with a story, and He gave the father Esau’s legs.",
    "id": "ch07-p112"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It ends at a feast. One son is inside at the table. The other one will not come in.",
    "id": "ch07-p113"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the father does not send for him. He gets up, leaves his own party, and goes out to the door to plead with a son standing in the dark on purpose.",
    "id": "ch07-p114"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David waited two years for Absalom to come to him. This father would not wait through dinner.",
    "id": "ch07-p115"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The story never tells you whether the older son went in. It just leaves the father standing out there at the edge of the light, with the music going on behind him.",
    "id": "ch07-p116"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Elijah has just come off the most public miracle in generations, fire out of the sky on Carmel, at his word, in front of the assembled nation. And one death threat from one queen later, he is a day into the wilderness, under a juniper tree, asking God to take his life.",
    "id": "ch07-p117"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Whatever fell on Carmel, it is not holding him up now.",
    "id": "ch07-p118"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He walks forty days to Horeb, the mountain of God.",
    "id": "ch07-p119"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This mountain has become, by now, where the lost keep getting sent: a nation met the fire there, a shepherd met the bush there, and now a burned-out prophet crawls into a cave there, because crawling into a hole is what is left of his strength.",
    "id": "ch07-p120"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then God gives him Sinai again, piece by piece.",
    "id": "ch07-p121"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A wind tears the mountains apart; the LORD is not in the wind.",
    "id": "ch07-p122"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "An earthquake; He is not in the earthquake.",
    "id": "ch07-p123"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A fire; He is not in the fire.",
    "id": "ch07-p124"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "All the old spectacle, emptied out, as if heaven were retiring it in front of him.",
    "id": "ch07-p125"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Even his own fire. Especially his own fire.",
    "id": "ch07-p126"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And after the fire comes something the Hebrew can barely say and English cannot: <em>qol demamah daqqah</em> — a voice of thin silence. A sound so quiet it can only be heard by a man who has run out of everything else.",
    "id": "ch07-p127"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "When Elijah heard it, he wrapped his face in his mantle and went out and stood in the entrance of the cave. And behold, a voice came to him and said, “What are you doing here, Elijah?”"
    ],
    "ref": "1 Kings 19:13",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The wind did not make him move.",
    "id": "ch07-p128"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The earthquake did not make him move.",
    "id": "ch07-p129"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The whisper brings him to the mouth of the cave with his cloak pulled over his face, the same thing Moses did at the bush.",
    "id": "ch07-p130"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He knows exactly Who is in the thin silence.",
    "id": "ch07-p131"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the whisper does not scold him for despairing, and it does not heal him either; there is no verse where the darkness lifts.",
    "id": "ch07-p132"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It asks him a question, twice, and lets him empty his heart twice, and then it gives him what God gives His exhausted ones in the hiding centuries: work to do, a successor to find, and a corrected count.",
    "id": "ch07-p133"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seven thousand others, Elijah; you were never as alone as the despair said.",
    "id": "ch07-p134"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then it sends him back the way he came.",
    "id": "ch07-p135"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And here the scene refuses to resolve.",
    "id": "ch07-p136"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Elijah walks off the mountain of God with his face still wrapped, a man who heard the voice but never saw the glory.",
    "id": "ch07-p137"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The appointment is still on the books.",
    "id": "ch07-p138"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "My sister Hannah moved to heaven when she was twenty. A blood clot reached her heart, and I found out suddenly, in another state.",
    "id": "ch07-p139"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She threw me a surprise party once and would not simply let me walk into it. She hid the whole thing behind a scavenger hunt and made me find it, one clue at a time, until I opened the last door and everybody I knew was standing in the room.",
    "id": "ch07-p140"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When I moved away, she threw me a going-away party with a pineapple theme. Pineapples were mine, and she knew it the way she knew everything about me. She got every friend I had into one room for it.",
    "id": "ch07-p141"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I only found out later what a pineapple on a gatepost used to mean. Sea captains were said to set one out when they had made it home. It was the sign for a safe return.",
    "id": "ch07-p142"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She hung it on a party about leaving.",
    "id": "ch07-p143"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She was in the stands at my baseball games, all of them, including the ones that did not matter to anybody but the people playing in them.",
    "id": "ch07-p144"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And she was there on the worst days. She just came.",
    "id": "ch07-p145"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She had been doing that my whole life.",
    "id": "ch07-p146"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then she was not there, and the question that moved into the space she left was not about her.",
    "id": "ch07-p147"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was whether He had been in the stands.",
    "id": "ch07-p148"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Years later I was at a school of supernatural ministry in Pennsylvania, learning to listen.",
    "id": "ch07-p149"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The exercise was simple. A pen. A blank sheet of paper. A question for Jesus. Then you listened, and wrote down whatever came.",
    "id": "ch07-p150"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the top of mine, I wrote:",
    "id": "ch07-p151"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jesus, do You love me?",
    "id": "ch07-p152"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then nothing.",
    "id": "ch07-p153"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A minute passed. Maybe two.",
    "id": "ch07-p154"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I was overthinking it, because I already knew what I was waiting for. A sentence. Words in my head. The voice I had decided He used.",
    "id": "ch07-p155"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Not God, exactly.",
    "id": "ch07-p156"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The format I expected God to use.",
    "id": "ch07-p157"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nothing came in that format.",
    "id": "ch07-p158"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "My head went down, and the tears I had not cried in years began falling onto the page.",
    "id": "ch07-p159"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Onto the question.",
    "id": "ch07-p160"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then I saw Him.",
    "id": "ch07-p161"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I saw Jesus over the same page I was over, weeping, His tears falling onto the question from the other side.",
    "id": "ch07-p162"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They came down over my handwriting.",
    "id": "ch07-p163"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The ink began to run.",
    "id": "ch07-p164"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The words blurred beneath the water until I could no longer read what I had written.",
    "id": "ch07-p165"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I did not see His face.",
    "id": "ch07-p166"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I saw His tears.",
    "id": "ch07-p167"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And somewhere in the water spreading across that page, mine and His became impossible to separate.",
    "id": "ch07-p168"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He did not answer the question.",
    "id": "ch07-p169"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He answered it wet.",
    "id": "ch07-p170"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The shortest verse in the English Bible is two words long, and it is about His face.",
    "id": "ch07-p171"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Jesus wept."
    ],
    "ref": "John 11:35",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I had been waiting for the wind. The earthquake. The fire.",
    "id": "ch07-p172"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I had already decided which one I wanted.",
    "id": "ch07-p173"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But He came to me the way He came to that cave.",
    "id": "ch07-p174"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Not as the thing I was watching for.",
    "id": "ch07-p175"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "As tears.",
    "id": "ch07-p176"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God’s hiding is not like ours at one point: when He turns His face away, His attention does not go with it.",
    "id": "ch07-p177"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The face may disappear from the scene.",
    "id": "ch07-p178"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That scavenger hunt my sister sent me on was never meant to keep me out of the room.",
    "id": "ch07-p179"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The finding was the gift.",
    "id": "ch07-p180"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I did not know that while I was looking.",
    "id": "ch07-p181"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And there is one psalm where David takes the hiding word itself and turns it over in his hand.",
    "id": "ch07-p182"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He uses the same root twice in a single line — Cain’s root, the one Deuteronomy doubled into bold — and he uses it as an address.",
    "id": "ch07-p183"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "You hide them in the secret place of Your presence from the conspiracies of man."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 31:20",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Word for word, the Hebrew says: <em>You hide them in the hiding of Your face.</em>",
    "id": "ch07-p184"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Everywhere else, the hiding of the face is the catastrophe. Here it is the shelter.",
    "id": "ch07-p185"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is a hiding that puts you outside the face of God.",
    "id": "ch07-p186"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And there is a hiding that puts you inside it.",
    "id": "ch07-p187"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Isaiah, who named God twice by His hiding, is the one who finally puts a clock on it.",
    "id": "ch07-p188"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "In an outburst of anger I hid My face from you for a moment, but with everlasting lovingkindness I will have compassion on you."
    ],
    "ref": "Isaiah 54:8",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A moment, against everlasting.",
    "id": "ch07-p189"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He does not deny the anger and He does not soften the hiding. He measures it. And the measurement is the whole comfort: the thing that felt like the permanent weather of a life is, in the accounting of the One doing it, a moment.",
    "id": "ch07-p190"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He proves it twice.",
    "id": "ch07-p191"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first proof was on file four hundred years before the glory moved an inch: the way back was written before the hiding ever began.",
    "id": "ch07-p192"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The night Solomon dedicated the temple was the housewarming of God. The glory filled the building that week with such density that the priests could not stand up to minister; the opening ceremony ended with the clergy sitting down, because the Presence left no room to work.",
    "id": "ch07-p193"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that night God appears to Solomon, and He does something that reads almost unbearable in hindsight. In the middle of blessing the brand-new house, He begins to talk about the day His face will be hidden from it.",
    "id": "ch07-p194"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“When I shut up the heavens,” He says. “When the locusts come. When the rot sets in because My people have left Me.” On the happiest night in Israel’s history, with the cedar still fresh, God writes the disaster plan, and at the center of it He places this:",
    "id": "ch07-p195"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "and My people who are called by My name humble themselves and pray and seek My face and turn from their wicked ways, then I will hear from heaven, will forgive their sin and will heal their land."
    ],
    "ref": "2 Chronicles 7:14",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seek My face.",
    "id": "ch07-p196"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the next verse is the other half of it.",
    "id": "ch07-p197"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Now My eyes will be open and My ears attentive to the prayer offered in this place."
    ],
    "ref": "2 Chronicles 7:15",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He asks to be looked for, and in the same breath promises to be looking.",
    "id": "ch07-p198"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The people who wear My name, the name the blessing has been settling on them generation after generation, can end the hiding whenever they turn.",
    "id": "ch07-p199"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A door left closed, but never locked.",
    "id": "ch07-p200"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The way you leave a door when you want it opened.",
    "id": "ch07-p201"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hiding ends where the seeking starts.",
    "id": "ch07-p202"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And somebody did it.",
    "id": "ch07-p203"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Daniel is an old man in Babylon when he works out from the scrolls how long the exile is meant to run, and he does not celebrate. He fasts. He puts on sackcloth.",
    "id": "ch07-p204"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Twenty-five men once stood inside that house with their backs to it and their faces to the east.",
    "id": "ch07-p205"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Daniel is east. His windows are cut the other way.",
    "id": "ch07-p206"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Three times a day he kneels at those windows and faces a hill with nothing left on it.",
    "id": "ch07-p207"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He starts by giving his face away.",
    "id": "ch07-p208"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Hebrew has him hand it over before he says a word. Sackcloth, ashes, and a man surrendering the thing Adam carried into the trees.",
    "id": "ch07-p209"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then he prays one of the longest prayers in the Old Testament, and near the end of it he reaches for words he has known since he was a boy in Jerusalem.",
    "id": "ch07-p210"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "So now, our God, listen to the prayer of Your servant and to his supplications, and for Your sake, O Lord, let Your face shine on Your desolate sanctuary."
    ],
    "ref": "Daniel 9:17",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Let Your face shine.",
    "id": "ch07-p211"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is the blessing. The English reaches for a different verb here, and underneath it is the same one: the same word for shining, the same word for face, the one the priests had raised their hands and spoken over him every year of his childhood, the one that went into the ground on silver back home.",
    "id": "ch07-p212"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But he does not say it the way they said it.",
    "id": "ch07-p213"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When the priests raised their hands, the verb was about God. May He make His face shine. A wish spoken over a crowd, God in the third person, the way you talk about someone who is not in the room.",
    "id": "ch07-p214"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Daniel puts Him in the room. One letter changes at the front of the word, and the blessing stops being about God and starts being said to Him.",
    "id": "ch07-p215"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is not making a wish. He is telling God where to look.",
    "id": "ch07-p216"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Make Your face shine.",
    "id": "ch07-p217"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And he does not ask it for himself.",
    "id": "ch07-p218"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He asks it for the building. He asks God to turn His face back toward the room His face walked out of.",
    "id": "ch07-p219"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Gabriel reaches him, the text says, about the time of the evening offering.",
    "id": "ch07-p220"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every evening of his childhood a lamb went up at that hour. Smoke over the city every single day, so steady you did not need to be told what time it was. You could smell it.",
    "id": "ch07-p221"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is no lamb now. Nothing has gone up over that hill in nearly fifty years.",
    "id": "ch07-p222"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hour survives in one place, and he is kneeling in it.",
    "id": "ch07-p223"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is the hour heaven answers on.",
    "id": "ch07-p224"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He was not the only one. There is a psalm that prays the same line as a chorus, three times over, at the start and the middle and the end, the way a nation leans on a door: cause Your face to shine on us, and we will be saved.",
    "id": "ch07-p225"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Ezekiel, of all people, the man who had to watch the glory back out of its own house, is the one who gets shown the homecoming.",
    "id": "ch07-p226"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Years later, in a final vision, he is standing at the gate of a temple yet to be built, the east gate, the very gate the glory paused at on its way out.",
    "id": "ch07-p227"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "and behold, the glory of the God of Israel was coming from the way of the east. And His voice was like the sound of many waters; and the earth shone with His glory."
    ],
    "ref": "Ezekiel 43:2",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch07-v15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The same road.",
    "id": "ch07-p228"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He left by the east gate to the mountain east of the city, and He takes the identical route back.",
    "id": "ch07-p229"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The departure was walked backward on purpose, so that one day every step of it could be walked forward.",
    "id": "ch07-p230"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And this time nothing is backing away.",
    "id": "ch07-p231"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The very ground catches what His nearness does: <em>the earth shone with His glory</em>.",
    "id": "ch07-p232"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So Ezekiel ends with a road map.",
    "id": "ch07-p233"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "East, out. East, back in.",
    "id": "ch07-p234"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The last place on earth the glory was seen was the ridge east of the city, the Mount of Olives, standing over Jerusalem like a promise only one deportee was shown.",
    "id": "ch07-p235"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And for six hundred years, that is where the story waited: an east gate, an empty house, and a road with the return trip written into it.",
    "id": "ch07-p236"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Six hundred years later, a rabbi from Galilee comes up to Jerusalem for the feast, with crowds ahead of Him and crowds behind.",
    "id": "ch07-p237"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Of every approach a traveler can choose into the city, He chooses the east.",
    "id": "ch07-p238"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Over the Mount of Olives.",
    "id": "ch07-p239"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Down the glory’s old road, toward the glory’s old gate, walking the road of departure in the opposite direction.",
    "id": "ch07-p240"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "East, out. East, back in.",
    "id": "ch07-p241"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And as He crests the ridge, with the whole city laid out shining below Him, He stops.",
    "id": "ch07-p242"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The city is there. The temple is there. The road is there.",
    "id": "ch07-p243"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then He looks at Jerusalem.",
    "id": "ch07-p244"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "num": 8,
  "id": "ch08",
  "title": "The Face Set Like Flint",
  "hook": "Moses was told he could not see the face of God and live. On this mountain he is standing in front of it.",
  "glossary": [
   {
    "term": "metamorphoo",
    "gloss": "The Gospel writers, reaching for a word to file this morning under, chose a Greek one: metamorphoo — transformed, changed from the inside out."
   },
   {
    "term": "schizo",
    "gloss": "Mark tells it in one verse, and Mark has been saving the verb: schizo — to rip, to tear violently."
   }
  ],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch08-flint"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Before He takes the east road into Jerusalem, He climbs a different mountain, and heaven holds a reunion on it.",
    "id": "ch08-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He brings three fishermen up with Him to pray, and the climb has emptied them; Luke admits they are heavy with sleep.",
    "id": "ch08-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They are about to sleep through the moment when Sinai and the exodus walk onto the mountain together.",
    "id": "ch08-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A sight that would have rivaled anything between Sinai and the empty tomb nearly plays to an audience of none: three men asleep on a mountainside, and a few feet away, it begins.",
    "id": "ch08-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "And while He was praying, the appearance of His face became different, and His clothing became white and gleaming."
    ],
    "ref": "Luke 9:29",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Matthew says it plainer: <em>His face shone like the sun</em>.",
    "id": "ch08-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A face soaked in glory on a mountain, watchers afraid, brightness announcing where a man has been standing.",
    "id": "ch08-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But every other time, the shine was borrowed.",
    "id": "ch08-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses glowed the way a wall glows at sunset, catching light from a face not his own, and when the visits stopped, the glow died.",
    "id": "ch08-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This face is not reflecting the glory. It is where the glory is.",
    "id": "ch08-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And still Luke pins down a detail nobody inventing a God-scene would think to invent: it happened while He was praying.",
    "id": "ch08-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Even the source, in the hour of His own shining, is turned toward His Father.",
    "id": "ch08-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Glory never stops being a face turned toward a face.",
    "id": "ch08-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Not even His.",
    "id": "ch08-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the disciples’ sleep breaks, and they see that the shining man is not alone.",
    "id": "ch08-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two men are talking with Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses, and Elijah.",
    "id": "ch08-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And suddenly the whole history of borrowed glory is standing beside the source.",
    "id": "ch08-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Heaven did not pick this guest list at random.",
    "id": "ch08-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses: the man who begged, “Show me Your glory,” and got a crack in a rock and a hand over the gap, told with perfect tenderness, “You cannot see My face and live.”",
    "id": "ch08-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now he is seeing it.",
    "id": "ch08-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And he is alive.",
    "id": "ch08-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And no one on that mountain can explain either fact.",
    "id": "ch08-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Elijah: the man who stood on Horeb with his cloak over his face, who heard the thin silence but never saw the glory, whose encounter Scripture left open on purpose.",
    "id": "ch08-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The appointment was never cancelled. Here it is, kept.",
    "id": "ch08-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His face is bare, and it is turned toward the shining.",
    "id": "ch08-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The two great face-stories of the Old Testament stand on one summit with everything they were denied streaming down on them.",
    "id": "ch08-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses sees the face he was told he could not see. Elijah sees the glory he left without seeing. And both of them are standing beside the One who was always the answer.",
    "id": "ch08-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Luke, alone, leans close enough to tell us what the three of them are discussing, and the word he chooses is a buried charge in the Greek. <em>They were speaking of His departure, which He was about to accomplish at Jerusalem.</em>",
    "id": "ch08-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His exodus.",
    "id": "ch08-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses is standing on a mountain talking through an exodus one last time. And this time he is not leading it; he is looking at the One who will: an exodus not out of Egypt but out of the grave, with a whole enslaved race behind Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The three brightest figures on earth are calmly planning a crucifixion. The most radiant scene in the Gospels exists to schedule the darkest one, and nobody shining looks surprised.",
    "id": "ch08-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Peter, of course, says something. “Lord, it is good for us to be here; let us make three tabernacles.” Luke covers for him gently: he did not realize what he was saying.",
    "id": "ch08-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A cloud swallows the summit, and a voice comes out of it, and the Father preaches His whole sermon in a single sentence: “This is My Son, My Chosen One; listen to Him.” And then the glory folds itself away, and the mountain is just a mountain, and Jesus is found alone.",
    "id": "ch08-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Gospel writers, reaching for a word to file this morning under, chose a Greek one: <em>metamorphoo</em> — transformed, changed from the inside out. In the whole New Testament, that word is spent on only four sentences. Two of them describe this mountain.",
    "id": "ch08-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The other two are waiting somewhere else in the New Testament. And they are not about Jesus.",
    "id": "ch08-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The face that has just shone like the sun now turns toward Jerusalem.",
    "id": "ch08-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Luke says: <em>When the days were approaching for His ascension, He was determined to go to Jerusalem.</em>",
    "id": "ch08-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Determined.",
    "id": "ch08-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Greek says something more physical: He set His face.",
    "id": "ch08-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He fixed it there. Aimed it, the way you aim something that is never turning around.",
    "id": "ch08-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Isaiah, seven centuries earlier, had already heard that sentence coming, and wrote out what setting that face would cost, in the voice of a Servant who saw the whole road:",
    "id": "ch08-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "For the Lord GOD helps Me, therefore, I am not disgraced; therefore, I have set My face like flint, and I know that I will not be ashamed."
    ],
    "ref": "Isaiah 50:7",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Flint.",
    "id": "ch08-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Marble is stone for admiring. Granite is stone for enduring.",
    "id": "ch08-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Flint is the stone you strike fire from.",
    "id": "ch08-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And one verse earlier, the same Servant has already counted what the setting will cost: “I did not cover My face from humiliation and spitting.”",
    "id": "ch08-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There it is. Written down seven hundred years in advance.",
    "id": "ch08-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A book full of covered faces: Moses under his veil, Elijah under his cloak.",
    "id": "ch08-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And now one face refuses the veil at the precise moment a veil is finally justified.",
    "id": "ch08-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He knows what Jerusalem is going to do to that face.",
    "id": "ch08-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He aims it there anyway.",
    "id": "ch08-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man who shone on the mountain now walks toward Golgotha with that same face set straight ahead.",
    "id": "ch08-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Luke did not invent that phrase. He took it out of the Greek Old Testament, where it already belonged to a prophet.",
    "id": "ch08-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Set your face. God says it to that man over and over, and it is never gentle. Set your face toward the mountains of Israel. Toward the south. Toward the daughters of your people. And once, toward a city.",
    "id": "ch08-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Son of man, set your face toward Jerusalem… and prophesy."
    ],
    "ref": "Ezekiel 21:2",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Same verb. Same noun. Same city.",
    "id": "ch08-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God used those words to aim a prophet at Jerusalem. Luke uses them when the Son turns toward it.",
    "id": "ch08-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One of them set his face at that city to tell it what was coming. The other set His to go and take it.",
    "id": "ch08-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A deportee by a canal watched the glory stop on a ridge and stand there, hoping to be called after.",
    "id": "ch08-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "An east gate.",
    "id": "ch08-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "An empty house.",
    "id": "ch08-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A promise about a road.",
    "id": "ch08-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now He comes up to the city for the feast from the east, over the Mount of Olives, down the glory’s old ridge.",
    "id": "ch08-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And this time, Jerusalem is waiting for Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Coats spread on the road, branches cut down, a king’s welcome at last.",
    "id": "ch08-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The welcome that never came when the glory left has finally arrived, six centuries late, at the top of a thousand lungs.",
    "id": "ch08-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then He crests the ridge, and His face changes.",
    "id": "ch08-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "When He approached Jerusalem, He saw the city and wept over it,"
    ],
    "ref": "Luke 19:41",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Tears.",
    "id": "ch08-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The glory returns by the promised road and comes over the rise sobbing.",
    "id": "ch08-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Luke’s word is not a discreet welling-up; it is the loud grief of a funeral, the kind of crying that bends a man forward.",
    "id": "ch08-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He gets the words out through it:",
    "id": "ch08-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“If you had known in this day, even you, the things which make for peace!",
    "id": "ch08-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“But now they have been hidden from your eyes.”",
    "id": "ch08-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Even His grief comes out in the language of hidden and seen.",
    "id": "ch08-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The city that failed to recognize the face of God is losing the ability to see.",
    "id": "ch08-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "When the glory left this city, it left in silence, and nobody noticed.",
    "id": "ch08-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Today the glory comes back weeping out loud, and still nobody notices.",
    "id": "ch08-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The face of God, wet, coming down the Mount of Olives into the city that will kill Him by Friday, because not going was never on the table for a face set like flint.",
    "id": "ch08-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What Jerusalem does with that face, Isaiah had also already written.",
    "id": "ch08-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "He was despised and forsaken of men, a man of sorrows and acquainted with grief; and like one from whom men hide their face He was despised, and we did not esteem Him."
    ],
    "ref": "Isaiah 53:3",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Like one from whom men hide their face.</em>",
    "id": "ch08-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The direction of the hiding has reversed.",
    "id": "ch08-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "For seven chapters, human beings have hidden from the face of God: Adam in the trees, a nation behind its spokesman, a prophet under his cloak.",
    "id": "ch08-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now the faces turning aside are ours, and the face being avoided is His.",
    "id": "ch08-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is Eden’s move again, with one new fact in it: the face now has skin.",
    "id": "ch08-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And skin can be reached.",
    "id": "ch08-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Between those two sentences there is a garden.",
    "id": "ch08-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He goes out of the city, down through the ravine and up among the olive trees, the way He has gone so many nights that the man betraying Him never has to ask where to look.",
    "id": "ch08-p85"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Mark says He fell to the ground.",
    "id": "ch08-p86"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Matthew tells you what part of Him landed first.",
    "id": "ch08-p87"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "He went a little beyond them, and fell on His face, and prayed, “My Father, if it is possible, let this cup pass from Me.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Matthew 26:39",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v6"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The last time human beings met God in a garden in the evening, they put trees between their faces and His.",
    "id": "ch08-p88"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is back among the trees. It is evening. And He is the one face down.",
    "id": "ch08-p89"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the high priest’s house, after the arrest, in the small hours of Friday, they reach it.",
    "id": "ch08-p90"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Then they spat in His face,</em> Matthew writes, <em>and beat Him with their fists.</em>",
    "id": "ch08-p91"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Once, a single spit landed on Miriam’s face, and God Himself called it a defilement worth seven days of exile from the camp.",
    "id": "ch08-p92"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Tonight the spit lands on the face of God, again, and again, and again.",
    "id": "ch08-p93"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And there is no quarantine.",
    "id": "ch08-p94"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He does not cover His face.",
    "id": "ch08-p95"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seven hundred years out, the Servant had promised: “I did not cover My face.”",
    "id": "ch08-p96"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He keeps that word at the hour when keeping it costs the most.",
    "id": "ch08-p97"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Isaiah has been standing here from the beginning — the flint, the spitting, the faces turned away. There is a reason he keeps knowing what this face would do.",
    "id": "ch08-p98"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He had seen it.",
    "id": "ch08-p99"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His call came the year King Uzziah died. The Lord, high and lifted up, the train of His robe filling the temple, and over the throne the burning ones — six wings each, and two of the six spent covering their own faces.",
    "id": "ch08-p100"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Isaiah’s first word in that room is not worship.",
    "id": "ch08-p101"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Woe is me, for I am ruined!… For my eyes have seen the King, the LORD of hosts."
    ],
    "ref": "Isaiah 6:5",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then John, writing about a city that would not believe, reaches back into that throne room for the reason why. And he adds a sentence that changes what Isaiah saw.",
    "id": "ch08-p102"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "These things Isaiah said because he saw His glory, and he spoke of Him."
    ],
    "ref": "John 12:41",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His glory. Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p103"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The face on that throne is the face in this room tonight.",
    "id": "ch08-p104"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The burning ones would not look at it.",
    "id": "ch08-p105"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The soldiers are spitting on it.",
    "id": "ch08-p106"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then they do something they could never have done if they had understood the story they were standing inside.",
    "id": "ch08-p107"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They blindfold Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p108"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They knot a cloth over the eyes of God, and they beat His face, and they make a game of it:",
    "id": "ch08-p109"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Prophesy! Who is the one who hit You?”",
    "id": "ch08-p110"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every veil until now was hung to protect the human being from the face of God.",
    "id": "ch08-p111"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses veiled his face to spare the people.",
    "id": "ch08-p112"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The priests’ fingers made a lattice God peered through in love.",
    "id": "ch08-p113"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The temple curtain stood so that sinners could live next door to glory and survive.",
    "id": "ch08-p114"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This one is different.",
    "id": "ch08-p115"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This veil is not protecting them from seeing God. It is protecting them from being seen by Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p116"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Tied on so that men can beat the face of God without being looked at while they do it.",
    "id": "ch08-p117"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The night is Sinai turned inside out.",
    "id": "ch08-p118"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Once, a nation begged the face to keep its distance.",
    "id": "ch08-p119"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Tonight, the distance walks up to the face, fist by fist.",
    "id": "ch08-p120"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Out in the courtyard of that same house, by a charcoal fire, Peter is coming apart.",
    "id": "ch08-p121"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The servants and officers have built the fire against the cold, and Peter stands among them warming himself, close enough to see in, far enough to pass as one of them.",
    "id": "ch08-p122"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And one question at a time, he denies everything.",
    "id": "ch08-p123"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A servant girl, a bystander, a relative of the man whose ear he cut off.",
    "id": "ch08-p124"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Three times:",
    "id": "ch08-p125"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“I do not know Him.”",
    "id": "ch08-p126"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Matthew adds the detail Luke spares us: by the third time, Peter is swearing to it — he began to curse and swear, “I do not know the man” — calling down oaths to make the lie hold.",
    "id": "ch08-p127"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The oath is still in his mouth when the rooster goes off like an alarm.",
    "id": "ch08-p128"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then the face he has just denied turns toward him.",
    "id": "ch08-p129"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Luke records what happened next in a sentence as heavy as any he ever wrote.",
    "id": "ch08-p130"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The Lord turned and looked at Peter."
    ],
    "ref": "Luke 22:61",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the middle of His own trial, bound, spat on, condemned, Jesus has not lost track of Peter.",
    "id": "ch08-p131"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His face is swollen from the fists; between Him and the courtyard there is a crowd, and smoke, and dark; and at the exact moment the third denial leaves Peter’s mouth, that face comes around and finds him.",
    "id": "ch08-p132"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Of all the faces in the courtyard, it finds the one trying hardest not to be known.",
    "id": "ch08-p133"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Peter had been under that look once before. It is where his name came from.",
    "id": "ch08-p134"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The day they met, John says Jesus looked at him. Long, and fixed, and in no hurry. The same looking, and the same word. Then He said: you are Simon, son of John. You will be called Rock.",
    "id": "ch08-p135"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The look that named him is the look that finds him now.",
    "id": "ch08-p136"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Scripture does not tell us what was in the look, only what it did: Peter went out and wept bitterly.",
    "id": "ch08-p137"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was not the look that says, “I am finished with you.” And it was not surprise, either. He had named the rooster hours before.",
    "id": "ch08-p138"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was the look of Someone who had known the worst in advance and come anyway.",
    "id": "ch08-p139"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "If you have ever failed Him at the exact point you swore you never would, and then felt found in the middle of the lie, you know what Peter carried out of that courtyard.",
    "id": "ch08-p140"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Peter stumbles out into the dark with the smell of the charcoal fire soaked into his clothes, the face behind him and the night ahead.",
    "id": "ch08-p141"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is where we leave him: undone by a look, smelling of smoke.",
    "id": "ch08-p142"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then Friday.",
    "id": "ch08-p143"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The nails at nine in the morning.",
    "id": "ch08-p144"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And a man lifted into the air where a crowd has no choice but to look at him.",
    "id": "ch08-p145"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There was one other time God told people to do that, and the instruction asked the dying to look straight at the thing that was killing them. A camp full of snakebite, people going down in their tents, and the order comes: make a bronze serpent, set it up on a pole, and <em>everyone who is bitten, when he looks at it, he will live.</em>",
    "id": "ch08-p146"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The verb is <em>ra'ah</em>. A slave girl’s verb, from a spring in the desert.",
    "id": "ch08-p147"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Since the cleft in the rock, the rule has been one sentence long. <em>No man can see Me and live.</em>",
    "id": "ch08-p148"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Here it runs backward.",
    "id": "ch08-p149"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Look, and live.",
    "id": "ch08-p150"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He had said out loud, months before this morning, what the pole had always been for. <em>As Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of Man be lifted up.</em>",
    "id": "ch08-p151"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And at noon, when the sun should be at full strength, darkness comes down over the whole land and stays for three hours.",
    "id": "ch08-p152"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hiding of the face had always been weather a soul could feel; today the sky performs it.",
    "id": "ch08-p153"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "As if creation itself pulled a veil across, because what is happening beneath it should not be looked at.",
    "id": "ch08-p154"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And out of that darkness, near three o’clock, comes the loudest sentence of the crucifixion.",
    "id": "ch08-p155"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And it is not in Greek.",
    "id": "ch08-p156"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And not in the holy Hebrew of the temple.",
    "id": "ch08-p157"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is street Aramaic, His mother tongue, the language mothers in Nazareth used to call their children in at dusk:",
    "id": "ch08-p158"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani.”",
    "id": "ch08-p159"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“My God, My God, why have You forsaken Me?”",
    "id": "ch08-p160"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Everything funnels into those words.",
    "id": "ch08-p161"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Cain’s dread.",
    "id": "ch08-p162"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David’s how long.",
    "id": "ch08-p163"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The psalm that ends in darkness.",
    "id": "ch08-p164"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every one of them pours into one throat now. The Son’s.",
    "id": "ch08-p165"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The hiding has reached the place where it is most unbearable: the Son crying out to the Father from inside the darkness.",
    "id": "ch08-p166"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It sounds like the end of everything.",
    "id": "ch08-p167"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But He is quoting.",
    "id": "ch08-p168"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The cry is the first line of the twenty-second psalm, and in that world, a rabbi who gives you the opening line of a psalm is placing the whole psalm in your hands.",
    "id": "ch08-p169"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He knew this psalm the way you know the songs of your childhood, and He knew what was in it:",
    "id": "ch08-p170"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "the pierced hands,",
    "id": "ch08-p171"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "the divided garments,",
    "id": "ch08-p172"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "the mockers shaking their heads,",
    "id": "ch08-p173"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "the psalm being performed around Him, verse by verse, by people who had no idea they were inside it.",
    "id": "ch08-p174"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He knew where the psalm lands, twenty-three verses past its terrible opening, on a sentence nobody standing at Golgotha that afternoon could have believed:",
    "id": "ch08-p175"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "For He has not despised nor abhorred the affliction of the afflicted; nor has He hidden His face from him; but when he cried to Him for help, He heard."
    ],
    "ref": "Psalm 22:24",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He has not hidden His face from him.",
    "id": "ch08-p176"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The answer was inside the cry the whole time.",
    "id": "ch08-p177"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He had said so out loud, centuries before He was born.",
    "id": "ch08-p178"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Isaiah once wrote a line about waiting on a God who would not show Himself. I will wait for the LORD, who is hiding His face from the house of Jacob, and I will look for Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p179"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The letter to the Hebrews quotes that line, and it does not assign it to Isaiah.",
    "id": "ch08-p180"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It puts it in the mouth of the Son.",
    "id": "ch08-p181"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "And again: “I will put My trust in Him.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Hebrews 2:13",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The one Person who would ever have that face turned away from Him had already said, in advance, that He would wait for it.",
    "id": "ch08-p182"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the sentence quoted in the same breath is the other half of it. Here am I, and the children God has given Me. He is not standing in front of the hidden face alone. He has everyone who ever ran into the trees and the caves and the boats gathered in behind Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p183"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The cry and the answer came from the same pen.",
    "id": "ch08-p184"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that is why He chose this psalm for His dying words: it was the one song in the hymnbook that could scream the darkness and keep the Father’s face in the same breath.",
    "id": "ch08-p185"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Its final line, the note the whole poem resolves to, is this:",
    "id": "ch08-p186"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He has done it.",
    "id": "ch08-p187"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The work is accomplished.",
    "id": "ch08-p188"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So when Jesus pushes up against the nails one last time and says, “It is finished,” He is not collapsing at a random verse.",
    "id": "ch08-p189"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is landing the psalm.",
    "id": "ch08-p190"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first line comes out of the darkness.",
    "id": "ch08-p191"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The final line arrives with His last breath.",
    "id": "ch08-p192"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the last line of the Servant song says what it was He lifted.",
    "id": "ch08-p193"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "He poured out Himself to death… yet He Himself bore the sin of many."
    ],
    "ref": "Isaiah 53:12",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Bore.</em> It is the word from the field east of Eden, the one Cain said could not be moved. It is also what God called Himself in the crack of the rock, the day Moses asked to see Him and got a name instead.",
    "id": "ch08-p194"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The verb that lifts a face and the verb that carries a sin were never two verbs.",
    "id": "ch08-p195"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And on Friday, one man was up in the air doing both.",
    "id": "ch08-p196"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the moment He died, across the city, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom.",
    "id": "ch08-p197"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Mark tells it in one verse, and Mark has been saving the verb: <em>schizo</em> — to rip, to tear violently.",
    "id": "ch08-p198"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He spends it only twice in his whole Gospel.",
    "id": "ch08-p199"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the baptism, the heavens are torn open, and the Father’s voice comes through the tear:",
    "id": "ch08-p200"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“You are My beloved Son.”",
    "id": "ch08-p201"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the death, the curtain is torn open, and no voice comes through at all, because this time the tear itself is the announcement.",
    "id": "ch08-p202"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The barrier that had kept the Presence and the people apart is ripped open in a single direction: from top to bottom.",
    "id": "ch08-p203"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "From the high end.",
    "id": "ch08-p204"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "From the side no human hand could reach.",
    "id": "ch08-p205"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And there was something woven into that curtain that almost nobody thinks about.",
    "id": "ch08-p206"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God gave the pattern Himself, thread by thread: blue and purple and scarlet, fine twisted linen, the work of a skilled designer. And worked into the cloth, cherubim.",
    "id": "ch08-p207"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Cherubim. The last time those appeared in the story, they were not decoration.",
    "id": "ch08-p208"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They were posted at the east side of a garden with a flaming sword, to guard the way back to the tree of life.",
    "id": "ch08-p209"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That curtain hung in the house of God for a thousand years with Eden’s guards embroidered on it. The barrier between the people and the Presence had been wearing a picture of the first barrier there ever was.",
    "id": "ch08-p210"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And when it came apart, the guards came apart with it.",
    "id": "ch08-p211"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At Sinai, we begged for distance from the face. The veil became that distance, woven into the architecture of worship and renewed every generation by our fear.",
    "id": "ch08-p212"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "We could never have taken it down ourselves. We could only keep adding to it.",
    "id": "ch08-p213"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was always going to have to be torn from His side.",
    "id": "ch08-p214"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "On Friday afternoon, it was.",
    "id": "ch08-p215"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch08-veil"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The letter to the Hebrews, looking back, names the curtain with four words:",
    "id": "ch08-p216"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "through the veil, that is, His flesh.",
    "id": "ch08-p217"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The final veil between the face of God and the faces of men was not woven.",
    "id": "ch08-p218"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It breathed.",
    "id": "ch08-p219"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every curtain has been ours: our terror, our request, our hiding.",
    "id": "ch08-p220"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The tearing is His.",
    "id": "ch08-p221"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And out at the hill, one man was looking straight at Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p222"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "For eight chapters, people have turned away from this face. Adam into the trees. A nation backing off a mountain. <em>Like one from whom men hide their face.</em> A blindfold, a few hours ago, in a room across town.",
    "id": "ch08-p223"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Mark keeps a detail most readers walk straight past. The centurion was standing <em>ex enantias autou</em> — opposite Him. Facing Him. He watched the whole thing from the front.",
    "id": "ch08-p224"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And when it was over, the first human being in that Gospel to say out loud what He was, was the man assigned to kill Him.",
    "id": "ch08-p225"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Truly this man was the Son of God!"
    ],
    "ref": "Mark 15:39",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch08-v13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The women who loved Him were watching from a distance.",
    "id": "ch08-p226"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man who did not look away was on the payroll.",
    "id": "ch08-p227"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They took Him down before sundown.",
    "id": "ch08-p228"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A borrowed tomb, a rolled stone, a Roman seal, and the Sabbath came down over Jerusalem like one more veil.",
    "id": "ch08-p229"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The friends who loved Him scattered into locked rooms.",
    "id": "ch08-p230"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And by every measure the world knows how to take, the story was over.",
    "id": "ch08-p231"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The face that shone like the sun on the mountain lay in the dark under a stone.",
    "id": "ch08-p232"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The eyes that found Peter across a crowded courtyard were closed.",
    "id": "ch08-p233"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Three days later, before there was light enough to see a face, a woman came down to the garden in the dark.",
    "id": "ch08-p234"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "num": 9,
  "id": "ch09",
  "title": "Eyes Opened",
  "hook": "Seven miles beside Him, arguing about Him, and it is the way He breaks the bread that gives Him away.",
  "glossary": [
   {
    "term": "lechem ha-panim",
    "gloss": "Lechem ha-panim."
   },
   {
    "term": "anthrakia",
    "gloss": "The Greek word is anthrakia, and in the entire New Testament it burns in just two places."
   },
   {
    "term": "hester panim",
    "gloss": "So Pentecost is hester panim ending: the return date finally arrived, the answer arriving for every psalm so far that cried, “How long will You hide Your face?”"
   },
   {
    "term": "prosopon",
    "gloss": "The Greek word Peter says is prosopon."
   },
   {
    "term": "metamorphoo",
    "gloss": "The Greek is a word you have seen on a mountain: metamorphoo — the transfiguration word."
   }
  ],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch09-emmaus"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is still dark when she reaches the tomb, and the stone is already moved.",
    "id": "ch09-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Mary runs for Peter and John. They come, and look, and go home again, and she is left standing outside by herself, crying.",
    "id": "ch09-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then she bends down and looks in.",
    "id": "ch09-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "and she saw two angels in white sitting, one at the head and one at the feet, where the body of Jesus had been lying."
    ],
    "ref": "John 20:12",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One at the head. One at the feet. And the empty space in between.",
    "id": "ch09-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She says nothing about it. She has not come to look at angels. They ask her why she is crying and she answers the way you answer strangers at a bad time: they have taken away my Lord, and I do not know where they have put Him.",
    "id": "ch09-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then she turns around, and He is standing there, and she does not know Him.",
    "id": "ch09-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She has walked with this man for years. She knows that voice. And on the first morning of the new world she looks straight into the face of God and takes Him for the groundskeeper.",
    "id": "ch09-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Which tells you where she is standing. A garden. Early. Before the heat of the day.",
    "id": "ch09-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He asks her two questions, and the second one is the oldest one He has.",
    "id": "ch09-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Woman, why are you weeping? Whom are you seeking?</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She does not recognize the voice. She does not recognize the face.",
    "id": "ch09-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So He uses the one thing that has never once failed.",
    "id": "ch09-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Mary.</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Her name. Nothing else. No explanation, no proof, no rebuke for missing Him. The same move that turned a runaway slave around at a spring, and an old shepherd at a bush, and a short man out of a tree.",
    "id": "ch09-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And she comes apart on one word.",
    "id": "ch09-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Rabboni.</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first person on earth to see the risen face of God was not a priest, and not an apostle, and not anyone whose testimony would have been admitted in a court in that country.",
    "id": "ch09-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She was a woman crying in a garden at dawn who thought she was talking to the help.",
    "id": "ch09-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seven miles is a long walk when hope is behind you.",
    "id": "ch09-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two of them are walking to a village called Emmaus, putting distance between themselves and Jerusalem, when a stranger falls in step and asks what they are discussing.",
    "id": "ch09-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Luke catches their answer in a single Greek word: they stopped, and stood there, downcast.",
    "id": "ch09-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Gloomy-faced.",
    "id": "ch09-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One of them, Cleopas, manages the bitter little joke of the shattered:",
    "id": "ch09-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Are You the only visitor to Jerusalem who does not know what happened there these last days?”",
    "id": "ch09-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is asking it of the one person in Judea who knows exactly what happened.",
    "id": "ch09-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the stranger does not reveal Himself.",
    "id": "ch09-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He starts at Moses and walks the whole scroll forward, showing them that the suffering was never the plan failing. The suffering was the plan.",
    "id": "ch09-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hope starts moving in their chests before they have any idea Whose voice is moving it.",
    "id": "ch09-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the village they press Him: “stay with us, it is nearly evening.”",
    "id": "ch09-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So He goes in.",
    "id": "ch09-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And at the table, the guest becomes the host; He takes the bread, and blesses it, and breaks it, and hands it to them, and the oldest wound in the world begins to open backward.",
    "id": "ch09-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Then their eyes were opened and they recognized Him; and He vanished from their sight."
    ],
    "ref": "Luke 24:31",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Those three words are already in the Bible. In the garden, over food, about two people.",
    "id": "ch09-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Then the eyes of both of them were opened, and they knew that they were naked; and they sewed fig leaves together and made themselves loin coverings."
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 3:7",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the Greek Bible the early church carried, those two lines match word for word.",
    "id": "ch09-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Their eyes were opened.",
    "id": "ch09-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In Eden, their eyes were opened when they ate.",
    "id": "ch09-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The fruit went in, and the seeing changed, and the seeing drove them from the face.",
    "id": "ch09-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At Emmaus, their eyes do not open on the road, for all seven miles of teaching.",
    "id": "ch09-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They open when He hands them bread and they eat.",
    "id": "ch09-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the garden the food was taken. At the table it is handed to them.",
    "id": "ch09-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first food opened human eyes to shame. The bread in His hands opens human eyes to Him.",
    "id": "ch09-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Israel’s sanctuary had been saying it in furniture all along. Twelve loaves on a golden table, set out before the Presence, replaced every week, century after century.",
    "id": "ch09-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Lechem ha-panim.</em> The bread of the face.",
    "id": "ch09-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A jar of manna had already been set before Him in the wilderness and kept there. From the wilderness on, there was always bread in front of His face.",
    "id": "ch09-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The order came with a word attached: <em>tamid</em>, continually. Never an empty table. And when the psalms tell Israel to seek His face, they reach for that same word. Seek His face continually. The bread of the face, continually. One standing order, written twice, once for the table and once for the people.",
    "id": "ch09-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And bread was not the only thing on that table. The same instructions set jars on it, for pouring out wine. Bread and wine, in front of the face of God, for as long as there was a sanctuary standing.",
    "id": "ch09-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Tonight the Face is on the other side of the bread, handing it across the table.",
    "id": "ch09-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then everything runs backward.",
    "id": "ch09-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In Eden, opened eyes sent two people into the trees, away from the face, out of the garden, east, exiled.",
    "id": "ch09-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At Emmaus, opened eyes put two people on the road that same hour, in the dark, running seven miles back toward Jerusalem, the city where their hope had just been crucified.",
    "id": "ch09-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Opened eyes used to be how paradise ended.",
    "id": "ch09-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Now they are how the news gets out.",
    "id": "ch09-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And as they run, they finally name what the road had done to them:",
    "id": "ch09-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Were not our hearts burning within us while He spoke?”",
    "id": "ch09-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Fire again.",
    "id": "ch09-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And it is moving.",
    "id": "ch09-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In Galilee, the man who walked out of that courtyard undone by a look, smelling of smoke, has gone back to fishing.",
    "id": "ch09-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Whatever the others made of the empty tomb and the locked-room appearances, Peter went back to the boats, to the one thing he knew how to do before Jesus ever said, “Follow Me.”",
    "id": "ch09-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He fishes all night and catches nothing, which is exactly how his story with Jesus began.",
    "id": "ch09-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And just as day is breaking, a figure stands on the beach and calls out about the nets.",
    "id": "ch09-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The net fills past hauling.",
    "id": "ch09-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And John says the words to Peter that John always sees first:",
    "id": "ch09-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“It is the Lord.”",
    "id": "ch09-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Peter does not wait for the boat.",
    "id": "ch09-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He throws himself into the sea and beats the sunrise to shore, comes up the beach soaked and heaving, and stops.",
    "id": "ch09-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "So when they got out on the land, they saw a charcoal fire already laid and fish placed on it, and bread."
    ],
    "ref": "John 21:9",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v4"
   },
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch09-charcoal"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A charcoal fire. Because smell is the sense time cannot silence; one breath of charcoal smoke and Peter is standing in that courtyard again, the rooster still ringing in his ears.",
    "id": "ch09-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Greek word is <em>anthrakia</em>, and in the entire New Testament it burns in just two places. One is the high priest’s courtyard, the fire Peter warmed himself over while he denied Jesus three times. The other is here, on a beach at dawn, a fire the risen Lord built with His own hands before the boat ever landed.",
    "id": "ch09-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first fire had nothing on it. Peter stood at that one with his hands empty and swore he had never met the man.",
    "id": "ch09-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This one was already cooking when he got there.",
    "id": "ch09-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Of all the ways to cook a fish, He chose the one that smells like Peter’s worst night. Jesus has rebuilt the scene of the denial: the same smoke in the air, the same gray hour before sunrise, a small ring of people around a fire. And He does not open with the questions. “Come and have breakfast,” He says: the risen Lord of glory handing bread and fish to tired men in wet clothes, waiting until they have eaten before He says the hard thing.",
    "id": "ch09-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then, three times, the question. “Simon, son of John, do you love Me?” By the third asking Peter is grieved, because he can count, and he knows exactly which three this three is for. And what he answers with is the courtyard’s sentence answered at last. There, his sentence was “I do not know Him.” Here, pressed the third time, all he has left is that sentence’s mirror image: “Lord, You know all things; You know that I love You.” The man who swore he did not know stakes everything on being known. He cannot appeal to his record; his record is the denials. So he appeals past his own testimony to the only witness who saw all the way down, and the very exposure that terrified a liar by a fire becomes the ground a restored man stands on.",
    "id": "ch09-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And every answer is met, not with a verdict, but with a job: “feed My lambs. Shepherd My sheep.” Three denials by one fire. Three commissions by another.",
    "id": "ch09-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the courtyard, the look had to cross a crowded distance. On the beach there is no distance left. The face and the failure are at arm’s length, with breakfast cooking between them.",
    "id": "ch09-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "On the road, seven miles of conversation with Him and nothing. It took bread.",
    "id": "ch09-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "On the water, Peter is looking straight at Him and does not know Him. It took a net full of fish.",
    "id": "ch09-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Both times, the face gave itself away by feeding them.",
    "id": "ch09-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That same night in Jerusalem, when the whole room decided He was a ghost, He did not argue the point. He asked whether there was anything to eat, and He ate a piece of broiled fish while they watched Him do it. The proof that He had a body you could look at was a meal, eaten in front of your face.",
    "id": "ch09-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A few days after Hannah’s celebration of life, friends sat me down and prayed for me.",
    "id": "ch09-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was evening. I had reached the place where it was no longer enough to think God was real. I needed to know.",
    "id": "ch09-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Before they started, they asked me to say out loud the lies I had been believing. I said three.",
    "id": "ch09-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That God could not forgive me, and that I was not good enough.",
    "id": "ch09-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That God was angry with me.",
    "id": "ch09-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that He had not been there when my sister died. What I actually said was, <em>Lord, if You had been here.</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then they prayed, and something in my chest let go — a tension I had carried so long I had stopped hoping for a cure.",
    "id": "ch09-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then one of them put a Bible in my hands and told me to open it without looking and put my finger down.",
    "id": "ch09-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I opened it. I put my finger on the page.",
    "id": "ch09-p85"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "“I, even I, am the one who wipes out your transgressions for My own sake, and I will not remember your sins.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Isaiah 43:25",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I had to clear my throat before I could read it out loud.",
    "id": "ch09-p86"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I did it again.",
    "id": "ch09-p87"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "John 11. And the heading over the page said: <em>Jesus comforts the sisters of Lazarus.</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p88"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two sisters who had lost their brother. Two brothers who had lost their sister.",
    "id": "ch09-p89"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the sentence I had said out loud before they prayed is in that chapter. Both sisters say it to His face. <em>Lord, if You had been here.</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p90"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I did it a third time.",
    "id": "ch09-p91"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "“Well done, good and faithful servant.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Matthew 25:21",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every one of them answering the exact sentence I had said out loud before anybody opened anything.",
    "id": "ch09-p92"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The tears did not fall that time. They rose.",
    "id": "ch09-p93"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Forty days after the empty tomb, He walked them out over the same ridge the glory had once backed away across, and did the last thing anyone on earth ever saw Him do.",
    "id": "ch09-p94"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He lifted up His hands.",
    "id": "ch09-p95"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is not a wave goodbye. That is the priest’s gesture, the same posture the sons of Aaron had been raising over bowed heads for fifteen hundred years.",
    "id": "ch09-p96"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And you already know what a priest says with his hands up like that.",
    "id": "ch09-p97"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "While He was blessing them, He parted from them and was carried up into heaven."
    ],
    "ref": "Luke 24:51",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "While He was blessing them.",
    "id": "ch09-p98"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Greek has a way to say <em>after He had blessed them.</em> Luke did not use it. He used the one that says <em>while.</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p99"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He did not finish and then go. He went mid-sentence, hands still up, and nobody standing on that hill ever heard the end of it.",
    "id": "ch09-p100"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Fifty days after Passover, the fire finds its destination.",
    "id": "ch09-p101"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They are all together in one place in Jerusalem when a sound like a violent rushing wind fills the whole house, and then the fire appears — dividing itself, coming apart into tongues, and coming to rest on each of them, one flame per head, nobody missed.",
    "id": "ch09-p102"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This fire has a history. It burned in a bush for an audience of one, and the one man hid his face. It burned on a mountain for a nation, and the nation begged it to keep its distance. It came out of a tent on the day the priesthood opened, and it consumed what was on the altar, and a whole nation shouted and fell on its face. Eight verses later it came out of the same tent and consumed two priests. All through it, the fire of God has been the thing you approach through a mediator, behind a veil, at the foot of the mountain, at the far end of the counteroffer.",
    "id": "ch09-p103"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This morning the approach reverses. The fire leaves the mountaintops, crosses the city, comes indoors, and lands on people, and nobody is consumed, and nobody backs away. The fire came to the people, and the people held still, and then the people spoke.",
    "id": "ch09-p104"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Twice before, God had put His hand on a human mouth right before He sent it out to speak. Once on a shepherd who said he was heavy of tongue. Once on a prophet who said he was a man of unclean lips, and a burning coal came off an altar and touched them.",
    "id": "ch09-p105"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Tonight the fire does not come for one mouth.",
    "id": "ch09-p106"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the shape the fire chose is its own sermon. Of every form flame can take, it arrives as tongues. The God who once stood in a bush saying, “I will be with your mouth,” has kept His word: the fire of the Presence, resting on the organ of speech, and every mouth in the room opening at once.",
    "id": "ch09-p107"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The wind arrived first. A house filling with breath, and then fire landing on mouths. Breath, and then speech.",
    "id": "ch09-p108"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Mouth to mouth, with the doors open.",
    "id": "ch09-p109"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And a wish that has been hanging in the air since the wilderness finally comes down. It was flung into the sky over a camp by the one man who had the access everyone else lacked, and for fifteen centuries God neither granted it nor refused it: “Would that all the LORD’s people were prophets, that the LORD would put His Spirit upon them!” On this morning, in this house, God reaches up and takes the wish down off the hook.",
    "id": "ch09-p110"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "All of them, prophesying. Sons and daughters, the sermon will say within the hour, young and old, servants and women: the Spirit poured out on all flesh.",
    "id": "ch09-p111"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Moses asked, and heaven said nothing, and the silence outlived empires. Then God granted the request to the letter, in one morning, at a scale Moses could not have dreamed. Some prayers are not denied. They are scheduled.",
    "id": "ch09-p112"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the promise that reaches furthest into Pentecost was written centuries before it — in Ezekiel, by the God whose glory once backed out of a temple eastward while nobody called. Here is how He described the day the hiding would end.",
    "id": "ch09-p113"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "“and I will not hide My face from them any longer, for I will have poured out My Spirit on the house of Israel,” declares the Lord GOD."
    ],
    "ref": "Ezekiel 39:29",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Not hide My face any longer, for I will have poured out My Spirit. By God’s own grammar, the Spirit poured out and the hiding ended are the same event. The Spirit is how the face comes back.",
    "id": "ch09-p114"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Zechariah says it in the same order, from the far end of the exile. <em>I will pour out on the house of David the Spirit of grace and of supplication, and they will look on Me whom they have pierced.</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p115"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Pour out the Spirit. Then they look.",
    "id": "ch09-p116"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So Pentecost is <em>hester panim</em> ending: the return date finally arrived, the answer arriving for every psalm so far that cried, “How long will You hide Your face?”",
    "id": "ch09-p117"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "David felt the connection a thousand years early. In the wreckage of his worst sin he had already asked for the opposite, two verses up: hide Your face from my sins. It is the only prayer in Scripture that asks God to hide it. Hide it from what I did. Then, in the next breath, do not hide it from me. He begged for two things at once: “do not cast me away from Your face, and do not take Your Holy Spirit from me.” He could not have explained the theology, but his terror knew: to lose the Spirit and to lose the face are the same loss.",
    "id": "ch09-p118"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And his terror had a name.",
    "id": "ch09-p119"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Years before any of this, a prophet had told King Saul that God had gone looking for a man after His own heart and had found one. Saul heard that sentence long before he ever met the boy it was about. Then the oil ran down David’s head and the Spirit rushed on him, and in the very next line of the story the Spirit left Saul. The two sentences sit against each other on the page.",
    "id": "ch09-p120"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then the court sent for the boy with the harp.",
    "id": "ch09-p121"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Again and again David went in and played for a man God had walked out of, near enough to watch what it was doing to him.",
    "id": "ch09-p122"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So when he begs, do not take Your Holy Spirit from me, he is not reaching for a doctrine. He is asking not to become the man he used to play for.",
    "id": "ch09-p123"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Pentecost answers both halves of that prayer at once, for the whole people of God.",
    "id": "ch09-p124"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This is the sunrise all the dawn-seeking was for.",
    "id": "ch09-p125"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the preacher is Peter. Heaven had its pick of the whole company, and it gave the first sermon in church history to the man from the two fires.",
    "id": "ch09-p126"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Standing in front of the crowd, he quotes David. He opens at the line where the man who danced the ark up the street says he kept the Lord in front of him always, and Peter’s word for in front of him is <em>enopion</em>, built out of the Greek for eye. Before my face.",
    "id": "ch09-p127"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And earlier in the same psalm, David had already named what he was holding: <em>the LORD is the portion of my inheritance and my cup.</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p128"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Three verses on, still inside the same psalm: “You have made known to me the ways of life; You will make me full of gladness with Your presence.” Your presence. The Greek word Peter says is <em>prosopon</em>. Face. You will make me full of gladness with Your face. The first sermon in Christian history has the face of God in it, preached seven weeks after its preacher swore to the enemies of that face that he had never met the man.",
    "id": "ch09-p129"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And a few days later, on the temple steps, he says it again: “repent and return, so that times of refreshing may come from the face of the Lord.” Refreshing, from the face. For Peter, that word is autobiography before it is theology. He is the one man in Jerusalem who can tell you what it does to a ruined man when that face turns toward him holding breakfast instead of a verdict. He is not reciting a doctrine. He is describing a beach.",
    "id": "ch09-p130"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The church’s first theology of the face was preached by its most famous face-denier.",
    "id": "ch09-p131"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the man who writes the last word on all of this got it by going blind.",
    "id": "ch09-p132"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Saul is on the Damascus road with arrest warrants in his bag when a light puts him in the dirt. Not a vision he watched. A light that took his eyes. He gets up unable to see, and stays that way three days, eating nothing, in a stranger’s house, in the city he came to make arrests in.",
    "id": "ch09-p133"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then a man he has never met puts hands on him, and something like scales falls away, and he can see.",
    "id": "ch09-p134"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Eyes opened.",
    "id": "ch09-p135"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He spends the rest of his life writing about the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Christ. It sounds like poetry. He was describing the thing that took his eyesight.",
    "id": "ch09-p136"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The veil had one more thing under it, and it took Paul to say what it was. He says it in a letter, in a Greek word that means fading away: the glory of Moses’s face was fading away, and the veil was there so that Israel would not watch it end.",
    "id": "ch09-p137"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was always going to end, and God meant it to. What Moses carried down that mountain was the shadow the real thing throws ahead of itself.",
    "id": "ch09-p138"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then Paul turns from what faded to what does not. The veil had a schedule: on among the people, off whenever Moses went in before the LORD. Paul takes that picture and throws the doors open: now we all, he writes — all of us, with unveiled face — behold the glory of the Lord. Not one shepherd on one mountain. We all.",
    "id": "ch09-p139"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Whenever a person turns to the Lord, the veil is taken away. Turns: the same motion we have watched since a shepherd turned aside toward a burning bush. The believer lives permanently in Moses’s went-in position: face bare, turned toward glory, all the time.",
    "id": "ch09-p140"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And what happens to a face that stays there? It changes. The Greek is a word you have seen on a mountain: <em>metamorphoo</em> — the transfiguration word. Four uses in the whole New Testament. Two of them describe His face shining like the sun on the summit. This is the third, and it is aimed at you. And run the direction of it: from glory to glory, increasing. Moses’s shine ran downhill and faded beneath the veil; yours is designed to climb.",
    "id": "ch09-p141"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Beholding as in a mirror, Paul says, and he knows his audience: he mailed this letter to Corinth, a city famous for its bronze mirrors. Your seeing now is bronze-mirror seeing, real but dim, a true face in soft focus. Dim is not nothing; dim is how every sunrise starts.",
    "id": "ch09-p142"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the wilderness has its own mirror hidden in the story, in a single verse about a washbasin.",
    "id": "ch09-p143"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Moreover, he made the laver of bronze with its base of bronze, from the mirrors of the serving women who served at the doorway of the tent of meeting."
    ],
    "ref": "Exodus 38:8",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The women gave up the instrument for inspecting their own faces, and it was melted down and remade into the place where those who approached God washed. The thing you once used to study your own reflection becomes part of the way in.",
    "id": "ch09-p144"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Paul does something with one more piece of that furniture, and nobody before him had the standing to do it.",
    "id": "ch09-p145"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Deep in the building, behind the curtain, sat a lid on a box. Two cherubim bent over it, faces turned toward each other, and blood on it one day a year. It was the only address God ever gave for meeting a human being. <em>There I will meet with you, from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubim.</em>",
    "id": "ch09-p146"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One at the head. One at the feet.",
    "id": "ch09-p147"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the dark that morning, a woman bent down and looked straight into that shape, and all she saw was where a body used to be.",
    "id": "ch09-p148"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Sakak</em> is what those cherubim are doing over that lid. Covering. Screening. Spreading something protective overhead. It is the same verb Exodus uses for a palm laid over a crack in a mountain while the glory went past. The shelter and the meeting place were always the same word.",
    "id": "ch09-p149"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Greek Bible calls that lid the <em>hilasterion</em>. One man saw it, one day a year, in the dark.",
    "id": "ch09-p150"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And when Paul reaches for a word to say what God did with His Son, that is the word he takes. <em>God displayed Him publicly as a hilasterion.</em> And the verb under <em>displayed publicly</em> is the one the Greek Old Testament uses for laying out the bread of the face.",
    "id": "ch09-p151"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He did not send us in behind the curtain.",
    "id": "ch09-p152"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He carried the meeting place out into the daylight and set it down where anyone could walk up to it.",
    "id": "ch09-p153"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then Paul reaches the summit, and to get there he has to go back to the first page of the Bible, to the first darkness.",
    "id": "ch09-p154"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "For God, who said, “Light shall shine out of darkness,” is the One who has shone in our hearts to give the Light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Christ."
    ],
    "ref": "2 Corinthians 4:6",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch09-v10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This book opened over black water: darkness on the deep, the Spirit of God hovering over the waters, light about to be spoken into the dark.",
    "id": "ch09-p155"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Paul stands inside that verse and announces: it has happened again. The same God, the same “Let there be light,” a second creation morning.",
    "id": "ch09-p156"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Only this time, the darkness He spoke into was not over an ocean.",
    "id": "ch09-p157"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It was inside us.",
    "id": "ch09-p158"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And when the light came, it did not remain an idea.",
    "id": "ch09-p159"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It had a face.",
    "id": "ch09-p160"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first creation began when light touched the face of the waters. The new creation begins when the face of Christ dawns over the darkness inside a person, one person at a time. He starts where He has always started: hovering over a deep, saying light.",
    "id": "ch09-p161"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the Bible has one more scene with hiding in it. It is the last one.",
    "id": "ch09-p162"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At the end of all things, John sees the sky peeled back and every category of human being diving for cover: kings and commanders, the rich and the strong, every slave and every free man.",
    "id": "ch09-p163"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And they call up to the mountains: “Fall on us, and hide us from the face of Him who sits on the throne.” Rev 6:16. They do not beg to be hidden from the armies of heaven, or the trumpet, or the throne itself. They beg to be hidden from the face. It is Eden playing one final time, at the scale of the species: the instinct that once pressed two people into the trees now pressing the whole race into the rocks, still begging geology to do what the fig leaves could not.",
    "id": "ch09-p164"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Scripture writes the end of that road in one phrase: <em>eternal destruction, away from the face of the Lord</em>. Not fire first, not chains first. Away from the face. That is the loss all the other losses are made of.",
    "id": "ch09-p165"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two crowds, then, at the end of the Bible. One is hiding in the rocks, begging not to be looked at. One has been running toward the look since a dinner table in Emmaus. And it is the same face.",
    "id": "ch09-p166"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>I never knew you.</em> Set against the last line the Torah ever wrote about Moses. Whom the LORD knew face to face.",
    "id": "ch09-p167"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Whom the LORD knew.",
    "id": "ch09-p168"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I never knew you.",
    "id": "ch09-p169"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every name in history will stand under one of them.",
    "id": "ch09-p170"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "num": 10,
  "id": "ch10",
  "title": "Face to Face",
  "hook": "Every veil in this book was hung to hide a face. The last one is hung to promise one, and it does not tear — it lifts.",
  "glossary": [
   {
    "term": "prosagoge",
    "gloss": "Through Him, writes Paul, we have our prosagoge — our access, our being brought in — ushered past every guard, into the royal presence by the King’s own Son."
   },
   {
    "term": "parrhesia",
    "gloss": "And Hebrews adds the credential no empire ever issued to anyone: parrhesia, the right to speak freely at the throne."
   },
   {
    "term": "prosopon pros prosopon",
    "gloss": "And Paul’s phrase for what waits behind it, prosopon pros prosopon, is Moses’s panim el panim in another language."
   }
  ],
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch10-morning"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is the last night of Jesus’ life, and Philip has been listening to Him talk about the Father all through supper. Something in Philip finally runs out of patience with hints.",
    "id": "ch10-p1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "“Lord,” he says, “show us the Father, and it is enough for us.”",
    "id": "ch10-p2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Philip is asking Moses’s question, centuries on, wearing a Galilean accent: “show me Your glory.” The request that once got a man tucked into a cleft with a hand over the gap, told that he could not see God’s face and live. Philip has no idea whose words he is carrying.",
    "id": "ch10-p3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is asking for what the human race has been asking for ever since. Show us. Just let us see. It would be enough.",
    "id": "ch10-p4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Philip has been near this from nearly the beginning.",
    "id": "ch10-p5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The first words out of Jesus’ mouth in John’s Gospel are a question. Two men start following Him down a road, and He turns around on them.",
    "id": "ch10-p6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>What do you seek?</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They have no answer ready, so He hands them one.",
    "id": "ch10-p8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Come, and you will see.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The next morning He found Philip.",
    "id": "ch10-p10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seek, and see. Those are the only two verbs. He opened His mouth for the first time in that Gospel and said both of them — and three years later the man He called the following day is at the table, still asking to be shown.",
    "id": "ch10-p11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He asks it one more time before that Gospel is finished. On the first morning of the new world, in a garden, of a woman who could not see Him through her own crying.",
    "id": "ch10-p12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Whom are you seeking?</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The same verb at both ends. Only the object moved.",
    "id": "ch10-p14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What — and then Whom.",
    "id": "ch10-p15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And this time, for the first time in the history of the request, the answer is not a cleft in a rock. The answer has been sitting at the table all evening.",
    "id": "ch10-p16"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Jesus said to him, “Have I been so long with you, and yet you have not come to know Me, Philip? He who has seen Me has seen the Father; how do you say, ‘Show us the Father’?”"
    ],
    "ref": "John 14:9",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v1"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>He who has seen Me has seen the Father.</em> Philip is asking for the face that is holding the bread. Three years of dusty roads and shared dinners and boat crossings, and the whole time, the face Moses was shielded from has been within arm’s reach, learning Philip’s name, laughing at his jokes, falling asleep in his boat.",
    "id": "ch10-p17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What changed between Sinai and this table was not the glory. The glory did not get smaller, and it did not get safer.",
    "id": "ch10-p18"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It got near.",
    "id": "ch10-p19"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "On the mountain, the face was more than a life could carry. At the table, the face came into range: it took on eyes that could be met, a voice with an accent, a gaze a fisherman could meet without dying. The answer to all those centuries of show us turns out to be: you have been looking at Him all through dinner.",
    "id": "ch10-p20"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Earlier that evening, before any of this, He had told them why they were all in the room. <em>I have earnestly desired to eat this Passover with you before I suffer.</em> The Greek doubles the verb the way Hebrew does when it means it. With desire I have desired.",
    "id": "ch10-p21"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The last time a verb doubled like that, it was God saying <em>I will surely hide.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p22"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And before the table breaks up, Jesus makes a promise with a strange verb in it.",
    "id": "ch10-p23"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "He who has My commandments and keeps them is the one who loves Me; and he who loves Me will be loved by My Father, and I will love him and will disclose Myself to him."
    ],
    "ref": "John 14:21",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v2"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Disclose. Manifest. Let Myself be seen.",
    "id": "ch10-p24"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is Moses’s word. In the Greek Old Testament, the one the New Testament quotes from, the request in the cleft comes out as a single verb: <em>manifest Yourself to me.</em> And that is the verb Jesus just used, at supper, with bread on the table.",
    "id": "ch10-p25"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And somebody at that table catches what He just did. Not Judas. He has already gone out into the night. The other Judas. This is the only time he ever speaks.",
    "id": "ch10-p26"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Lord, what has happened that You are going to disclose Yourself to us, and not to the world?",
    "id": "ch10-p27"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He asks it in Moses’s words. There is no sign he knows.",
    "id": "ch10-p28"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Why us. Why not everybody.",
    "id": "ch10-p29"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Somebody finally asks it out loud, in a room, to His face. And Jesus does not take the question back.",
    "id": "ch10-p30"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "If anyone loves Me, he will keep My word; and My Father will love him, and We will come to him and make Our abode with him."
    ],
    "ref": "John 14:23",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v3"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Minutes earlier in the same speech He had said there were many rooms in His Father’s house, and He was going to prepare one. It is the same word. The room He is getting ready for you and the room He makes in you — that word turns up twice in the whole New Testament, and both times are that night.",
    "id": "ch10-p31"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Anyone. Not the eleven. Anyone.",
    "id": "ch10-p32"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is talking about everyone who would come after.",
    "id": "ch10-p33"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "People who will never share His table. Never know the small music of His voice, the accent that would have told them, before He even turned, who it was.",
    "id": "ch10-p34"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Never watch His face turn toward them.",
    "id": "ch10-p35"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He is talking about you.",
    "id": "ch10-p36"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the letter to the Hebrews — the one that traces His priesthood back past Aaron to a king with no genealogy — finishes the thought from the other direction, with what is happening right now, this minute, while you are hearing this sentence: <em>Christ entered heaven itself, now to appear in the presence of God for us.</em> The Greek under presence is the word you know by heart now. The face. He appears before the face of God, for us. It runs in both directions at once. Toward us, He shows the Father’s face. Toward the Father, He stands before the face with ours. One person, facing both ways, holding the two ends of the oldest separation there is together in Himself until there is no separation left.",
    "id": "ch10-p37"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At Sinai, the middleman was a concession to fear: put a man in the middle so we do not have to stand there. The Middleman who is there now is the opposite idea, God’s own idea: not a buffer between the faces, but the place where they finally meet.",
    "id": "ch10-p38"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That opens a door that has been shut since a Persian throne room: the empire where seven men out of millions had the right to see the king’s face, a title worth killing over, a door that ate the unsummoned.",
    "id": "ch10-p39"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The New Testament walks into that throne room and starts handing out credentials to fishermen and widows and runaway slaves. Through Him, writes Paul, we have our <em>prosagoge</em> — our access, our being brought in — ushered past every guard, into the royal presence by the King’s own Son.",
    "id": "ch10-p40"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Hebrews adds the credential no empire ever issued to anyone: <em>parrhesia</em>, the right to speak freely at the throne. “Draw near with confidence,” it says. Esther fasted three days before she tried that door. You are told to walk that corridor like a child who has never once had to knock on the door of her father’s study, because the door has never been shut.",
    "id": "ch10-p41"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The rank empires killed men to possess is now the birthright of every believer alive. The scepter is not extended once, dramatically, at the last moment.",
    "id": "ch10-p42"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is never lowered again.",
    "id": "ch10-p43"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Something is missing from that throne room, and it went missing in the tabernacle first.",
    "id": "ch10-p44"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "God specified that building down to the rings and the poles — the ark, the table, the lampstand, the altars, the basin. He never ordered a place for a priest to sit. They stood. Hebrews says why: <em>every priest stands daily ministering and offering time after time the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p45"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The work was never finished, so nobody ever sat down.",
    "id": "ch10-p46"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then one sentence, about one priest: <em>He, having offered one sacrifice for sins for all time, sat down at the right hand of God.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p47"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the psalm that named that priesthood had opened with it a thousand years earlier, three lines before it ever got to Melchizedek — the man who walked out of nowhere to meet Abram, carrying bread and wine, and whose city was called Peace.",
    "id": "ch10-p48"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Sit at My right hand.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p49"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That seat is His, and Hebrews will not let anyone else near it. The first priest in the Bible arrived with bread and wine in his hands. <em>To which of the angels has He ever said, \"Sit at My right hand\"?</em> The question is asked to be left unanswered. Nobody in the universe has ever been told to sit there but the Son.",
    "id": "ch10-p50"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He sat down, and that was the end of the standing.",
    "id": "ch10-p51"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Except that one man saw Him do it.",
    "id": "ch10-p52"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man who waited tables, whose face went bright in front of the council that voted to kill him. Before a single stone was thrown, he told the room what he could see.",
    "id": "ch10-p53"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Behold, I see the heavens opened and the Son of Man standing at the right hand of God."
    ],
    "ref": "Acts 7:56",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v4"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They covered their ears and rushed him for it.",
    "id": "ch10-p54"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody ever told that man why his face was shining. It was shining because of what he was looking at.",
    "id": "ch10-p55"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He sat down because the work was finished. He stood up because somebody He loved was about to die alone.",
    "id": "ch10-p56"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The sitting is about the work. The standing is about the person.",
    "id": "ch10-p57"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "But the throne He sits on, He does not keep to Himself.",
    "id": "ch10-p58"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "On the last night, at that same table, He turned to eleven men who were hours from failing Him and made them a promise in two halves: <em>you may eat and drink at My table in My kingdom, and you will sit on thrones.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p59"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A table and a seat. He said it with the bread still in His hands.",
    "id": "ch10-p60"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And there was one more thing in His hands that night.",
    "id": "ch10-p61"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "This cup is the new covenant in My blood."
    ],
    "ref": "Luke 22:20",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v5"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hours later, in a garden, He will ask for a cup to be taken away from Him. This one He does not ask about. He holds it out.",
    "id": "ch10-p62"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The cup He could not put down is the reason there is one He can hand you.",
    "id": "ch10-p63"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Absalom lived two years in his father’s city and never once sat at his father’s table.",
    "id": "ch10-p64"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And those two men stood in the same valley.",
    "id": "ch10-p65"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Abram met the priest with the bread and the wine in a place called the King’s Valley. Absalom raised his pillar in the King’s Valley, because, he said, <em>I have no son to preserve my name.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p66"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One of them went there to put a blessing on another man’s name. The other went there to cut his own into a rock, because there was nobody left to carry it.",
    "id": "ch10-p67"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You have a chair.",
    "id": "ch10-p68"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "At Sinai the question stopped being Can a human being see God? and became Will a human being stand there?",
    "id": "ch10-p69"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And what happens to a person who actually uses that access? Paul answers in one sentence, written in the present tense about everyone who turns:",
    "id": "ch10-p70"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "But we all, with unveiled face, beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, are being transformed into the same image from glory to glory, just as from the Lord, the Spirit."
    ],
    "ref": "2 Corinthians 3:18",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v6"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "We all. With unveiled face.",
    "id": "ch10-p71"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Right now, at whatever kitchen table or highway or dark bedroom this finds you in, that is your position: face bare, turned toward glory, standing where Moses once stood when he went in. And you do not have to leave.",
    "id": "ch10-p72"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You do not have to feel it for it to be true. Moses could not feel his own shine either. And you may be hearing this at the end of a day you are ashamed of. The verse does not say we all, except. It says we all.",
    "id": "ch10-p73"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the beholding is not sightseeing. You become what you look at. That has been true since the nursery, since you were days old and searching for one face above your crib. Behold, and be changed, glory to glory, brighter as it goes. The shine that faded off one man’s face is now the direction of yours.",
    "id": "ch10-p74"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Ten chapters, and every one of them has been about our ache: to see His face.",
    "id": "ch10-p75"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In the Song of Songs, the great love poem the rabbis called the holy of holies of Scripture, a young woman has hidden herself away, up in the rocks, tucked into the crevices of a cliff, hiding the way people have hidden since two faces pressed into the leaves.",
    "id": "ch10-p76"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And her Lover has climbed after her. On one side of the stone: a face turned away, hiding. On the other side, inches away: a face pressed toward the gap, seeking.",
    "id": "ch10-p77"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then He speaks. He could demand surrender. He could demand an apology, an account of all the hiding.",
    "id": "ch10-p78"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "My dove is hiding behind the rocks, behind an outcrop on the cliff. Let me see your face; let me hear your voice. For your voice is pleasant, and your face is lovely."
    ],
    "ref": "Song of Songs 2:14",
    "translation": "NLT",
    "id": "ch10-v7"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Let me see your face.</em> He is asking for yours. All this way, us seeking His face, and He has been seeking ours. While we were rehearsing our approaches, He was standing outside our hiding places, asking us to come out.",
    "id": "ch10-p79"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This Lover is the one from the lattice. Five verses above this cliff, in the same poem, the rabbis found him pressed against the barrier between himself and those below, eye to the gaps in the lattice, watching the bowed heads of a nation that had asked for distance.",
    "id": "ch10-p80"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Behind the lattice: God enduring a barrier He never wanted, leaning against it, looking through it, refusing to leave. At the cliff: God done with barriers, standing at the mouth of the last hiding place, asking it to open.",
    "id": "ch10-p81"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every curtain between His face and ours, He has outlasted or torn. One remains. The final barrier in the long history of barriers is no longer a temple veil or a nation’s terror. It is the hider’s own answer.",
    "id": "ch10-p82"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that is the one barrier He will not tear. He asks. <em>Let me see your face.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p83"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The dove is hiding in the rocks. In the cracks of a cliff. It is Moses’s address. The crack in the rock was his shelter: the cleft where God tucked a man and covered him with His own hand so the glory would not undo him. And now the Brightness Himself is standing outside the crack in the rock, asking the one hiding inside it to come out.",
    "id": "ch10-p84"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What was once the only safe place to be near the glory has become the last place we hide from it. And then He says why He wants her out. Lovely. Of every word heaven could choose for the face you have hidden in shame, managed in mirrors.",
    "id": "ch10-p85"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The word He chooses is lovely.",
    "id": "ch10-p86"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Your voice is pleasant. Your face is lovely. It is the face He crosses the mountains to look at. Eden’s question and the Song’s request are the same sentence at two temperatures. <em>Where are you?</em> was the winter of it. <em>Let me see your face</em> is the spring.",
    "id": "ch10-p87"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So the ending was never in doubt. Paul says it with a mirror, the bronze kind his Corinthian readers made famous, the kind that gives back a warm blur: <em>now we see in a mirror dimly, but then face to face</em>.",
    "id": "ch10-p88"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A bride comes down an aisle with a veil over her face, and at the threshold of the covenant the veil is lifted back, so the two can look at each other face to face. Almost nobody in the room can tell you why. The custom has outlived the explanation for it. But the veil has a story, and it begins on Moriah.",
    "id": "ch10-p89"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jewish tradition does not call what happened on Moriah the sacrifice of Isaac, because Isaac was not sacrificed. It calls it the <em>Akedah</em>. The Binding. A father bound his son to the altar, and the son — in the tradition’s telling a grown man, strong enough to resist, choosing not to — let himself be bound: two surrenders on one altar, and a voice from heaven closing the distance between the binding and the knife.",
    "id": "ch10-p90"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "For three days Abraham had climbed toward that altar carrying a son he had already surrendered as dead, and the letter to the Hebrews tells you what really happened up there: <em>he received his son back</em>, it says, <em>as good as raised from the dead</em>. The Bible’s first glimpse of resurrection is a son who carried the wood for his own death up a hill, two thousand years before another Son carried another piece of wood up another hill, just outside the same city.",
    "id": "ch10-p91"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And after the substitute had been provided, God sealed the promise with an oath unlike any other in Scripture: <em>He swore by Himself, because there was no one greater to swear by</em>.",
    "id": "ch10-p92"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is the bedrock under all of it. Every covenant hangs on whatever binds it, and every human covenant hangs on something breakable. This one, God fastened to Himself. The only One who could ever break it is the One who bound Himself inside it, which is why it has never broken, and never will. Jacob held on to God all night with two hands, and heaven honored the grip. But your place in this covenant has never hung from your grip.",
    "id": "ch10-p93"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It hangs from His oath.",
    "id": "ch10-p94"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And then the son goes quiet. In the text, Abraham comes down the mountain, and Isaac is never named coming down at all. From the altar on, the offered son simply drops out of view, unmentioned, until the story turns back to him for one thing only: to bring him a bride.",
    "id": "ch10-p95"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "His father will not choose her from the people close at hand. He sends a servant across a far country, to a land the son has never walked, carrying nothing but the father’s wealth and the father’s message, to find a woman who has never once seen the son’s face, and ask her to come home to him.",
    "id": "ch10-p96"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And her name is already waiting in the closing lines of the Binding. On the same page as the altar and the oath, the story pauses to announce a birth in that far country: a girl named Rivka. Rebekah. Her name comes from a root that means to tie fast: to bind, to tether, to hold secure.",
    "id": "ch10-p97"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The chapter of the Binding closes by naming the bride whose name means bound. The covenant God had just tied to Himself so that nothing in heaven or earth could ever break it, and a bride whose name means bound. Scripture lays them on the same page on purpose.",
    "id": "ch10-p98"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The woman who marries into the unbreakable promise is named after the very thing that makes it hold. And she earns the name before she ever sees the groom.",
    "id": "ch10-p99"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The servant finds her at a well and lays the whole impossible thing in front of her: leave your home, your country, everyone you have ever loved, and go to a man in a land you will not come back from, a man you have never laid eyes on. She answers in three words: “I will go.” She binds herself, sight unseen, to a face she has to trust before she can see it.",
    "id": "ch10-p100"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Paul says the quiet part out loud. He writes it to an ordinary little church, in plain daylight: “I promised you to one husband, to present you to Christ.” You are the bride. Spoken for by a Son you have never seen, in a country you have never walked, on the word of a messenger who came a long way to find you.",
    "id": "ch10-p101"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And you said yes before your eyes ever could.",
    "id": "ch10-p102"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Though you have not seen Him, you love Him.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p103"
   },
   {
    "type": "slot",
    "slot": "ch10-veil-lift"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then she arrives. Isaac has gone out into the field toward evening, and Genesis says he lifts up his eyes and sees. Then Rebekah lifts up her eyes and sees. Twice in two verses, at the very moment they meet, the same verb lands on both of them: they see. She looks up from her camel and there he is, Isaac, walking out across the evening field to meet her, the son who came through the Binding alive, walking home from a well called Beer-lahai-roi, the well of the Living One who sees me, the very spring where a runaway slave girl gave God His name.",
    "id": "ch10-p104"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "She said to the servant, “Who is that man walking in the field to meet us?” And the servant said, “He is my master.” Then she took her veil and covered herself."
    ],
    "ref": "Genesis 24:65",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v8"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And in the one moment she is finally free to look at the face she said yes to sight unseen, she takes her veil and covers herself.",
    "id": "ch10-p105"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Not to hide. The dove in the cliff hid her face to keep it from love. This bride covers hers because she has already been given to him.",
    "id": "ch10-p106"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The binding came before the face.",
    "id": "ch10-p107"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A bridal veil is the one covering that exists for the sake of its own removal: worn at the meeting precisely because it is meant to be lifted when the covenant is made. And the bride is not the one who lifts it. No bride lifts her own veil; she wears it until the groom reaches out and raises it himself. Even the unveiling was never hers to do. It was always his. Every other veil was hung to hide a face. This one is hung to promise one. It is hiddenness with a wedding morning inside it.",
    "id": "ch10-p108"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There was one other veil, and it did not lift — it tore. Sixty feet of temple curtain, ripped from the top by a hand no one in the room could reach, the afternoon the true Son died, so that we could come in. Two veils, and the same story underneath both: death, then resurrection. One torn so we could enter, one worn in hope so it could be lifted, and we could be seen. And John saw where it finishes: the holy city descending out of heaven dressed as a bride adorned for her husband: a bride who bound herself to a Groom she loved before she saw Him, wearing the veil that exists to be lifted. Of all the veils that have gone up or come down, the last one does not tear.",
    "id": "ch10-p109"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It lifts.",
    "id": "ch10-p110"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And there is a vow that goes with it. God wrote it out in the middle of the ugliest marriage in the Bible.",
    "id": "ch10-p111"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hosea’s wife had gone after other men. God is the husband in that story, and after the thorns and the wilderness and the speaking upon her heart, He says what He means to do about it. He does not say I will forgive her. He does not say I will take her back.",
    "id": "ch10-p112"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He says I will betroth you.",
    "id": "ch10-p113"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Three times.",
    "id": "ch10-p114"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "I will betroth you to Me forever. I will betroth you to Me in righteousness and justice, in lovingkindness and compassion. I will betroth you to Me in faithfulness."
    ],
    "ref": "Hosea 2:19–20",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v9"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the words at the end of it are the ones everything has been walking toward since the Torah closed on a man God knew face to face.",
    "id": "ch10-p115"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And you shall know the LORD.",
    "id": "ch10-p116"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And a few verses before the betrothal, He asks her for something else. She has been calling Him by a title that means master. He asks her to stop.",
    "id": "ch10-p117"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You will call Me my husband, He says, and you will no longer call Me my master.",
    "id": "ch10-p118"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Of everything God could require at the end of a reconciliation, He asks for a change of name. Not more reverence. Less distance.",
    "id": "ch10-p119"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Israel never let the vow go. To this day, when an observant Jewish man straps on the <em>tefillin</em> in the morning, he winds the leather three times around his middle finger, once for each I will betroth you, and says those words while he does it.",
    "id": "ch10-p120"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A wedding ring. Every weekday, before the day starts.",
    "id": "ch10-p121"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A man once walked away from a river wearing a limp like one. His children have been putting on the real thing every morning for three thousand years, and the words they say while they do it are God’s.",
    "id": "ch10-p122"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And on the far side of every wedding there has ever been, there is a table.",
    "id": "ch10-p123"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Blessed are those who are invited to the marriage supper of the Lamb."
    ],
    "ref": "Revelation 19:9",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v10"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Isaiah had that dinner on the calendar seven hundred years early, and he set three things on one mountain.",
    "id": "ch10-p124"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The LORD of hosts will prepare a lavish banquet for all peoples on this mountain… And on this mountain He will swallow up the covering which is over all peoples, even the veil which is stretched over all nations. He will swallow up death for all time, and the Lord GOD will wipe tears away from all faces."
    ],
    "ref": "Isaiah 25:6–8",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v11"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A feast. A veil destroyed. And every face in the room dried by the hand of God.",
    "id": "ch10-p125"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The covering He swallows is called, word for word, <em>the face of the shroud</em>. This book’s own word, worn by the thing that covers us.",
    "id": "ch10-p126"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He takes the face off the veil. Then He comes around the table and takes the tears off ours.",
    "id": "ch10-p127"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Bible opens with a meal that cost us a face. It ends with an invitation to dinner.",
    "id": "ch10-p128"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A table and a seat. He said it with the bread still in his hands. Of all the faces in the courtyard, it finds the one trying hardest not to be known.",
    "id": "ch10-p129"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And in between, the food keeps turning up.",
    "id": "ch10-p130"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seventy men see God on a mountain, and what Scripture stops to record is that they ate and drank. The first priest in the Bible walks out of nowhere carrying bread and wine. The holy place is built with a table in it and bread kept in front of the face, and it is never once allowed to stand empty. Eyes that closed over food in a garden come open again over bread at a table in Emmaus.",
    "id": "ch10-p131"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And a man who denied Him three times got breakfast before he got a single question.",
    "id": "ch10-p132"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every time the distance closes, somebody is eating.",
    "id": "ch10-p133"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The opposite of a veil was never an open door. It was a table.",
    "id": "ch10-p134"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And Paul’s phrase for what waits behind it, <em>prosopon pros prosopon</em>, is Moses’s <em>panim el panim</em> in another language. Face to face. The old promise, carried across the centuries and opened to everyone.",
    "id": "ch10-p135"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What the Torah could say about only one man in its whole history, Paul mails to an ordinary congregation as their certain future.",
    "id": "ch10-p136"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "For now we see in a mirror dimly, but then face to face; now I know in part, but then I will know fully just as I also have been fully known."
    ],
    "ref": "1 Corinthians 13:12",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v12"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Fully known. Not the language of inspection, but the language the Torah saved for its final word about Moses, when the LORD knew him face to face, the word a drenched fisherman staked his whole restoration on beside a charcoal fire, now promised to you as your own future.",
    "id": "ch10-p137"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A man in the Psalms got there first, in the dark, with nothing to go on.",
    "id": "ch10-p138"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>As for me, I shall behold Your face in righteousness; I shall be satisfied with Your likeness when I awake.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p139"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Psalm 17:15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Wake up, and the first thing is His face, and it is enough. He wrote that a thousand years before there was an empty tomb to hang it on.",
    "id": "ch10-p140"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And John lands the promise softer still:",
    "id": "ch10-p141"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Beloved, now we are children of God, and it has not appeared as yet what we will be. We know that when He appears, we will be like Him, because we will see Him just as He is. And everyone who has this hope fixed on Him purifies himself, just as He is pure."
    ],
    "ref": "1 John 3:2–3",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v13"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two days of laundry at the foot of a mountain. That is what it was for.",
    "id": "ch10-p142"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody scrubs the house for a guest who is not coming.",
    "id": "ch10-p143"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There it is. In the first chapter I gave you a sentence from a hillside: <em>blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God</em>. It has been burning quietly for ten chapters, through the tomb and the trees and the mountains and the fires.",
    "id": "ch10-p144"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "They shall see God. And the seeing will finish us. Not end us; that old warning, “you cannot see Me and live,” has expired with every other barrier.",
    "id": "ch10-p145"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seeing Him completes us into His likeness. Finish us, the way a sculptor finishes a figure.",
    "id": "ch10-p146"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the name. The blessing has been moving toward this moment since the wilderness. In the wilderness, the name of God rested on exactly one forehead in all the world: a plate of pure gold on the high priest’s turban, <em>Holy to the LORD</em>, one man wearing it on behalf of everyone.",
    "id": "ch10-p147"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In Ezekiel’s Jerusalem it widened to a remnant: a mark set on the foreheads of all who grieved over the ruin. Stage by stage, age by age, the name has been working its way toward every face, exactly as the blessing said it would: <em>so shall they put My name on the sons of Israel</em>.",
    "id": "ch10-p148"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And on the Bible’s last page, in its second-to-last scene, the blessing reaches everyone. His bond-servants will serve Him, John writes of the city where the story ends. And with that sentence the old distance between servant and king disappears. In Persia, seven aristocrats saw the face while every servant served at a distance. In this kingdom, the servants are the ones who see the face.",
    "id": "ch10-p149"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then John writes the last engraving in the Bible: <em>His name will be on their foreheads</em>. The blessing, landed. Three thousand years of priests’ hands raised over bowed heads, and every syllable of it was aimed here: the name once carried by one man on behalf of everyone, resting at last above every set of eyes that ever watched for the dawn.",
    "id": "ch10-p150"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Bind these words on your hand, Moses told them. And wear them between your eyes.",
    "id": "ch10-p151"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every morning since, in houses all over the world, a man has strapped a small box to his forehead and stood there with the words pressed to the front of his skull.",
    "id": "ch10-p152"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody ever told him he was standing in the one place God had already picked out.",
    "id": "ch10-p153"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And there is a second name, and it never goes on a forehead.",
    "id": "ch10-p154"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "In one of the letters to the churches, He promises the one who overcomes some of the hidden manna, and a white stone, and something written on it.",
    "id": "ch10-p155"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "a new name written on the stone which no one knows except the one who receives it."
    ],
    "ref": "Revelation 2:17",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v14"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hidden manna. The jar that stood in front of His face in the wilderness, then went into the dark behind the curtain. He hands it out.",
    "id": "ch10-p156"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And a name no one else will ever read. Not the one raised over crowds by priests with their hands up. One name, for one person, said in private.",
    "id": "ch10-p157"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He writes His name on you where anyone can see it. Then He gives you one that is only His to say.",
    "id": "ch10-p158"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Shiloh. Before there is a temple, before there is a king, there is a tent, and a woman standing in front of it with her mouth moving and no sound coming out.",
    "id": "ch10-p159"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She has wanted a child for years. She has stopped eating.",
    "id": "ch10-p160"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the Hebrew for where she is standing is <em>lifnei YHWH</em>. Before the face of the LORD.",
    "id": "ch10-p161"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The priest on duty is Eli. He is the man whose whole office is turning God’s face toward people.",
    "id": "ch10-p162"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He watches her lips. He decides she is drunk. He tells her to put the wine away.",
    "id": "ch10-p163"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He never looks at her face.",
    "id": "ch10-p164"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She tells him she has been pouring out her soul. And he blesses her anyway, and the last thing he says to her is the last word of the blessing. <em>Go in peace.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p165"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And she answers him with the middle one. <em>Let your servant find chen</em> <em>in your eyes.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p166"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the sentence that ends the scene.",
    "id": "ch10-p167"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>And the woman went her way and ate, and her face was no longer sad.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p168"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Face is <em>panim</em>. The same word as God’s. And under the English there is no word for <em>sad</em> at all. It says her face was no longer hers.",
    "id": "ch10-p169"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nothing had changed. She was still childless walking out that door. And she ate.",
    "id": "ch10-p170"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two chapters later the book mentions Eli’s eyes. They had begun to grow dim, it says. He could not see.",
    "id": "ch10-p171"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The word is <em>ra'ah</em>.",
    "id": "ch10-p172"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is one word in that blessing I have never been able to say evenly.",
    "id": "ch10-p173"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The middle verse asks two things: that His face would shine on you, and that He would be gracious to you. In Hebrew the second is one word. Its root means grace, and the picture inside it is stooping: bending down to somebody who cannot come up to you.",
    "id": "ch10-p174"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It is my sister’s name.",
    "id": "ch10-p175"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Hannah.",
    "id": "ch10-p176"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She spent twenty years bending down to people, and nobody ever told her it was her own name.",
    "id": "ch10-p177"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody ever told the woman at the tent either. She stood in front of the face of God and asked for the one thing she was already called.",
    "id": "ch10-p178"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One afternoon I took her to the aquarium in Denver.",
    "id": "ch10-p179"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "She had always loved Dory, the blue fish in Finding Nemo who cannot hold on to anything and keeps going anyway. So we went looking until we found one, and my sister stood at the glass in a dark room full of strangers and sang <em>just keep swimming</em> out loud, and made Nemo jokes the rest of the afternoon.",
    "id": "ch10-p180"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "There is a moment late in that film I did not think about for years. Dory is begging her friend not to leave her, and she is reciting an address at him to prove she can still remember it, and what she finally gets out is this: <em>when I look at you, I can feel it. And I look at you, and I'm home.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p181"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A fish who cannot keep anything keeps it while she is looking at a face. And the word she reaches for is home.",
    "id": "ch10-p182"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That was the last day I saw her face.",
    "id": "ch10-p183"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And that is the afternoon God keeps bringing me back to. Of everything I have of her, He picks that one. When He brings me to His face, He takes me to the bad lighting and the tank and my sister singing a cartoon song in public, not caring who heard her.",
    "id": "ch10-p184"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I used to think being near God would feel like the mountain.",
    "id": "ch10-p185"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "It keeps feeling like that afternoon.",
    "id": "ch10-p186"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the sentence standing right beside that engraving closes the other long account: the city has no need of lamp or sun, because the Lord God will illumine them. Every borrowed beam — the shine that faded off one man’s face, the glory that backed out of a building — has finally come home to its source. A whole city, lit the way Moses was lit, with no veil and no fading, because the Light is not visiting anymore.",
    "id": "ch10-p187"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The Old Testament had ended five verses from its last page still waiting on a sunrise. A sun of righteousness, the prophet called it, rising with healing in its wings. The city at the end of the Bible does not need one.",
    "id": "ch10-p188"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He lives there.",
    "id": "ch10-p189"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And on that same page, standing in the middle of the open street, is the tree.",
    "id": "ch10-p190"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nobody is guarding it. There is no sword. The cherubim came off the curtain one Friday afternoon and were never posted again.",
    "id": "ch10-p191"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The way back to the tree and the sight of His face turn out to be the same address.",
    "id": "ch10-p192"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "That is the other crowd, the one that ran toward the look. Faces bare. Name in place. Nothing left in the middle.",
    "id": "ch10-p193"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man who wrote the last words of the Bible was at that supper table, leaning against Jesus.",
    "id": "ch10-p194"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He got old writing letters. And twice he ends one the same way: I have much to write to you, and I will not do it with paper and ink. I want to come and speak with you instead.",
    "id": "ch10-p195"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "<em>Stoma pros stoma</em>, he writes. Mouth to mouth.",
    "id": "ch10-p196"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The phrase God used about Moses on the mountain, spent by an old man who only wanted to see his friends’ faces.",
    "id": "ch10-p197"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He was exiled to a bare island in the sea. And there, at the far end of his own long road, he saw it again. <em>His face was like the sun shining in its strength.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p198"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The man who had once laid his head on that chest fell at His feet like a dead man.",
    "id": "ch10-p199"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then the hand came down on him. <em>Do not be afraid.</em>",
    "id": "ch10-p200"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And he was the one shown how the story ends.",
    "id": "ch10-p201"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Silver in a tomb, wearing a prayer about a shining face.",
    "id": "ch10-p202"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A spring in the desert, and a slave girl giving Him the name: the One who sees me.",
    "id": "ch10-p203"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Two faces pressed into the leaves in the evening, and a voice asking where.",
    "id": "ch10-p204"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A face fallen over an offering, and a man who walked out into a field rather than let it be lifted.",
    "id": "ch10-p205"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A prophet sailing west, and a fish appointed before the storm ever rose.",
    "id": "ch10-p206"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A man in ashes, refusing every answer his friends brought him, asking God why the face was turned away.",
    "id": "ch10-p207"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A song that goes down into the dark and never comes back up, kept anyway, and set to music.",
    "id": "ch10-p208"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A brother’s face, dreaded for twenty years, wet with tears at sunrise.",
    "id": "ch10-p209"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A river in the dark, and a grip that would not quit, and a limp like a wedding ring.",
    "id": "ch10-p210"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A bush that would not burn out, and a man hiding his face from the fire that made him.",
    "id": "ch10-p211"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A mountain on fire, a nation stepping backward, a God saying if only.",
    "id": "ch10-p212"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Seventy elders on that same mountain, seeing God and passing the food.",
    "id": "ch10-p213"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A cloud at a tent door, and a man inside it spoken to the way a man speaks to his friend.",
    "id": "ch10-p214"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A hand over a cleft while the glory went by.",
    "id": "ch10-p215"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A shine on a face that could not feel it, fading under a veil.",
    "id": "ch10-p216"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A lattice of priests’ fingers, with the Lover’s eye at the gaps.",
    "id": "ch10-p217"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A king out of his robes in the street, dancing at the face of God.",
    "id": "ch10-p218"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Glory backing out of its own house, still facing the people, waiting to be called after.",
    "id": "ch10-p219"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A cloak pulled over a face in the mouth of a cave, for a whisper.",
    "id": "ch10-p220"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The two who had covered their faces, back on a mountain, with nothing over them now.",
    "id": "ch10-p221"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A face set like flint, wet on a ridge above Jerusalem.",
    "id": "ch10-p222"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Spit. A blindfold. A curtain torn from the top.",
    "id": "ch10-p223"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A look across a courtyard, and a rooster, and a man undone.",
    "id": "ch10-p224"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A woman in a garden at first light, who did not know His face until He said her name.",
    "id": "ch10-p225"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Eyes opened over broken bread, and a run back through the dark.",
    "id": "ch10-p226"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A charcoal fire rebuilt on a beach, and the smell of the denial becoming breakfast.",
    "id": "ch10-p227"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Fire again, in the shape of tongues, on everyone.",
    "id": "ch10-p228"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A road to Damascus, a light that took a man’s eyes, and the rest of his life spent writing about a face.",
    "id": "ch10-p229"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And now the question comes back to you.",
    "id": "ch10-p230"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Where are you hiding — and who told you that was safer?",
    "id": "ch10-p231"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "What face have you been rehearsing for, all these years, instead of His?",
    "id": "ch10-p232"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And what would change, starting tonight, if you believed that He is not enduring your gaze — He is asking for it?",
    "id": "ch10-p233"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And He had done it once before, in the same poem the dove came out of.",
    "id": "ch10-p234"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Five verses past the cliff, she is inside and asleep, and there is a sound at the door.",
    "id": "ch10-p235"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Listen! My beloved is knocking: “Open to me, my treasure, my darling, my dove, my perfect one.”"
    ],
    "ref": "Song of Songs 5:2",
    "translation": "NLT",
    "id": "ch10-v15"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Same poem. Same dove. He asked to see her face in chapter two, and by chapter five He is standing at her door asking her to open it.",
    "id": "ch10-p236"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "The last invitation in the Bible is not a summons.",
    "id": "ch10-p237"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "Behold, I stand at the door and knock; if anyone hears My voice and opens the door, I will come in to him and will dine with him, and he with Me."
    ],
    "ref": "Revelation 3:20",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v16"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "A door left closed and never locked, and the One outside it asking to be let in to dinner.",
    "id": "ch10-p238"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the sentence directly after it is the one Absalom never got.",
    "id": "ch10-p239"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "He who overcomes, I will grant to him to sit down with Me on My throne."
    ],
    "ref": "Revelation 3:21",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v17"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "He knocks to be let in for a meal. And once He is in, He gives away the chair He is sitting on.",
    "id": "ch10-p240"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And what follows is only a road. Walk it in your own voice. His ear is tuned to yours.",
    "id": "ch10-p241"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Father, I have been hiding.",
    "id": "ch10-p242",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And I have been calling it other things. Busy. Tired. Waiting. Not ready.",
    "id": "ch10-p243",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You have heard me answer every question except the one You keep asking.",
    "id": "ch10-p244",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I have known what You wanted for a long time now. It was never complicated. You wanted my face.",
    "id": "ch10-p245",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And You never turned Yours away. I turned mine. I know that now.",
    "id": "ch10-p246",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So here I am.",
    "id": "ch10-p247",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "This is my face — the one I manage, the one I arrange, the one I have kept from You. The one You call lovely.",
    "id": "ch10-p248",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Song of Songs 2:14",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And I am only now seeing what the hiding cost. All those years I called it safe, You were standing there with Your hands full.",
    "id": "ch10-p249",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And I am not asking You to look past what You will see. I am asking for the harder thing.",
    "id": "ch10-p250",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Create in me a clean heart. Make in me the very thing You keep asking me to bring.",
    "id": "ch10-p251",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Psalm 51:10",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I am done hiding.",
    "id": "ch10-p252",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You are real. Not an idea I inherited. The Living One who sees me.",
    "id": "ch10-p253",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Genesis 16:13",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And I am not praying to You through a curtain. That veil is already torn — top to bottom, and the tearing was Yours. So I am coming all the way in.",
    "id": "ch10-p254",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Matthew 27:51",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nothing in this room is a surprise to You. You saw all of it before I walked in.",
    "id": "ch10-p255",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So whatever I am carrying, I am choosing to look at You.",
    "id": "ch10-p256",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Jesus, You set Your face toward me when I was the cost of it. You saw my worst night, and You came back at dawn and built a fire anyway. What You did for Peter, do for me: walk me back onto the ground where I failed, and give me new lines to say there.",
    "id": "ch10-p257",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Holy Spirit, come.",
    "id": "ch10-p258",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You are how the face comes back — so come. Rest on me the way You rested on every head in that house. Fill this room. Fill me. I am not asking for a feeling. I am asking for You.",
    "id": "ch10-p259",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Acts 2:3",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And I will hold still now, and expect You, because I know how You pursue. You came walking in the evening, calling for the ones in the trees. You sent a storm after a runaway, with the rescue appointed before he ever boarded the ship. You backed out of Your own house still facing the family You were losing. You did not chase my face this far to go quiet the moment I finally look up.",
    "id": "ch10-p260",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And the thing You have been asking me to do — the one I keep pretending not to hear — I will do it. You know the one.",
    "id": "ch10-p261",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Then take this face You asked for and put it to work. The light was never traveling to me; it was traveling through me. Send me to the ones still hiding. Let me be the voice at the edge of somebody else’s trees.",
    "id": "ch10-p262",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I have not seen You. I love You anyway.",
    "id": "ch10-p263",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "1 Peter 1:8",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "So bind me to You — like the bride who bound herself to a face she had never seen, and gave the only answer that matters:",
    "id": "ch10-p264",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "I will go.",
    "id": "ch10-p265",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "ref",
    "ref": "Genesis 24:58",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "And make my waiting a bridal veil — hung not to hide my face, but to promise it.",
    "id": "ch10-p266",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Turn me toward You and keep me turned, until the veil lifts and I see what I have loved.",
    "id": "ch10-p267",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Face to face.",
    "id": "ch10-p268",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Amen.",
    "id": "ch10-p269",
    "zone": "prayer"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "One more thing, and it is not mine. It was never written to be read. It was written to be said out loud, over somebody.",
    "id": "ch10-p270"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "You heard it at the start of this book, coming up out of a grave on a piece of silver.",
    "id": "ch10-p271"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Listen to the last line.",
    "id": "ch10-p272"
   },
   {
    "type": "beat"
   },
   {
    "type": "verse",
    "lines": [
     "The LORD bless you, and keep you;",
     "The LORD make His face shine on you,",
     "And be gracious to you;",
     "The LORD lift up His face on you,",
     "And give you peace."
    ],
    "ref": "Numbers 6:24–26",
    "translation": "NASB",
    "id": "ch10-v18"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Every hiding and every seeking, every veil and every tearing, every psalm sent up at a silent sky. All of it has been traveling, the whole time, toward five words.",
    "id": "ch10-p273"
   },
   {
    "type": "p",
    "html": "Nothing comes after them. When they come true, every other longing you have ever had will turn out to have been this one, wearing a disguise.",
    "id": "ch10-p274"
   },
   {
    "type": "swell"
   },
   {
    "type": "fivewords",
    "text": "They will see His face.",
    "ref": "Revelation 22:4"
   }
  ]
 }
];
